window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("node-ts-for-ai", {"topics":[{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","sources":["Official docs: TypeScript Handbook (tsconfig reference, Narrowing, Conditional and Mapped Types)"],"why":"- **AI services are glue between shapeless data.** JSON from a model, tool-call arguments the model\n  invented, server-sent events in four or five variants, SDK objects with deeply optional fields. In\n  OpenAI's Chat Completions shape, `choices[0]?.message?.content` is legitimately `null` when the model\n  returned a tool call instead of text.\n- **JavaScript lets you read anything.** The natural failure is `TypeError: Cannot read properties of\n  undefined` thrown halfway through a request, after tokens have been billed and partially streamed.\n- **\"Write tests\" is not enough.** Tests exercise the paths you thought of; a type checker exercises every\n  call site on every change, including the SDK upgrade that silently made a field optional.\n- **Default TypeScript catches almost nothing.** Without `strict`, untyped parameters become `any`, `null`\n  is a member of every type, and `JSON.parse` returns `any` that flows unchecked into domain objects. Worse,\n  it creates a false sense that shapes are checked.\n- **What strictness buys.** The compiler says *no* exactly where a dynamic language says *maybe*: null\n  handling, variant handling, and the boundary where untrusted data becomes typed data.","how":"### What `strict` actually switches on\n`\"strict\": true` is an umbrella for a family of flags, each closing a specific hole.\n\n| Flag | What it does | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`noImplicitAny`** | An untyped parameter or variable is an error, not silently `any` | `any` is viral: one untyped parameter turns everything derived from it into `any` |\n| **`strictNullChecks`** | `null` and `undefined` are their own types; a `string \\| null` must be narrowed before `.trim()` | Turns \"Cannot read properties of undefined\" from a runtime surprise into a compile error |\n| **`strictFunctionTypes`** | Parameters of function-typed values are checked contravariantly | A handler typed to accept `TextEvent` would receive events it cannot handle if passed where a handler of `StreamEvent` is expected |\n| **`strictPropertyInitialization`** | Class fields must be initialised at declaration or in the constructor | No half-built objects |\n| **`strictBindCallApply`** | `.call/.apply` are type-checked | Dynamic calls stay checked |\n| **`noImplicitThis`** | `this` must have a known type | No accidental global `this` |\n| **`alwaysStrict`** | `\"use strict\"` is emitted | JS strict semantics everywhere |\n| **`useUnknownInCatchVariables`** | `catch (e)` makes `e: unknown`; narrow before reading `e.message` | Anything can be thrown in JS, including strings and `undefined` |\n\n- **Method vs property syntax.** Parameters declared with method syntax (`onEvent(e: T): void` in an\n  interface) stay bivariant for compatibility, so prefer property syntax (`onEvent: (e: T) => void`) for\n  callback contracts.\n- **The family grows.** New TypeScript releases occasionally add flags to `strict` (5.6 added\n  `strictBuiltinIteratorReturn`), so upgrading the compiler can surface new errors; that is the point.\n\n### Three flags worth adding beyond `strict`\n- **`noUncheckedIndexedAccess`.** `arr[i]` and `record[key]` become `T | undefined`, which is the truth:\n  `choices[0]` can be missing.\n- **`exactOptionalPropertyTypes`.** An optional property may be absent but may not be explicitly\n  `undefined`. It matters because `{ ...defaults, ...overrides }` with `temperature: undefined`\n  *overwrites* the default, and `\"temperature\" in opts` is true for a present-but-undefined key.\n- **`noFallthroughCasesInSwitch`.** A `case` that falls into the next without `break` is an error.\n\n### How narrowing works\nStrictness is livable only because of *control-flow analysis*: the checker tracks, per code path, what a\nvariable can be. After `if (x === null) return;` the type of `x` below is `T`, not `T | null`.\n\n- **Built-in narrowing:** `typeof x === \"string\"`, `x instanceof Error`, `\"tool_calls\" in msg`, equality on\n  a literal field (`ev.type === \"done\"`), and truthiness.\n- **Type predicate:** `function isToolCall(x: unknown): x is ToolCall` narrows in the caller's `if`.\n- **Assertion function:** `function assertDefined<T>(x: T): asserts x is NonNullable<T>` narrows everything\n  after the call, or throws.\n- **Both are trusted blindly.** If a guard's body is wrong, the compiler believes the lie, so keep guards\n  tiny or generate them from a schema.\n\n### `unknown` versus `any`\n- **`any` switches the checker off** for that value.\n- **`unknown` is the honest top type.** You can hold it, but cannot call, index or dereference it until you\n  narrow it with `typeof`, `instanceof`, a user-defined type guard\n  (`function isToolCall(x: unknown): x is ToolCall`), or a runtime validator.\n- **The rule that follows.** Wrap `JSON.parse` so it returns `unknown`, and make every boundary (HTTP body,\n  model output, env var, webhook) go through a parse step that yields a typed value or throws. Types\n  inside the boundary can then be trusted *because* nothing crosses it unvalidated.\n\n### Discriminated unions and exhaustiveness\n1. **Model the stream as a union** of object types sharing a literal `type` field:\n   `{ type: \"text_delta\"; text: string } | { type: \"tool_call\"; name: string; args: unknown } |\n   { type: \"done\"; usage: Usage }`.\n2. **Switch on the tag.** `switch (ev.type)` narrows `ev` in each branch, and a `default` branch that\n   assigns `ev` to a variable of type `never` fails to compile the day a provider adds a new variant.\n3. **Why unions beat class hierarchies** for protocol data: the compiler enumerates the call sites you must\n   update.\n\n### Generics with constraints\n- **Tie parser to handler.**\n  `function defineTool<A, R>(spec: { parse: (x: unknown) => A; run: (args: A) => Promise<R> })` means a\n  handler cannot be registered with a parser that produces a different shape.\n- **`extends` constrains** what a type parameter may be (`K extends keyof T`).\n- **Inference from arguments** means callers rarely write type parameters by hand.\n\n### Conditional, mapped and template-literal types\n| Kind | Examples | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mapped** | `Partial<T>`, `Readonly<T>`, `Record<K, V>`, or a custom nullable map (below) | Transform every property |\n| **Conditional** | `T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T` | A simplified `Awaited`; the built-in also recurses and unwraps any thenable. `ReturnType` and `Parameters` are built the same way with `infer` |\n| **Template-literal** | `` `${Provider}:${Model}` `` | Build string unions |\n\n- **Custom mapped type:** `{ [P in keyof T]: T[P] | null }` makes every property nullable.\n- **Where they belong.** Library and contract code (an SDK wrapper, a tool registry), not everyday\n  application logic, where they cost readability.\n\n### Branded types\n- **The problem: TypeScript is structural.** Any `string` fits any `string` parameter, so an unvalidated\n  user string and a sanitized SQL fragment, or a token count and a dollar amount, are interchangeable.\n- **The fix: a phantom property** only one function can produce:\n  `type ValidatedPrompt = string & { readonly __brand: \"ValidatedPrompt\" }`. Only\n  `validatePrompt(s: string): ValidatedPrompt` returns one (via a single internal\n  cast), so `callModel(p: ValidatedPrompt)` cannot be handed raw input by mistake.\n- **Zero runtime cost** because the brand is erased; it encodes \"this check already happened\" in the type,\n  the same idea as parsing at the boundary.\n\n### `as const` and `satisfies`\n- **`as const`** makes a literal deeply readonly and keeps literal types (`\"gpt-4o\"` instead of `string`).\n- **`satisfies`** checks a value against a type *without* widening it, so a model table can be validated as\n  `Record<string, ModelSpec>` while `keyof typeof table` still yields the literal model names.\n- **A plain annotation** would widen the keys to `string` and lose that.\n\n### What the compiler cannot do\n- **`tsc` erases every type;** no check runs at runtime.\n- **A cast is untyped JS.** A strict program that casts `JSON.parse(body) as Request` is exactly as unsafe\n  as untyped JS.\n- **Pair with runtime validation** at the edges, the subject of the schema-validation lesson.","example":"### Typed stream handler, model table and brand\nA typed stream handler, an exhaustiveness check, a model table that keeps literal keys, and a branded\ntype that makes \"validated\" visible to the compiler. Compile with `\"strict\": true` and\n`\"noUncheckedIndexedAccess\": true` in `tsconfig.json`.\n\n```ts\ntype Usage = { inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number };\ntype StreamEvent =\n  | { type: \"text_delta\"; text: string }\n  | { type: \"tool_call\"; name: string; args: unknown }\n  | { type: \"done\"; usage: Usage };\n\nconst models = {\n  fast: { id: \"small-model\", maxOutput: 4096 },\n  smart: { id: \"large-model\", maxOutput: 16384 },\n} as const satisfies Record<string, { id: string; maxOutput: number }>;\ntype ModelKey = keyof typeof models;            // \"fast\" | \"smart\", not string\n\nfunction parseJson(text: string): unknown {      // never let `any` escape JSON.parse\n  return JSON.parse(text);\n}\n\nfunction handle(ev: StreamEvent, out: string[], tools: Map<string, (a: unknown) => void>) {\n  switch (ev.type) {\n    case \"text_delta\": out.push(ev.text); break;          // ev narrowed: .text exists\n    case \"tool_call\": {\n      const fn = tools.get(ev.name);                        // Map.get is T | undefined\n      if (!fn) throw new Error(`unknown tool ${ev.name}`);\n      fn(ev.args); break;\n    }\n    case \"done\": console.log(ev.usage.outputTokens); break;\n    default: { const never: never = ev; return never; }   // compile error if a variant is added\n  }\n}\n\nfunction pick(key: ModelKey) { return models[key].id; }    // models[\"typo\"] is a compile error\n\ntype ValidatedPrompt = string & { readonly __brand: \"ValidatedPrompt\" };\nfunction validatePrompt(s: string): ValidatedPrompt {\n  if (!s.trim() || s.length > 32_000) throw new Error(\"bad prompt\");\n  return s as ValidatedPrompt;                              // the one sanctioned cast\n}\ndeclare function callModel(p: ValidatedPrompt): Promise<string>;\n// callModel(rawInput) fails to compile; callModel(validatePrompt(rawInput)) compiles\nconst first = [\"a\", \"b\"][0];                                // string | undefined under noUncheckedIndexedAccess\ntry { parseJson(\"{\"); } catch (e) {                        // e: unknown\n  if (e instanceof SyntaxError) console.error(e.message);\n}\n```\n\n### A typed tool registry with a constrained generic\n`defineTool` ties each tool's parser to its handler, so the registry cannot hold a mismatched pair. The\ntype guard here is deliberately tiny, as the narrowing section advises.\n\n```ts\ntype Tool<A, R> = { name: string; parse: (x: unknown) => A; run: (args: A) => Promise<R> };\n\nfunction defineTool<A, R>(spec: Tool<A, R>): Tool<A, R> { return spec; }\n\nfunction isRecord(x: unknown): x is Record<string, unknown> {\n  return typeof x === \"object\" && x !== null && !Array.isArray(x);\n}\n\nconst getWeather = defineTool({\n  name: \"get_weather\",\n  parse: (x) => {\n    if (!isRecord(x) || typeof x.city !== \"string\") throw new Error(\"city must be a string\");\n    return { city: x.city };                 // A inferred as { city: string }\n  },\n  run: async (args) => `Sunny in ${args.city}`,   // args is { city: string }, no annotation needed\n});\n\nasync function dispatch<A, R>(tool: Tool<A, R>, rawArgs: unknown): Promise<R> {\n  return tool.run(tool.parse(rawArgs));      // model-invented args are parsed before use\n}\n// dispatch(getWeather, JSON.parse('{\"city\":\"Oslo\"}')) resolves to \"Sunny in Oslo\"\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: an SDK upgrade that made a field nullable\nA team upgraded a provider SDK; the response field holding text became `string | null` because tool calls\nnow return no text.\n\n| Codebase setting | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| `strict: false`, result cast with `as` | Compiled cleanly; `content.trim()` threw `TypeError` in production on every tool-call turn |\n| `strict: true` | `tsc` flagged each `.trim()` call site on the upgrade PR; fixed before merge |\n| `strict` plus `never` default in the event switch | The new `tool_call` variant also failed to compile in the handler until a case was added |","patterns":["**Strict baseline in a shared tsconfig.** Every service extends one base config with `\"strict\": true`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` and `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, and CI runs `tsc --noEmit` on every PR. Monitor the error count when bumping the TypeScript version, since new releases can add strict flags.","**Lint bans on escape hatches.** Enable `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any` and `consistent-type-assertions` as errors, with reviewed per-line disables. Track the count of `any` and `as` over time as a debt metric.","**`unknown` at the edge, parse once.** Wrap `JSON.parse`, request bodies, webhook payloads and model tool arguments so they return `unknown`, then run one schema parse that yields a typed value. Monitor boundary validation-failure rates per source.","**Exhaustive switches on provider events.** Model each provider's stream as a discriminated union with a `never` default branch, so an SDK bump that adds a variant breaks the build, not the stream. Watch for \"stream ended early\" reports after upgrades.","**Ratcheted migration for legacy code.** Turn flags on one at a time, fix or explicitly suppress the errors, and set a deadline, because a half-strict codebase leaves types that lie."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"HTTP body, model output, env\"] --> U[\"JSON.parse returns unknown\"]\n    U --> P{\"Parse or type guard\"}\n    P -->|\"fails\"| E[\"Throw at the boundary\"]\n    P -->|\"passes\"| T[\"Typed value, e.g. StreamEvent\"]\n    T --> S[\"switch on ev.type\"]\n    S --> B1[\"text_delta branch\"]\n    S --> B2[\"tool_call branch\"]\n    S --> B3[\"done branch\"]\n    S -.->|\"unhandled variant\"| N[\"never check: compile error\"]","caption":"Untrusted data enters as `unknown`, is parsed once at the boundary, and from then on strict checking and\nexhaustive switches guard every use."},"interview":[{"q":"What does `strictNullChecks` change, and why is it the most valuable strict flag for AI services?","a":"- **Definition:** `null` and `undefined` stop being members of every type, so a `string | null` must be\n  narrowed before you call methods on it.\n- **Why it matters here:** provider responses are full of legitimately missing fields, such as message\n  content being `null` on a tool-call turn, and this flag turns those runtime crashes into compile errors."},{"q":"A provider added a new stream event type and users report answers ending early. How do you debug and prevent it?","a":"- **Diagnose:** the handler's `switch` has no case for the new variant, so the event falls through and is\n  dropped silently; log unknown `type` values at the boundary to confirm.\n- **Prevent:** model events as a discriminated union and add a `default` that assigns `ev` to `never`, so\n  the next variant breaks the build on the upgrade PR instead of in production."},{"q":"When would you not reach for conditional and mapped types?","a":"- **In application code with one or two users of a type,** where a plain type is clearer and the compile-time\n  and reading cost outweigh the precision.\n- **Use them in shared contracts** such as an SDK wrapper or tool registry, where one precise type is\n  consumed by many call sites and keeping hand-written copies in sync would be the bigger risk."},{"q":"How would you migrate a 200-file service from `strict: false` to strict?","a":"- **Ratchet flag by flag:** start with `noImplicitAny` and `strictNullChecks`, fix or explicitly suppress\n  errors, and block new violations in CI.\n- **Track the debt:** count `any`, `as` and suppressions per release, and set a deadline, because a\n  half-migrated codebase has types that lie.\n- **Fix boundaries first:** wrap `JSON.parse` and SDK calls to return `unknown`, since that is where most\n  runtime `TypeError`s originate."},{"q":"What is a branded type and where would you use one?","a":"- **Definition:** an intersection like `string & { readonly __brand: \"ValidatedPrompt\" }` that only one\n  factory function can produce, erased at runtime so it costs nothing.\n- **Where:** places where mixing same-shaped values is a security or billing bug, such as raw vs validated\n  prompt text, or token counts vs dollar amounts."}],"tradeoffs":["**Safety vs up-front work.** Strict mode front-loads work: every `null` must be handled explicitly, which costs time on a fresh codebase but pays back the first time a provider makes a field optional. It flips against you only on throwaway scripts where no second reader exists.","**Precision vs readability.** Advanced types (conditional, mapped, template-literal) make contracts precise but raise compile times and reading cost. They pay off when many call sites consume one contract (an SDK wrapper, a tool registry) and cost more than they save when a type has one or two users.","**Honesty vs boilerplate.** `unknown` at every boundary forces a parse step, extra code and a small runtime cost, but the alternative is trusting casts. It only flips for data your own process produced and never serialized, where the compiler already knows the shape.","**Brands vs friction.** Branded types prevent mixing up same-shaped values (raw vs validated input, tokens vs dollars), but every construction site needs the brand's factory. Worth it where a mix-up is a security or billing bug, noise where it is merely a naming issue.","**Unions vs classes.** Discriminated unions give exhaustiveness checks, class hierarchies give polymorphic dispatch. Unions win for protocol data that crosses the wire; classes win when behaviour outweighs shape.","**Gradual vs big-bang adoption.** `strict` off with flags turned on one by one keeps a migration shippable but leaves types that lie; set a deadline or the migration never finishes."],"pitfalls":["**Casting with `as` to silence an error** (`JSON.parse(x) as Response`). Symptom: a runtime `TypeError` on a field the type said was present, usually after an upstream API change. Fix: return `unknown` and parse.","**Leaving `strict: false` and trusting the red squiggles.** Symptom: implicit `any` everywhere, so renaming a field breaks nothing at compile time and everything at runtime. Fix: enable `strict`.","**Forgetting the `never` default branch** in a `switch` over a union. Symptom: a new event type silently dropped, often noticed as \"the stream ends early for tool calls\".","**Annotating instead of using `satisfies`,** so a config map loses its literal keys. Symptom: `keyof typeof config` becomes `string` and typos in model names compile cleanly.","**Treating `catch (e: any)` as harmless.** Symptom: `e.message` is `undefined` because a library threw a string or non-Error object, hiding the real failure in logs. Fix: keep `e: unknown` and narrow.","**A guard that checks less than it claims** (`x is ToolCall` after only checking `typeof x === \"object\"`). Symptom: a crash on a field the guard \"proved\" existed, with no cast anywhere to grep for."],"when":"- **Day one:** turn on `strict` in any Node AI service and add `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`; the cost of\n  retrofitting grows with every file.\n- **Use discriminated unions** whenever data has variants (stream events, tool results, provider error\n  shapes), and **constrained generics** when a registry or SDK wrapper must tie an input type to a handler.\n- **Use conditional and mapped types** only when a plain type would have to be repeated or kept in sync by\n  hand.\n- **Measure:** the share of `any` and `as` in the codebase (lint with `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any`\n  and `consistent-type-assertions`) and how often runtime `TypeError`s reach production.\n- **Interviews:** explaining *why* `strictNullChecks` matters, what `unknown` buys over `any`, and how\n  exhaustiveness checking works is the expected depth; writing a mapped type from memory is not.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Models provider stream events and tool contracts as discriminated unions, keeps `JSON.parse` behind an `unknown`-returning wrapper, and fixes compile errors instead of casting past them.","architect":"Sets the compiler baseline (`strict`, index-access checks, lint bans on `any`) as a repository standard and decides which shared packages own the cross-service type contracts.","fde":"Reads a customer's `tsconfig.json` first when debugging \"random undefined\" incidents, and frames turning on `strict` as the cheapest reliability fix available in their stack."},"check":{"q":"A teammate argues that since TypeScript types are erased at runtime, enabling `strict` is\ncosmetic and runtime validation with a schema library is all that matters. How do you respond?","a":"Both are needed because they catch different failures. Runtime validation guards the boundary:\nit turns untrusted JSON into a value you can trust, but it only runs where someone remembered to call\nit. Strict typing guards everything inside the boundary: once a value is typed, every call site is\nchecked on every change, including the SDK upgrade that made a field nullable or the new stream\nvariant nobody handled. Without `strict`, a validated value can still be dereferenced as `any` two\nfunctions later. The right design is `unknown` at the edge, a schema parse that produces a typed value,\nand strict checking everywhere after that, so a mismatch surfaces either at compile time or at the\nboundary, never mid-request."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `unknown` preferred over `any` for the result of `JSON.parse`?","options":["`unknown` is faster because the engine skips property lookups","`unknown` cannot be used until narrowed, so the compiler forces a check before the value spreads through typed code","`any` cannot hold `null`, so parsed JSON with nulls would fail","`unknown` automatically validates the JSON against an interface"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`any` disables checking and propagates silently through every assignment. `unknown` holds the value but refuses every operation until a type guard or validator narrows it, which is exactly the discipline a boundary needs. Neither type performs any runtime validation."},{"q":"What does a `default: const never: never = ev` branch in a switch over a discriminated union achieve?","options":["It throws at runtime if an unexpected event arrives","It tells the bundler the branch is dead code and removes it","It makes the code fail to compile when a new union member is added but not handled","It converts the union into an enum for faster dispatch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If every variant is handled, `ev` has type `never` in the default branch and the assignment type-checks. Add a variant without a case and `ev` is no longer `never`, so the compiler reports every switch that needs updating. It provides no runtime behaviour on its own."},{"q":"Why use `satisfies` instead of a type annotation for a constant config table?","options":["`satisfies` performs runtime validation of the object","Annotations are not allowed on `const` declarations","`satisfies` checks the value against the type while keeping its literal keys and values, so `keyof typeof table` stays precise","`satisfies` makes the object deeply immutable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An annotation widens the value to the declared type, turning literal keys into `string`. `satisfies` only verifies compatibility, so the inferred type keeps `\"fast\" | \"smart\"` as keys and the compiler can reject typos in model names. Immutability comes from `as const`, not `satisfies`."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — The Node.js Event Loop, Timers, and process.nextTick()","Official docs: Node.js — perf_hooks (monitorEventLoopDelay)"],"why":"- **LLM services mostly wait.** A model request might take 2–30 seconds, during which the CPU does nothing\n  useful for that request.\n- **Thread-per-request wastes the wait.** One blocked OS thread per call spends a stack and a kernel thread\n  on every idle wait. It works at hundreds of concurrent calls, but overhead grows with *waiting* requests,\n  not with the work done.\n- **Node's bet: one thread, non-blocking I/O.** A loop runs the callback for whichever operation just\n  completed, so a pending HTTP response costs a few kilobytes of heap, not a thread.\n- **The price: the thread is shared by everything.** No scheduler preempts a long computation. If a handler\n  spends 300 ms synchronously tokenizing a 2 MB document or `JSON.parse`-ing a huge tool result, no other\n  callback runs for 300 ms: tokens stop flowing to every client, health checks time out, timers fire late.\n- **\"Async runs in parallel\" is wrong.** Only the *waiting* is concurrent; the JavaScript is strictly\n  sequential. Knowing the phases and microtask queues explains ordering (why a promise callback runs before\n  a `setTimeout(0)`), starvation (a recursive `nextTick` that never lets I/O run), and when to offload.","how":"### One thread, a loop, and libuv\n- **Two engines.** Node embeds V8 for JavaScript and libuv for the event loop and asynchronous I/O.\n- **Sockets use OS readiness** (epoll, kqueue, IOCP), so waiting on 10,000 sockets is one system call, not\n  10,000 threads.\n- **Blocking OS work goes to a pool.** Most filesystem calls, DNS lookups via `getaddrinfo` and some crypto\n  run on libuv's small thread pool (four threads by default, tunable with `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE`); results are\n  posted back to the loop as events.\n- **Your JavaScript never runs on those threads.**\n\n### The phases\nEach iteration (\"tick\") visits phases in a fixed order, running the callbacks queued for each:\n\n1. **timers** — callbacks of `setTimeout`/`setInterval` whose threshold has elapsed. A timer is a *minimum*\n   delay, not a schedule; it runs when the loop gets here.\n2. **pending callbacks** — deferred I/O callbacks from the previous iteration (some system errors). libuv\n   then runs internal idle/prepare hooks; nothing in user code schedules there.\n3. **poll** — the heart of the loop: run callbacks for completed I/O (socket data, HTTP responses, file\n   reads), then, if nothing else is due, block in the OS waiting for more. The block's timeout comes from the\n   nearest timer, and it does not block at all if `setImmediate` callbacks are queued. An idle Node process\n   sits here, asleep in `epoll_wait`, using no CPU.\n4. **check** — `setImmediate` callbacks, so code can say \"run right after the I/O I just handled\" without\n   competing with timers.\n5. **close callbacks** — `'close'` events for destroyed sockets and handles.\n\n- **Microtasks run between every callback.** Since Node 11 the drain happens after each individual timer or\n  immediate, not once per phase as in older versions.\n- **The loop exits** when there are no pending timers, handles or requests, which is why a script \"just\n  ends\" when its work is done.\n\n### Microtasks: `process.nextTick` and promise jobs\nTwo queues that are not phases at all:\n\n| Queue | What lands there | Drained |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nextTick` queue | `process.nextTick` callbacks | First, completely |\n| Promise microtask queue (the ECMAScript \"job queue\") | `.then` callbacks, `await` continuations, `queueMicrotask` | Second, completely |\n\nAfter each callback returns, Node drains the `nextTick` queue, then the promise queue, repeating until both\nare empty, *then* moves on. Microtasks queued while draining run in the same drain. Two consequences:\n\n- **Ordering is deterministic.** Synchronous code first, then every `nextTick`, then every promise\n  continuation, then the next macrotask (timer, I/O or immediate). An `await` on an already-resolved value\n  still yields to the microtask queue: enough to let other promise continuations run, *not* enough to let\n  I/O run.\n- **Starvation is possible.** A function that re-queues itself with `nextTick` or a resolved promise keeps\n  the drain going forever, so poll never gets a turn. Symptom: a process that is busy but handles no\n  requests. `setImmediate` is the safe way to yield to I/O.\n\n### Two ordering traps\n- **ES module top level.** It is evaluated inside a promise job, so code queued there sees the promise queue\n  drained *before* the `nextTick` queue (`.then` prints before `nextTick`). The \"nextTick first\" rule holds\n  in CommonJS modules and inside ordinary callbacks such as a request handler, which is where it usually\n  matters.\n- **`setTimeout(fn, 0)` vs `setImmediate(fn)`.** From the main module the order is non-deterministic (it\n  depends on whether 1 ms has elapsed when the timers phase is checked). Inside an I/O callback,\n  `setImmediate` always runs first, because check comes before the next timers phase.\n\n### What \"blocking the loop\" looks like in an AI service\n- **Anything not awaiting I/O runs to completion on the loop thread:** `JSON.parse` of a multi-megabyte\n  response, a regex over a long transcript, cosine similarity across 100k embeddings in plain JS, synchronous\n  `zlib`/`crypto` calls, or a tight loop building a prompt from a large document.\n- **Every open SSE stream freezes meanwhile,** because the `write()` calls that would flush their next tokens\n  are callbacks waiting for the loop.\n- **Lag is the first metric.** `perf_hooks.monitorEventLoopDelay()` samples how late the loop is; p99 lag\n  above tens of milliseconds means a handler is doing CPU work inline.\n- **Utilization is its companion.** `performance.eventLoopUtilization()` reports the fraction of time the\n  loop was busy rather than idle in poll.\n\n| Signal | Meaning | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Utilization near 1, lag rising smoothly | Saturated by many short callbacks | Scale out |\n| Lag spikes at moderate utilization | A few long callbacks | Find and offload them |\n\nRemedies, in order: make it async in chunks (`await setImmediate`-style yielding), move it to a worker\nthread, or move it out of the process.\n\n### Why chunk size is a latency budget\n- **Yielding bounds waiting, it does not speed work up.** If one slice takes *s* ms of synchronous time,\n  every other callback can wait up to *s* ms per slice, and the yielding request takes longer in total\n  because others run between its slices.\n- **Pick the slice from tolerable latency.** If other streams must never stall more than ~10 ms, a slice\n  must be measured to run under 10 ms on the production CPU.\n- **The yield must be a *macrotask* (`setImmediate`).** `await Promise.resolve()` or `await` on any\n  already-resolved value only re-enters the microtask queue, drained before poll, so it yields to nothing\n  that matters.","example":"### Ordering rules, a lag monitor and cooperative CPU work\nThe ordering rules and a lag monitor in one script. Run it as CommonJS (for example compile with `tsc` to\n`module: commonjs`, or run with `tsx` in a package without `\"type\": \"module\"`); as an ES module lines 3 and\n4 swap, for the reason given above.\n\n```ts\nimport { monitorEventLoopDelay } from \"node:perf_hooks\";\nimport { setImmediate as yieldToLoop } from \"node:timers/promises\";\n\nconsole.log(\"1 sync\");\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\"5 timeout (timers phase)\"), 0);\nsetImmediate(() => console.log(\"6 immediate (check phase)\"));\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"4 promise microtask\"));\nprocess.nextTick(() => console.log(\"3 nextTick (drained before promises)\"));\nconsole.log(\"2 sync\");\n// CommonJS: 1,2,3,4 then 5,6 (5/6 may swap from the main module; never from an I/O callback).\n// ESM top level: 1,2,4,3 then 5,6, because module evaluation itself runs inside a promise job.\n\n// Event-loop lag: sample the histogram and alert when p99 is high.\nconst h = monitorEventLoopDelay({ resolution: 10 });\nh.enable();\nsetInterval(() => {\n  console.log(`loop lag p99 ${(h.percentile(99) / 1e6).toFixed(1)} ms`);\n  h.reset();\n}, 5_000).unref();\n\n// A CPU-heavy scoring loop made cooperative: yield to I/O every 500 vectors so\n// streaming responses to other clients keep flowing while this request computes.\n// At 1536 dims, 500 vectors is ~768k multiply-adds per slice: measure it, it is\n// the worst-case stall every other client sees. 100k vectors = 200 slices.\nasync function topK(query: Float32Array, vectors: Float32Array[], k: number) {\n  const scores: Array<[number, number]> = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < vectors.length; i++) {\n    let dot = 0;\n    for (let d = 0; d < query.length; d++) dot += query[d]! * vectors[i]![d]!;\n    scores.push([dot, i]);\n    if (i % 500 === 499) await yieldToLoop();   // setImmediate: lets the poll phase run\n  }\n  return scores.sort((a, b) => b[0] - a[0]).slice(0, k);\n}\nvoid topK;\n```\n\n### Seeing a stall: a timer that fires late\nSave as `stall.mjs` and run with `node stall.mjs`. A 10 ms ticker should print about every 10 ms; a 300 ms\nsynchronous loop makes one tick arrive roughly 300 ms late, exactly what every SSE stream would feel.\n\n```js\nlet last = performance.now();\nconst ticker = setInterval(() => {\n  const now = performance.now();\n  const gap = now - last;\n  if (gap > 50) console.log(`tick late: ${gap.toFixed(0)} ms since previous`);\n  last = now;\n}, 10);\n\nsetTimeout(() => {\n  const end = performance.now() + 300;     // simulate a big JSON.parse or tokenizer pass\n  while (performance.now() < end) {}       // blocks the only JS thread\n  console.log(\"blocking work done\");\n}, 100);\n\nsetTimeout(() => clearInterval(ticker), 1000);\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one handler, every stream paused\nA gateway holds 2,000 SSE streams. One route parses tool results of up to 20 MB inline.\n\n| Observation | Reading |\n|---|---|\n| All streams pause ~500 ms at once, several times a minute | One callback holds the thread; per-stream causes would not be synchronized |\n| One core at 100%, provider latency normal, memory flat | CPU work inside the process, not upstream |\n| `monitorEventLoopDelay` p99 ≈ 500 ms, utilization moderate | A few long callbacks, not saturation |\n| CPU profile: `JSON.parse` under the tool-result handler | The culprit |\n| Fix: parse in a worker thread, cap tool-result size | p99 lag back under 10 ms (illustrative) |","patterns":["**Ship a loop-lag gauge with every service.** Enable `monitorEventLoopDelay({ resolution: 10 })` at startup, export p50/p99 every few seconds to your metrics system, and reset the histogram after each read. Alert on p99 lag next to the latency SLO.","**Shed load on loop pressure.** Return 503 from new requests when lag or `eventLoopUtilization()` crosses a threshold, so the process protects its in-flight streams; Fastify's `@fastify/under-pressure` plugin packages this. Monitor the shed rate and its correlation with deploys.","**Profile before guessing.** Reproduce under load with `node --cpu-prof` (writes a `.cpuprofile` you open in Chrome DevTools) or a `clinic`-style tool, and look for long synchronous frames under request handlers.","**Size the libuv pool for filesystem- or DNS-heavy services.** Set `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE` in the environment before the process starts, because the pool is created on first use. Watch for latency on `fs` and `dns.lookup` calls queuing behind each other.","**Ban sync APIs in hot paths.** Lint or review away `fs.readFileSync`, `child_process.execSync` and sync `zlib` outside startup code, since their small stalls multiply across every in-flight request."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"timers: setTimeout, setInterval\"] --> PC[\"pending callbacks\"]\n    PC --> P[\"poll: I/O callbacks, maybe block\"]\n    P --> C[\"check: setImmediate\"]\n    C --> CL[\"close callbacks\"]\n    CL -->|\"next tick\"| T\n    CB[\"after every callback\"] --> N[\"drain nextTick queue\"]\n    N --> PR[\"drain promise queue\"]\n    PR -->|\"new microtasks queued\"| N\n    P -.->|\"each callback\"| CB\n    T -.->|\"each callback\"| CB","caption":"One loop iteration: each phase runs its callbacks, and after every single callback both microtask queues\ndrain before the loop moves on."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can a single Node process hold thousands of open model streams?","a":"- **Waiting is cheap:** libuv watches sockets with the OS readiness API, so a pending response costs a few\n  kilobytes of heap, not an OS thread and stack.\n- **Work is short:** each stream only needs a brief callback when a chunk arrives, so one thread can serve\n  them all as long as no callback runs long."},{"q":"What is the difference between `process.nextTick`, a promise `.then` and `setImmediate`?","a":"- **`nextTick`** runs in the first microtask queue, drained right after the current callback.\n- **`.then`** runs in the promise queue, drained after `nextTick` (except at ES module top level).\n- **`setImmediate`** is a macrotask in the check phase, after poll, so it is the only one of the three that\n  lets I/O run first; use it to yield."},{"q":"When would chunking with `setImmediate` be the wrong fix?","a":"- **Sustained heavy compute:** chunking only spreads the cost on the same core, so total throughput does not\n  improve and the request gets slower.\n- **Use a worker thread or separate service** when the CPU work is frequent or large, and keep chunking for\n  occasional medium jobs."},{"q":"How would you set an alert threshold for event-loop lag?","a":"- **Derive it from the streaming budget:** if users notice token pauses over roughly a few tens of\n  milliseconds, alert when p99 lag exceeds that for several minutes.\n- **Pair it with utilization:** high lag at moderate utilization means long callbacks to offload; high lag\n  at utilization near 1 means saturation and a scale-out."},{"q":"Does raising `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE` help a handler that blocks on a large `JSON.parse`?","a":"- **No:** the pool runs native operations such as filesystem calls and `getaddrinfo`, never your JavaScript.\n- **`JSON.parse` runs on the loop thread,** so the fix is a worker thread, a size cap, or a streaming parser."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheap concurrency vs no CPU parallelism.** One thread with non-blocking I/O is very cheap for I/O-bound work like proxying model calls, but gives zero parallelism for CPU work. Once handlers do real computation the model flips and you need worker threads or separate processes.","**Determinism vs starvation.** Deterministic microtask ordering makes promise code predictable and easy to test, but the same rule lets a promise loop starve I/O, which thread-based runtimes would have preempted.","**Chunking vs simplicity.** `setImmediate` chunking keeps the process responsive without threads, at the cost of longer total time for that request and harder-to-read code. It wins for occasional medium jobs and loses for sustained heavy compute.","**Thread pool convenience vs queuing.** The libuv pool hides blocking syscalls, but with four threads by default a burst of file reads or DNS lookups queues; raising `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE` helps until memory and contention bite."],"pitfalls":["**CPU-heavy work inline in a handler** (tokenizing, big `JSON.parse`, similarity search). Symptom: every concurrent stream stalls at once and p99 spikes while one core sits at 100%.","**Recursive `process.nextTick` or resolved promises to \"spread work out\".** Symptom: a process alive and busy that accepts no new connections, because poll never runs.","**Assuming `setTimeout(fn, 100)` fires at 100 ms.** Symptom: timers drift by seconds under load, because they fire only when the timers phase is reached.","**Sync APIs in a hot path** (`fs.readFileSync`, `child_process.execSync`, sync `zlib`) because \"it is just a small file\". Symptom: appears only under concurrency, when the small stall multiplies across requests.","**Not watching event-loop lag.** Symptom: latency incidents with no CPU, memory or upstream signal to explain them, because the delay is inside the process.","**\"Yielding\" with `await Promise.resolve()` or `await null`** in a hot loop. Symptom: loop lag unchanged, because microtasks are drained before the loop reaches poll. Fix: yield with `setImmediate`."],"when":"- **You do not opt in; you design around it.** Use plain async I/O for everything that waits on a network or\n  a model, and expect thousands of concurrent requests per process.\n- **The decision point is CPU.** More than a few milliseconds of synchronous work per request: chunk with\n  `setImmediate` yielding when occasional, move to a worker thread or separate service when sustained.\n- **Measure:** `monitorEventLoopDelay` in production with a p99 lag alert next to the latency SLO; add\n  `--cpu-prof` or `clinic`-style profiling when lag is high.\n- **Interviews:** predict the output order of a snippet mixing `setTimeout`, `setImmediate`, `nextTick` and\n  promises, and explain why one slow handler hurts unrelated users.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps request handlers free of synchronous heavy work, yields or offloads CPU-bound steps, and ships an event-loop-lag gauge with every service so stalls are visible.","architect":"Decides which workloads belong on the loop (model proxying, streaming, orchestration) and which must live in workers or separate services (parsing, embedding math, batch jobs).","fde":"Diagnoses \"the assistant freezes for everyone at once\" at a customer by checking loop lag before blaming the model provider, and explains why a bigger VM will not fix a single-thread stall."},"check":{"q":"A streaming chat gateway shows all active streams pausing for about half a second several times\na minute. CPU is at 100% on one core, the model provider's latency is normal, and memory is flat. What is\nhappening and how would you confirm and fix it?","a":"The pattern points to event-loop blocking: some handler runs roughly 500 ms of synchronous\nJavaScript, and because every stream's next `write()` is a callback queued on the same loop, all streams\nfreeze together. Confirm by sampling `monitorEventLoopDelay` (p99 lag will match the pause) and taking a\nCPU profile to find the hot function; likely candidates are parsing a large tool result, tokenizing\ndocuments for context packing, or a similarity search over embeddings in plain JS. Fix by moving that\nwork to a worker thread (or a separate service) if it is sustained, or by chunking it with `setImmediate`\nyields if it is occasional, and keep a loop-lag alert so regressions are caught before users notice."},"quiz":[{"q":"In what order do these run: a `setTimeout(fn, 0)`, a resolved promise's `.then`, and a `process.nextTick` callback queued from the same synchronous block inside an HTTP request handler?","options":["setTimeout, then nextTick, then the promise callback","promise callback, then nextTick, then setTimeout","nextTick, then the promise callback, then setTimeout","All three run in the order they were queued"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After the synchronous block finishes, Node drains the nextTick queue, then the promise microtask queue, and only then advances the loop to the timers phase where the setTimeout fires. Queue order matters only within a single queue. (At the top level of an ES module the promise callback runs first, because module evaluation is itself a promise job; inside a handler callback the nextTick-first rule holds.)"},{"q":"Why does a loop that re-queues itself with `process.nextTick` prevent the server from accepting connections?","options":["nextTick callbacks run on the libuv thread pool, which fills up","The nextTick queue is drained completely before the loop reaches the poll phase, so I/O callbacks never run","nextTick disables the timers phase until the queue is empty","Each nextTick call opens a file descriptor that is never closed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Microtask queues are drained to empty after every callback, and work queued during the drain is included. A self-re-queuing nextTick is never empty, so the loop never gets to poll for socket events. Using setImmediate instead schedules in the check phase and lets I/O interleave."},{"q":"Which change most directly fixes a handler that stalls all concurrent streams for 300 ms while computing embeddings similarity in JavaScript?","options":["Increase `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE` so the computation runs on more threads","Wrap the computation in `async` and `await` its result","Move the computation to a worker thread or yield with `setImmediate` between chunks","Replace `setTimeout` calls in the handler with `setImmediate`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Marking a function `async` does not move its synchronous body off the loop, and the libuv pool only serves native operations, not your JavaScript. Only a worker thread gives true parallelism, and chunked yielding at least lets other callbacks run between slices."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — process 'unhandledRejection' event and --unhandled-rejections","Official docs: MDN — Promise, Promise.allSettled, Promise.any, Error cause"],"why":"- **Callbacks made errors easy to forget.** Callback-style Node passed errors as the first argument, so a\n  forgotten `if (err)` meant silent corruption, and five dependent I/O calls meant five levels of nesting\n  with five separate error paths.\n- **Promises made the result a value.** A chain of `.then` calls has one `.catch` at the end, and a\n  rejection anywhere skips straight to it. `async`/`await` made control flow readable again, with\n  `try`/`catch` working across awaits.\n- **What promises did not fix: ownership of a failure.** An agent loop makes dozens of concurrent calls (the\n  model, three tool executions, an embedding lookup, a logging sink). A started-but-never-awaited one has no\n  handler; modern Node treats that as fatal and exits, taking every other in-flight request with it.\n- **Combinators and wrappers hide detail.** With `Promise.all` the first rejection wins and the others keep\n  running unobserved. Rethrowing a `fetch` error as `new Error(\"model call failed\")` loses the status code\n  and the provider's error body before it reaches a log.\n- **AI failures demand precision.** Failures are dominated by upstream timeouts, rate limits and malformed\n  outputs, so the difference between a retryable 429 and a permanent 400 must survive the trip up the stack.","how":"### States and chaining\n- **Settles exactly once.** A promise is pending, then fulfilled (with a value) or rejected (with a reason,\n  conventionally an `Error`).\n- **`.then(onFulfilled, onRejected)` returns a *new* promise** whose outcome is whatever the handler returns\n  or throws; a returned promise is adopted (flattened).\n- **That is why chains propagate.** A throw in any step rejects that step's promise, every following\n  `.then` without a rejection handler passes it through unchanged, and the first `.catch` receives it.\n- **Handlers always run as microtasks,** never synchronously, even when the promise is already settled,\n  which keeps ordering consistent (see the event-loop lesson).\n\n### What `async`/`await` desugars to\n1. **An `async` function always returns a promise.** `return x` fulfils it.\n2. **A thrown exception rejects it,** including one thrown synchronously before the first `await`; it does\n   not escape to the caller.\n3. **`await p` suspends the function;** when `p` rejects, the rejection is re-thrown at the `await`\n   expression, so an enclosing `try`/`catch` catches it.\n4. **The single rule:** errors cross `await` boundaries as exceptions, so a stack of async functions behaves\n   like a stack of sync ones.\n\n- **The `return await` subtlety.** `return fetchModel()` inside a `try` block does **not** catch a rejection\n  of `fetchModel()`, because the promise is returned before it settles; `return await fetchModel()` does.\n  Outside a `try`, the two are equivalent apart from a stack frame.\n\n### Unhandled rejections\n- **The trigger.** A rejected promise with no handler attached by the end of the current microtask drain\n  fires `'unhandledRejection'`.\n- **Default since Node 15: `throw`.** The rejection is raised as an uncaught exception and the process exits,\n  unless an `'unhandledRejection'` listener is installed, which suppresses the throw.\n- **What the crash shows.** The original error and stack when the reason is an `Error`; only a non-Error\n  reason (a thrown string, say) is wrapped in an error with code `ERR_UNHANDLED_REJECTION`, which carries no\n  useful stack.\n- **The usual cause: a *floating promise*.** `logToSink(event)` called without `await` or `.catch`, or a\n  `.then` with no rejection branch.\n- **Fix the cause, not the symptom.** Every promise must be awaited, returned, passed to a combinator, or\n  given an explicit `.catch` that decides what to do. Lint for it (`@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises`).\n- **A log-only global handler is not a strategy.** A `process.on('unhandledRejection')` handler that only logs\n  turns a crash into silent data loss; it is acceptable as a last-resort reporter only.\n\n### The handler-attachment window\n\"Unhandled\" is judged per promise at the moment it rejects, not at the end of the function. That makes a\ntempting concurrency pattern dangerous:\n\n```\nconst a = callModel(p1);   // both start now: good\nconst b = callTool(t1);\nconst x = await a;         // while suspended here, b rejects -> nobody is listening yet\nconst y = await b;         // too late: the process may already have crashed\n```\n\n- **What goes wrong.** If `b` rejects while the function is still suspended on `a`, `b` has no handler, the\n  `'unhandledRejection'` check fires, and in the default mode the process exits. Attaching a handler later\n  only triggers the `'rejectionHandled'` event.\n- **The fix.** `await Promise.all([a, b])` (or `allSettled`) attaches handlers to both immediately, which is\n  the reason to use a combinator for concurrent work rather than staggered awaits.\n\n### Where propagation stops\n`await` carries errors only along promise edges. Four places break the chain:\n\n- **Callbacks scheduled from async code.** A `throw` inside `setTimeout(() => ...)`, an event-emitter\n  listener or a stream `'data'` handler is not inside your `try`; the async function has already moved on, so\n  it becomes an uncaught exception. Wrap the callback API in a promise (or use `events.once`,\n  `timers/promises`, `stream/promises`) so the error re-enters the chain.\n- **Emitter `'error'` events.** An `EventEmitter` that emits `'error'` with no listener throws. Streams and\n  SDK clients that expose events need a listener, or a promise-returning wrapper.\n- **Non-async functions that return promises.** `function call() { validate(x); return fetch(...) }` throws\n  *synchronously* if `validate` fails, so `call().catch(...)` never sees it. Marking it `async` converts the\n  throw into a rejection, making error handling uniform.\n- **Floating promises,** covered above: no edge means no propagation.\n\n### Combinators and their error semantics\n| Combinator | Fulfils | Rejects | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Promise.all(ps)` | With an array when all fulfil | With the **first** rejection; the rest run to completion unobserved, not cancelled | Any failure makes the whole result useless |\n| `Promise.allSettled(ps)` | Always, with one status object per input (below) | Never | Partial results are useful: fan-out to providers or tools where you want every outcome |\n| `Promise.any(ps)` | With the first fulfilment | Only when all reject, with an `AggregateError` whose `.errors` holds each reason | Racing equivalent providers |\n| `Promise.race(ps)` | Whichever settles first | Whichever settles first | Mostly timeouts, which `AbortSignal.timeout` does more cleanly because it also stops the work |\n\n- **`allSettled` result shape:** one `{status, value|reason}` object per input, in input order.\n- **Combinators do not cancel losers,** so pair them with an `AbortController` when the losing calls are\n  expensive (every model call is).\n\n### Keeping the cause\n- **Wrap, do not replace.** `new Error(\"tool failed\", { cause: err })` adds context; the `cause` option\n  (ES2022) keeps the chain; a custom error class keeps classification too.\n- **Carry classification.** A custom `class ProviderError extends Error` can carry `status`, `retryable` and\n  the provider's error body.\n- **Narrow before reading.** `catch (e: unknown)` forces narrowing with `instanceof` before reading fields.\n- **Prefer `await` for stack traces.** Node links async stack frames across `await`, but only when the\n  promise is awaited directly; a `.then` chain hides the originating frame.","example":"### Provider fan-out with classified errors\nFan-out to three providers with `allSettled` so every outcome is known, classify each failure with a status\nand a retryable flag, preserve the original cause when wrapping, abort the losers once a winner arrives,\nand give the fire-and-forget audit call an explicit `.catch` so it can never become an unhandled rejection.\n\n```ts\nclass ProviderError extends Error {\n  constructor(msg: string, readonly status: number, readonly retryable: boolean, opts?: { cause?: unknown }) {\n    super(msg, opts); this.name = \"ProviderError\";\n  }\n}\n\nasync function callProvider(name: string, prompt: string, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<string> {\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch(`https://${name}.example/v1/complete`, {\n      method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ prompt }), signal,\n    });\n    if (!res.ok) {\n      const body = await res.text();\n      throw new ProviderError(`${name} returned ${res.status}: ${body.slice(0, 200)}`,\n        res.status, res.status === 429 || res.status >= 500);\n    }\n    return await res.text();                 // `return await` so the try block sees a rejection\n  } catch (e: unknown) {\n    if (e instanceof ProviderError || signal.aborted) throw e; // classified, or a cancellation: not retryable\n    throw new ProviderError(`${name} transport failure`, 0, true, { cause: e });\n  }\n}\n\nasync function bestEffort(prompt: string) {\n  const ac = new AbortController();\n  const names = [\"alpha\", \"beta\", \"gamma\"];\n  let winner: string | undefined;\n  const calls = names.map((n) => callProvider(n, prompt, ac.signal).then((text) => {\n    winner ??= text; ac.abort(); return text;   // first success aborts the losers mid-flight\n  }));\n  const results = await Promise.allSettled(calls); // losers now settle quickly with AbortError\n  if (winner !== undefined) return winner;\n  const reasons = results.map((r) => (r as PromiseRejectedResult).reason);\n  const retryable = reasons.every((r) => r instanceof ProviderError && r.retryable);\n  throw new AggregateError(reasons, retryable ? \"all providers failed (retryable)\" : \"all providers failed\");\n}\n\n// Fire-and-forget logging is still a promise: attach a handler or the rejection becomes fatal.\nvoid (async () => { /* audit sink */ })().catch((e) => console.error(\"audit sink failed\", e));\n```\n\n### Watching the attachment window crash a process\nSave as `window.mjs`. `node window.mjs` prints the caught error; `node window.mjs staggered` crashes,\nbecause the fast tool rejects at 50 ms while the function is still suspended on the 200 ms model call.\n\n```js\nconst delay = (ms, fail) => new Promise((resolve, reject) =>\n  setTimeout(() => (fail ? reject(new Error(`tool failed at ${ms} ms`)) : resolve(ms)), ms));\n\nasync function staggered() {\n  const a = delay(200);          // slow model call\n  const b = delay(50, true);     // fast tool call that fails\n  try {\n    await a;                     // b rejects while we wait here: no handler yet\n    await b;\n  } catch (e) { console.log(\"caught\", e.message); }   // never reached\n}\n\nasync function combined() {\n  try { await Promise.all([delay(200), delay(50, true)]); }\n  catch (e) { console.log(\"caught\", e.message); }     // caught tool failed at 50 ms\n}\n\nawait (process.argv[2] === \"staggered\" ? staggered() : combined());\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sequential vs concurrent latency\nAn agent step needs a model call, a tool call and an embedding lookup that do not depend on each other\n(illustrative timings).\n\n| Pattern | Latency | Failure behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| `await` each in turn (1.2 s + 0.8 s + 0.3 s) | 2.3 s, the sum | Safe, but slow |\n| Start all, await one by one | 1.2 s, the max | Crashes if the 0.3 s call rejects during the 1.2 s wait |\n| Start all, `await Promise.allSettled([...])` | 1.2 s, the max | Every outcome known; retry only what failed |","patterns":["**Lint floating promises in CI.** Enable `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` (it needs type information) as an error, and require an explicit `void` plus `.catch` for intentional fire-and-forget. Track disables per file in review.","**Crash loudly, report first.** Keep Node's default `--unhandled-rejections=throw` (or use `strict`), and if you add a `process.on('unhandledRejection')` listener, make it log with the request context and then exit so the orchestrator restarts the process. Alert on restart count and on any such log line.","**One error class per boundary.** Provider, tool and validation errors each carry `status`, `retryable` and `cause`; retry logic reads `retryable` instead of parsing messages. Monitor the share of logged errors that lack a cause chain.","**Serialize the cause chain in logs.** Have the logger walk `err.cause` recursively and redact provider bodies at that boundary, rather than dropping the cause. Monitor log size per error event.","**Fan-out with `allSettled` plus abort.** Use `Promise.allSettled` with one `AbortController` per batch, and give side-effecting tools idempotency keys so a retry of one failed call cannot duplicate an email or charge. Monitor duplicate-side-effect reports and per-provider failure rates."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"fetch rejects: 429\"] --> W[\"Wrap: ProviderError with cause\"]\n    W --> A1[\"await in callProvider rethrows\"]\n    A1 --> A2[\"await in agent step rethrows\"]\n    A2 --> Q{\"try/catch on the path?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Handle: retry if retryable\"]\n    Q -->|\"no, promise floating\"| U[\"unhandledRejection check\"]\n    U -->|\"default mode throw\"| X[\"Process exits\"]\n    U -.->|\"listener installed\"| L[\"Logged only, work lost\"]","caption":"How a rejection travels: through every `await`, until a `catch` handles it, or out to the process-level\ncheck that crashes Node when nothing is listening."},"interview":[{"q":"What happens, step by step, when an awaited promise rejects inside a nested async call?","a":"- **At the innermost `await`:** the rejection is re-thrown as an exception at that expression.\n- **Up the stack:** each `async` function without a matching `catch` rejects its own returned promise, and\n  the next caller's `await` re-throws it, so it propagates like a synchronous exception.\n- **At the top:** if nothing catches it and the outermost promise has no handler, `'unhandledRejection'`\n  fires and, by default, the process exits."},{"q":"A service crashes every few hours with an unhandled rejection pointing at a metrics helper. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Find the floating call:** search for the helper's call sites without `await`, `.catch` or a combinator;\n  turn on `no-floating-promises` to list them all.\n- **Fix the edge:** await it, or make fire-and-forget explicit with `.catch` that logs and drops.\n- **Check the timing:** the crash appears only when the metrics backend fails, which explains the\n  every-few-hours pattern."},{"q":"When would you use `Promise.any` instead of `allSettled` for a multi-provider call?","a":"- **`any` when providers are equivalent** and you only need the first success; it rejects with an\n  `AggregateError` only if all fail.\n- **`allSettled` when every outcome matters,** for example to compare answers or to log every failure.\n- **Either way, abort the losers,** because neither combinator cancels them and each still costs tokens."},{"q":"How do you decide whether a provider error should be retried, and keep that decision intact through the stack?","a":"- **Classify at the boundary:** 429 and 5xx are retryable, other 4xx (such as 400) are permanent, and\n  transport failures are usually retryable; record it on a `ProviderError` with `status` and `retryable`.\n- **Wrap with `cause`** at each higher layer instead of creating a fresh error, so the retry loop and the\n  logs still see the original status and body."},{"q":"Your fan-out of five tool calls takes as long as their sum. What changed, and how do you fix it safely?","a":"- **Likely cause:** someone wrote sequential `await`s, so latency is the sum rather than the max.\n- **Safe fix:** start all five, then `await Promise.allSettled(...)` so every rejection has a handler from the\n  start; staggered awaits would reintroduce the crash window."}],"tradeoffs":["**`Promise.all`: simple vs lossy.** Fastest to fail, but discards the other outcomes and leaves them running; right only when a single failure invalidates the whole batch.","**`allSettled`: complete vs more code.** Full information at the cost of handling every outcome yourself, which is what you want for provider fan-out, tool batches and anything partially useful.","**`cause` chains: diagnostics vs exposure.** Wrapping keeps diagnostics but makes error objects larger and may carry sensitive provider bodies into logs; redact at the logging boundary rather than dropping the cause.","**Crash vs swallow.** Crashing on unhandled rejections loses in-flight requests but surfaces bugs immediately; swallowing keeps the process up while silently losing work, worse in anything that bills tokens.","**`await` vs `.then`.** Prefer `await` for readable stack traces, accepting that sequential awaits can serialize concurrent work. For concurrency, start the promises and await them through one combinator, not one by one, so every rejection has a handler from the start."],"pitfalls":["**Forgetting `await` on a call whose result you ignore** (logging, metrics, cache writes). Symptom: a crash whose stack points into a logging or cache helper rather than the request that started it (or `ERR_UNHANDLED_REJECTION` with no stack when a non-Error was thrown), or data that never arrives.","**`return promise` inside `try` instead of `return await promise`.** Symptom: a `catch` block that \"never runs\" while the error surfaces in the caller.","**Awaiting sequentially what could run concurrently** (`const a = await x(); const b = await y();`). Symptom: latency equal to the sum of calls instead of the max.","**\"Fixing\" that with staggered awaits.** Symptom: an intermittent unhandled-rejection crash that only happens when the second call fails while the first is slow.","**Throwing inside a `setTimeout` or event-listener callback** and expecting the surrounding `try` to catch it. Symptom: an `uncaughtException` crash whose stack has no request-handler frames.","**Rethrowing as a fresh `new Error(msg)` without `cause`.** Symptom: logs saying \"model call failed\" with no status code, no request id and no stack from the real failure.","**Retrying the whole batch on `Promise.all` rejection.** Symptom: duplicated tool side effects, because the calls that had succeeded are run again."],"when":"- **Default:** `async`/`await` with `try`/`catch` for all I/O.\n- **At explicit fan-out points:** `all` for all-or-nothing, `allSettled` for partial results, `any` for\n  racing equivalent providers, always paired with an `AbortController` so losers stop consuming resources\n  (whether a provider stops generating, and billing, when the client disconnects is vendor-dependent).\n- **Errors:** one error class per boundary (provider, tool, validation) carrying status and retryability,\n  wrapped with `cause`.\n- **Guardrails:** enforce `no-floating-promises` in lint and keep the default crash-on-unhandled-rejection\n  behaviour so bugs surface in staging.\n- **Measure:** the rate of unhandled rejections and of errors without a cause chain in logs.\n- **Interviews:** explain why `return await` differs inside `try`, what `all` does with the losers, and how\n  an unhandled rejection becomes a crash.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes provider and tool clients that classify errors, preserve causes, and never leave a promise floating, and runs with lint rules that catch missing awaits.","architect":"Defines the error taxonomy that crosses service boundaries (retryable vs permanent, provider vs validation) and the fan-out policy for multi-provider calls.","fde":"Traces a customer's \"random crash every few hours\" to an unawaited promise in a webhook handler, and shows how `cause` chains would have pointed at the upstream 429 immediately."},"check":{"q":"An agent service calls three tools concurrently with `Promise.all`. One tool rejects, the handler\ncatches it and retries the whole batch. Users report duplicated emails. Explain the bug and the fix.","a":"`Promise.all` rejects as soon as one promise rejects, but the other two keep running and in this\ncase one of them already sent an email. Retrying the batch re-runs that tool and sends a second email.\nThe fix has three parts: use `Promise.allSettled` so each outcome is known and only the failed tool is\nretried; give side-effecting tools an idempotency key so a retry cannot duplicate the effect; and pass an\n`AbortSignal` so that when the batch is genuinely abandoned, still-running tools are cancelled instead of\ncompleting unobserved. The error should also carry the original cause and a retryable flag so the loop\ncan decide whether a retry is even appropriate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `return fetchModel()` inside a `try` block fail to catch a rejection from `fetchModel()`, while `return await fetchModel()` catches it?","options":["`return` exits the try block synchronously before any promise is created","Without `await` the promise is returned still pending, so its rejection happens after the function has already left the try block","`await` converts the rejection into a return value of `undefined`","The two forms are identical; the difference is only in stack trace depth"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`try`/`catch` only sees exceptions thrown while control is inside the block. A returned pending promise settles later, outside the block, so the rejection propagates to the caller. `return await` suspends inside the block and re-throws there. Outside a try, the forms behave the same."},{"q":"What happens to the other promises when `Promise.all` rejects because one input rejected?","options":["They are cancelled and their rejections are suppressed","They are cancelled and their pending work is rolled back","They keep running to completion and their results are ignored","They are converted into unhandled rejections and crash the process"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Promises cannot be cancelled by the combinator; it only stops listening. The remaining work, including paid model calls and side effects, continues. Cancellation must be done cooperatively through an `AbortSignal` passed into each call."},{"q":"A service crashes on an unhandled promise rejection even though every `await` is inside a `try`. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A `catch` block rethrew the error","A promise was created without being awaited, returned, or given a `.catch`, and it rejected","`Promise.allSettled` rejected because all inputs rejected","The error object was not an instance of `Error`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rejection with no handler attached by the end of the microtask drain is unhandled, and Node's default is to raise it as a fatal exception. Floating promises from fire-and-forget calls are the usual source. `allSettled` never rejects, and a non-Error reason only changes how the crash is reported (`ERR_UNHANDLED_REJECTION` without a stack), not whether it happens."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — Stream (Backpressuring in Streams, stream.pipeline, Web Streams API)","Official docs: WHATWG Streams Standard"],"why":"- **Users should not wait for the whole answer.** An 800-token answer takes on the order of ten seconds at\n  typical decode rates of tens of tokens per second. Streaming turns that into a visibly \"typing\" response\n  with time-to-first-token in hundreds of milliseconds.\n- **Inputs are often unbounded.** A 300 MB PDF corpus to chunk and embed, a JSONL export of a million rows to\n  score, audio frames to transcribe. Loading them first means memory proportional to input size, and one\n  large input takes the process down.\n- **The two ends rarely match speed.** A model API pushes tokens faster than a phone on a poor connection can\n  acknowledge them; a file reader produces 64 KiB chunks far faster than an embedding call consumes them.\n  Without a rule, the fast side's output piles up in your process.\n- **Size it honestly.** Text chat is small (800 tokens is a few KB, perhaps tens of KB as SSE JSON events), so\n  an unbounded buffer is a latent bug. Raw 24 kHz 16-bit mono speech is 48,000 bytes per second: a 60-second\n  answer is about 2.9 MB, and a thousand stalled mobile clients is about 2.9 GB of heap. The same arithmetic\n  applies to file downloads, exports and proxied uploads.\n- **Backpressure is the actual fix.** \"Make the consumer faster\" does not exist for a network you do not\n  control. The consumer's capacity propagates backwards so the producer stops reading its source until there\n  is room; Node's stream abstractions make that, plus error and early-termination propagation, automatic.","how":"### The classic `node:stream` types\n| Type | Role | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Readable** | A source you pull from | `fs.createReadStream`, an HTTP request body, a socket |\n| **Writable** | A sink you push into | An HTTP response, a file, a socket |\n| **Duplex** | Both, independently | A TCP socket |\n| **Transform** | A Duplex whose output is computed from input | gzip, a line splitter, an SSE parser |\n\n- **Each has a buffer bounded by `highWaterMark`:** bytes for byte streams, item count in `objectMode` (where\n  the default is 16 objects). The byte default is tens of KiB and was raised in recent Node versions, so set\n  it explicitly when it matters.\n- **It is a *threshold*, not a hard cap.** The stream accepts a write beyond it but reports that it would\n  rather you stopped.\n- **Memory becomes budgetable.** Worst case per stream is roughly the sum of the `highWaterMark`s along the\n  chain plus one chunk per stage; per process, that figure times maximum concurrency.\n\n### The backpressure protocol\n1. **`writable.write(chunk)` returns `false`** when the internal buffer has reached `highWaterMark`.\n2. **The producer stops writing** and waits for the `'drain'` event, then resumes.\n3. **The readable stops calling `_read`** when nobody consumes it (the destination said stop), so the OS\n   socket buffer fills.\n4. **TCP flow control** then tells the remote server to slow down.\n\n- **End to end:** from a slow browser, through your process, back to the model provider's socket.\n- **Every link must honour the signal.** Hand-written `on('data', chunk => res.write(chunk))` loops are the\n  classic bug: they ignore the `false` return and buffer without bound.\n\n### `pipe` versus `pipeline`\n| | `readable.pipe(writable)` | `stream.pipeline(src, ...transforms, dest, cb)` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pause/drain | Yes | Yes |\n| Destination errors or closes | Source merely unpiped and left paused, never destroyed: its file descriptor or upstream HTTP connection stays open | Every stream in the chain destroyed |\n| Source errors | Destination not closed | Callback receives the error |\n| Verdict | Both directions leak | Backpressure *and* error propagation *and* cleanup |\n\n- **Promise form** lives in `node:stream/promises`. There is almost no reason to use `pipe` in a server.\n\n### Async iteration\n- **Readables are async iterables.** `for await (const chunk of readable)` pulls one chunk at a time and\n  applies backpressure naturally, because the loop body must finish before the next chunk is requested.\n- **The other direction:** `Readable.from(asyncGenerator)`.\n- **Often clearest for tokens:** an async generator yielding parsed events, consumed by a loop that writes to\n  the response and awaits `drain` when needed.\n\n### Web Streams\n- **What `fetch` returns.** In Node, `response.body` is a WHATWG `ReadableStream`, the same API as browsers and\n  the one many AI SDKs expose.\n- **Same ideas, different names:** a queuing strategy with a `highWaterMark`, `pipeThrough(transformStream)`,\n  `pipeTo(writable)` with error and abort propagation, and `reader.read()` for manual pulling.\n- **Pull-based backpressure.** A `ReadableStream`'s source `pull()` is called only while the queue's\n  `desiredSize` (high-water mark minus queued size) is positive.\n- **The `'drain'` equivalent:** `writer.ready`, a promise that resolves when there is room.\n- **Bridging.** `Readable.fromWeb()` and `Readable.toWeb()` convert between the two worlds, needed when a\n  provider SDK hands you a Web stream and your framework wants a Node stream (or the reverse). Both kinds are\n  async-iterable, so `for await` works on either.\n\n### Framing on the wire: SSE and chunked bodies\n- **TCP has no event boundaries;** a chunk may contain half a JSON event.\n- **Providers frame events with Server-Sent Events** (`data: {...}\\n\\n`), so your Transform must buffer partial\n  lines until a blank line arrives.\n- **Decode with a `StringDecoder`** (or `TextDecoderStream`), because a chunk boundary can fall inside a\n  multi-byte UTF-8 character.\n- **A `Transform` enforces backpressure per chunk.** It does not call `_transform` with the next chunk until\n  its readable side drains below `highWaterMark`. One chunk that expands into fifty events overshoots by that\n  one chunk's output, which is bounded.\n- **Where backpressure truly breaks:** a hand-written `Readable` or event-driven producer that keeps calling\n  `push()` after it has returned `false`; that buffer has no bound.","example":"### SSE-to-NDJSON proxy with `pipeline`\nA Transform that reframes a provider's SSE stream into newline-delimited JSON for the browser, wired with\n`pipeline` so a client disconnect or upstream error tears down the whole chain.\n\nOn client disconnect the\n`AbortSignal` cancels the upstream `fetch` and closes that connection; most providers stop generating when\nthe connection drops, but whether partial output is still billed is vendor-specific.\n\n```ts\nimport { Transform, Readable, type TransformCallback } from \"node:stream\";\nimport { pipeline } from \"node:stream/promises\";\nimport { StringDecoder } from \"node:string_decoder\";\nimport type { ReadableStream as WebStream } from \"node:stream/web\";\nimport type { IncomingMessage, ServerResponse } from \"node:http\";\n\nclass SseToNdjson extends Transform {\n  private buf = \"\";\n  private dec = new StringDecoder(\"utf8\");   // never splits a multi-byte character across chunks\n  override _transform(chunk: Buffer, _enc: BufferEncoding, cb: TransformCallback) {\n    this.buf += this.dec.write(chunk).replace(/\\r\\n/g, \"\\n\");   // SSE also allows CRLF\n    let idx: number;\n    try {\n      while ((idx = this.buf.indexOf(\"\\n\\n\")) !== -1) {           // one complete SSE event\n        const event = this.buf.slice(0, idx); this.buf = this.buf.slice(idx + 2);\n        for (const line of event.split(\"\\n\")) {\n          if (!line.startsWith(\"data:\")) continue;\n          const data = line.slice(5).trim();\n          if (data === \"[DONE]\") continue;                         // stream ends when upstream ends\n          this.push(JSON.stringify({ t: JSON.parse(data).delta ?? \"\" }) + \"\\n\");\n        }\n      }\n    } catch (e) { return cb(e as Error); }  // report parse errors to pipeline instead of throwing\n    cb();                                    // Transform waits for the reader before the next chunk\n  }\n  override _flush(cb: TransformCallback) {  // upstream ended: anything left is an incomplete event\n    this.buf += this.dec.end();\n    cb(this.buf.trim() ? new Error(\"stream ended mid-event\") : null);\n  }\n}\n\nexport async function proxy(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse) {\n  const ac = new AbortController();\n  res.on(\"close\", () => ac.abort());          // client went away -> stop upstream (no-op if finished)\n  const upstream = await fetch(\"https://provider.example/v1/stream\", {\n    method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ prompt: \"hi\", stream: true }), signal: ac.signal,\n  });\n  if (!upstream.ok || !upstream.body) { res.statusCode = 502; return res.end(); }\n  res.writeHead(200, { \"content-type\": \"application/x-ndjson\", \"x-accel-buffering\": \"no\" });\n  try {\n    const body = upstream.body as unknown as WebStream<Uint8Array>; // DOM vs node:stream/web typing only\n    await pipeline(Readable.fromWeb(body), new SseToNdjson(), res, { signal: ac.signal });\n  } catch (e) {\n    if (!ac.signal.aborted) console.error(\"stream failed\", e);   // disconnect is normal\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Seeing `write()` return `false`\nSave as `bp.mjs` and run `node bp.mjs`. A slow sink takes 5 ms per 4 KiB frame. Honouring `'drain'` keeps the\nbuffer at the 16 KiB threshold; delete the `await` line and the same loop buffers all 200 frames.\n\n```js\nimport { Writable } from \"node:stream\";\nimport { once } from \"node:events\";\n\nconst slow = new Writable({\n  highWaterMark: 16 * 1024,                         // 16 KiB threshold\n  write(_chunk, _enc, cb) { setTimeout(cb, 5); },   // a slow client: 5 ms per chunk\n});\n\nlet peak = 0;\nfor (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {\n  const ok = slow.write(Buffer.alloc(4096));        // one 4 KiB audio frame\n  peak = Math.max(peak, slow.writableLength);\n  if (!ok) await once(slow, \"drain\");               // honour backpressure\n}\nslow.end();\nconsole.log(`peak buffered: ${peak} bytes`);         // 16384 with the await, 819200 without\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: memory budget for a TTS gateway\nRaw 24 kHz 16-bit mono PCM is 48,000 bytes per second, streamed to mobile clients.\n\n| Design | Per stalled client | 1,000 stalled clients |\n|---|---|---|\n| Manual `'data'` → `res.write`, 60-second answer | ~2.9 MB (whole answer buffered) | ~2.9 GB heap: process killed |\n| `pipeline` with an explicit 64 KiB `highWaterMark` at each of 3 stages | ~192 KiB plus one chunk per stage | ~190 MiB, budgetable |\n| Same, plus abort upstream on client `'close'` | Freed when the client leaves | Abandoned streams stop costing memory and tokens |","patterns":["**`pipeline` everywhere, with the request's signal.** Use `pipeline(src, ...transforms, res, { signal })` from `node:stream/promises` for every proxied stream, and treat errors after an abort as normal. Monitor open file descriptors and upstream sockets after client disconnects.","**Abort upstream on client close.** Register `res.on(\"close\", () => ac.abort())` and pass `ac.signal` to the provider `fetch` or SDK call. Monitor upstream streams still open after disconnect and tokens billed for aborted requests.","**Turn off buffering in the path.** Send `X-Accel-Buffering: no` (or set `proxy_buffering off` in nginx, or the equivalent in your load balancer) and exclude streamed routes from compression middleware. Monitor time-to-first-token as measured at the client, not the server.","**Per-stream buffered-bytes metric and slow-client policy.** Sample `res.writableLength` across open responses; for small text answers, drain upstream into a bounded buffer and disconnect clients that stay over a cap. Alert on the p99 of buffered bytes per stream.","**Explicit `highWaterMark` for bulk data.** Set it per stage for audio, file and export streams, then compute worst-case memory as stages × threshold × max concurrency and size the container from that."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Model provider\"] -->|\"SSE bytes\"| R[\"Readable.fromWeb(fetch body)\"]\n    R --> T[\"Transform: SSE to NDJSON\"]\n    T --> W[\"ServerResponse\"]\n    W --> C[\"Slow mobile client\"]\n    C -.->|\"buffer over highWaterMark\"| W\n    W -.->|\"write returns false\"| T\n    T -.->|\"stop pulling\"| R\n    R -.->|\"TCP window fills\"| M\n    C -->|\"close\"| A[\"AbortController aborts fetch\"]","caption":"The happy path flows right; the backpressure signal flows back from the slow client to the provider's\nsocket. `pipeline` carries errors and aborts along the same chain."},"interview":[{"q":"What is backpressure, and why does streaming need it?","a":"- **Definition:** the consumer's capacity propagating back to the producer, so the producer pauses instead of\n  piling output into buffers.\n- **Why:** producers (model APIs, file readers) are usually faster than consumers (slow networks, embedding\n  calls), and without the signal memory grows with the number of slow consumers."},{"q":"Users say answers appear all at once although the server streams tokens. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the path, not the code:** a reverse proxy, load balancer or compression middleware is buffering the\n  response; compare timing with `curl -N` direct to the service and through the proxy.\n- **Fix:** send `X-Accel-Buffering: no` or disable proxy buffering for the route, and exclude streamed routes\n  from compression."},{"q":"When would you not apply strict end-to-end backpressure?","a":"- **Small text answers:** holding a provider connection open for a slow phone costs more than buffering tens of\n  KB, so drain upstream into a bounded buffer and disconnect clients that exceed it.\n- **Keep it strict for audio, files and exports,** where per-client buffers reach megabytes."},{"q":"How do you estimate worst-case stream memory for a service?","a":"- **Per stream:** the sum of `highWaterMark`s along the chain plus one chunk per stage.\n- **Per process:** that figure times maximum concurrent streams; for example 3 stages × 64 KiB × 1,000 streams\n  is about 190 MiB before chunk overshoot.\n- **Verify:** sample `res.writableLength` in production and compare with the budget."},{"q":"Why can `chunk.toString()` corrupt streamed text, and what is the fix?","a":"- **Cause:** a chunk boundary can split a multi-byte UTF-8 character, so each half decodes to a replacement\n  character.\n- **Fix:** keep a `StringDecoder` (or `TextDecoderStream`) per stream, which holds incomplete bytes until the\n  next chunk arrives."}],"tradeoffs":["**Low latency vs whole-output checks.** Streaming gives low time-to-first-token and bounded memory, but you cannot validate or moderate the complete output before the user sees it; add a trailing check or stream through a guard stage when the use case demands it.","**Strict backpressure vs upstream hold time.** A slow client slows the upstream read and holds a provider connection open longer. When the response is small (a text chat answer of tens of KB), draining upstream promptly into a bounded per-stream buffer and disconnecting clients that exceed it is often better. For audio and files, strict backpressure wins.","**`objectMode` convenience vs allocation.** Parsed-event streams let `highWaterMark` count events, but each object is a heap allocation; for bulk byte processing stay in `Buffer` mode.","**Web Streams portability vs Node tooling.** Web Streams are portable across runtimes (Node, browsers, edge) and match AI SDK surfaces; Node streams have richer tooling and wider framework support. Converting is cheap but easy to get wrong around error propagation.","**Bigger `highWaterMark` vs memory.** It smooths bursty producers and reduces pause/resume churn, at the cost of more memory per stream, which multiplies by concurrency."],"pitfalls":["**Ignoring the `false` return of `write()`** in a manual `'data'` handler. Symptom: heap growth proportional to the number of slow clients and eventual out-of-memory under load.","**Using `pipe` instead of `pipeline`,** so an error on the response does not destroy the upstream body. Symptom: leaked file descriptors and upstream connections held open after users disconnect.","**A custom `Readable` that keeps calling `push()` after it returns `false`** (for example, one fed by an SDK's event callbacks). Symptom: backpressure that \"works in tests\" but buffers unboundedly with real slow clients.","**Decoding each chunk with `chunk.toString()`** in a streaming parser. Symptom: occasional `\\uFFFD` replacement characters in non-English or emoji output where a character straddled two chunks.","**Buffering the whole stream (`await res.text()`) before streaming it on.** Symptom: time-to-first-token equal to total generation time.","**Proxies or compression middleware buffering the response.** Symptom: tokens arrive in one burst at the end although the server wrote them incrementally. Fix: nginx's `X-Accel-Buffering: no` header or the equivalent proxy setting, and no compression on streamed routes.","**Not aborting upstream on client disconnect.** Symptom: token spend on responses nobody receives."],"when":"- **Stream when output is incremental and a human is waiting** (chat completions, voice, progress of long agent\n  runs), and whenever input size is unbounded (files, exports, logs).\n- **Wire with `pipeline` or `pipeTo`,** not manual events; keep one `AbortController` per request tied to\n  client disconnect; build Transforms that respect chunk boundaries.\n- **Do not stream** when the consumer needs the complete result to act (a JSON tool call must be parsed whole)\n  or when the response is small and fast.\n- **Measure:** time-to-first-token, per-stream heap, and the count of upstream streams still open after client\n  disconnect.\n- **Interviews:** explain what `write()` returning `false` means, why `pipeline` beats `pipe`, and how a slow\n  browser ends up throttling a model provider.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the SSE-to-client proxy with `pipeline`, respects `drain`, converts between Web and Node streams at SDK boundaries, and aborts upstream on disconnect.","architect":"Decides where streaming is exposed in the API, sets buffer and concurrency budgets per process, and ensures proxies and gateways in the path do not buffer streamed routes.","fde":"Fixes \"the answer appears all at once\" at a customer by finding the buffering load balancer or compression layer, and explains why their memory alerts correlate with slow mobile clients."},"check":{"q":"A voice assistant gateway streams synthesized speech (raw 24 kHz 16-bit PCM) from a TTS provider\nto mobile clients. Memory climbs steadily during the evening commute and the process is eventually\nkilled. Provider latency is normal and request counts are flat. Walk through how streams could cause\nthis and how you would prove and fix it.","a":"The likely cause is missing backpressure between the provider stream and slow clients: the\nhandler reads audio as fast as the provider sends it and writes it to each response, ignoring\n`write()` returning `false`, so bytes for slow connections accumulate in the response's internal buffer.\nAt 48 KB per second of audio, a minute-long answer to a client on a stalled cellular link buffers about\n2.9 MB, so memory scales with the number of slow clients (commuters in tunnels), not with request\ncount. The same bug in a text chat gateway would barely show, because the whole answer is tens of KB;\nthat is why it survived testing. Prove it with a heap\nsnapshot showing large `Writable` buffers, or by correlating memory with the count of long-lived\nresponses. Fix by replacing manual wiring with `pipeline` (or `pipeTo`), which pauses the upstream read\nwhen the response buffer is full, and by aborting the upstream request on client `'close'` so abandoned\nstreams stop consuming memory and tokens. Add a per-stream buffered-bytes metric (`res.writableLength`\nsampled across open responses) so a regression is visible."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `writable.write(chunk)` returning `false` mean?","options":["The chunk was rejected and must be written again","The stream has ended and further writes will throw","The chunk was buffered but the buffer is at or above `highWaterMark`, so the producer should pause until `'drain'`","The underlying socket is closed and the data was lost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The write still succeeds; `false` is a request to stop producing. Ignoring it does not lose data, it grows the buffer without bound. Waiting for `'drain'` before writing again is the backpressure contract that `pipeline` implements for you."},{"q":"Why is `stream.pipeline` preferred over `readable.pipe(writable)` in a server?","options":["`pipeline` is faster because it skips the internal buffer","`pipeline` propagates errors and early closes to every stream in the chain and destroys them, while `pipe` leaves the source open","`pipe` does not implement backpressure","`pipeline` works with Web Streams and `pipe` does not"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`pipe` handles pause and drain correctly but does not forward errors or destroy the source when the destination fails, which leaves the source paused but open and leaks upstream connections. `pipeline` ties the lifetimes together and reports a single error."},{"q":"A provider SDK returns a WHATWG `ReadableStream`, and the HTTP framework expects a Node `Readable`. What is the right bridge?","options":["Read the whole stream with `await response.text()` and write the string","`Readable.fromWeb(stream)` and then `pipeline` into the response","Wrap each `reader.read()` result in `process.nextTick`","Use `JSON.parse` on the stream object directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`Readable.fromWeb` adapts the Web stream to a Node `Readable` while keeping backpressure and cancellation semantics, so `pipeline` can manage the chain. Buffering with `text()` destroys the streaming benefit, and the other options do not produce a stream at all."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — worker_threads","Official docs: MDN — Structured clone algorithm, Transferable objects, SharedArrayBuffer and Atomics"],"why":"- **Some AI steps are genuinely compute.** Counting tokens with a BPE tokenizer over a 200-page document,\n  parsing a 50 MB PDF, cosine similarity against a few hundred thousand in-memory embeddings, hashing files\n  for deduplication, decompressing a large export.\n- **On one thread they stall everyone.** Each can take hundreds of milliseconds to seconds of pure\n  JavaScript, during which streamed tokens for every other user stop and health checks fail.\n- **`async` does nothing.** It only yields at `await`, and there is nothing to await in a tight loop.\n- **Chunking and child processes fall short.** `setImmediate` chunking keeps the loop responsive but slows the\n  job and still spends the one core; two heavy requests halve each other's throughput. A child process per\n  job costs a whole Node runtime (tens of megabytes and tens of milliseconds, illustrative) and serializes\n  everything through pipes.\n- **Workers changed the trade.** The old advice was \"Node is not for CPU work; call another service\". A\n  worker is a second V8 isolate in the same process: far cheaper to start than a process, able to share\n  memory, and running real parallel work on other cores while the main loop keeps streaming.","how":"### Isolates, not shared heaps\n- **`new Worker(file, { workerData })`** starts an OS thread with its own V8 isolate and its own event loop.\n- **Nothing is shared by default:** separate heap and global scope, so no variables, module instances or open\n  sockets are shared with the parent.\n- **Why:** V8's heap is not thread-safe. Isolating heaps is *because* of that, it is what makes parallel\n  JavaScript possible at all, and it is why you cannot \"just call a function on another thread\".\n- **Cost:** each worker has its own heap and copies of loaded modules, and pays startup time to load and\n  compile its script, which is why workers are pooled and reused rather than created per request.\n\n### Message passing and structured clone\n- **Channels are `MessagePort`s:** `worker.postMessage(value)` on one side, `parentPort.on('message', ...)` on\n  the other.\n- **Values are copied by structured clone,** which handles plain objects, arrays, `Map`, `Set`, `Date`, typed\n  arrays and `Error`s.\n- **What does not survive.** Functions and symbols throw a `DataCloneError`; class instances arrive as plain\n  objects with prototype and methods gone, so a `Tokenizer` object cannot be sent, only the data to rebuild\n  one.\n- **Cloning is O(size) on the sender.** Sending a 100 MB string copies 100 MB, and the copy blocks the sending\n  thread.\n- **Design consequence:** the message is a job description (a file path, an id, a small payload); large data\n  is transferred or shared, not cloned.\n\n### Transfer and shared memory\n| Mechanism | Cost | Semantics | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Structured clone | O(size), on sender | Independent copies | Small job descriptions and results |\n| Transfer: `postMessage(value, [arrayBuffer])` | O(1) | Ownership moves; sender's view becomes detached (length 0) | A `Float32Array` of embeddings or a `Buffer` of file bytes, and the result buffer back |\n| `SharedArrayBuffer` | No copy | Genuinely shared memory; `Atomics` for sync (`Atomics.add`, `Atomics.wait`/`notify`) | Data both sides read concurrently |\n\n- **Shared memory is fast and dangerous in the usual ways:** no structural locking, so keep it to numeric\n  arrays and a clear protocol.\n\n### Pooling\n- **Shape.** A pool keeps N workers alive (typically the CPU count, minus one for the main thread), queues\n  jobs, dispatches each to an idle worker, and resolves a promise with the result.\n- **Build or borrow.** Writing one is about forty lines; `piscina` (created by Node.js core contributors) is\n  the common library and adds idle timeouts, cancellation and `AbortSignal` support.\n- **The pool is your concurrency limit.** With a queue in front, a burst of 1,000 tokenization requests\n  becomes N parallel jobs and a bounded wait, instead of 1,000 threads.\n- **Check the CPU count against the container quota.** On older Node versions and some orchestrators\n  `availableParallelism()` reflects the host, and a pool sized to 64 host cores inside a 2-CPU container just\n  thrashes.\n\n### Parallelism across jobs versus within one job\n- **A pool parallelises *across* jobs.** Eight queries on eight workers can finish in about the time of one,\n  if there are eight free cores and the work is compute-bound.\n- **Memory bandwidth can cap it.** A brute-force scan of a 1.2 GB matrix is often bandwidth-limited, so eight\n  concurrent scans share that bandwidth and the speed-up is smaller; measure rather than assume linear\n  scaling.\n- **It does nothing for one query's latency,** which still scans every vector on one thread.\n- **To cut one job's latency, split the data.** Give each of N workers a range of rows in the shared matrix,\n  have each return a local top-k, and merge the N small lists on the main thread.\n- **Cost of splitting.** Up to N× faster (less when memory bandwidth is the limit), at the cost of N messages\n  and a merge per query; it occupies the whole pool for one request, so it fits low-concurrency,\n  latency-sensitive paths, not high-throughput ones.\n\n### Cancelling work inside a worker\n- **A busy worker cannot hear \"stop\".** In a tight synchronous loop its own event loop is blocked, so a\n  `'message'` is never handled; an `AbortSignal` does not help either, since the abort is a callback the busy\n  worker never runs.\n\n| Option | How | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| `worker.terminate()` | Stops the thread at the next safe point regardless of what it is doing | Reliable, but throws away the worker; the pool must spawn a replacement and pay startup again |\n| Shared cancel flag | Job checks a flag in a `SharedArrayBuffer` with `Atomics.load` every few thousand iterations; main thread sets it with `Atomics.store` | Keeps the worker alive, but the job must cooperate |\n\n- **Libraries such as `piscina`** map an `AbortSignal` onto these mechanisms for you.\n\n### Memory limits per worker\n- **Each worker has its own V8 heap limits.**\n- **Cap them:** `new Worker(file, { resourceLimits: { maxOldGenerationSizeMb } })` makes one runaway document\n  parse kill that worker (an `'error'` the pool can handle) instead of pushing the process into the\n  container's out-of-memory killer.\n- **Budget:** container memory = main heap + N × worker heap + shared buffers.\n\n### Workers versus `cluster` and child processes\n| Option | What it scales | Isolation | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `cluster` or replicas behind a load balancer | N copies of the whole server use N cores for I/O-bound traffic; each still blocks on its own CPU work | Per process | N full runtimes |\n| Worker threads | One server, CPU work moved aside | Shared process | One heap per worker |\n| Child processes | Separate programs | Strong: a crash or native-module segfault does not take down the server | Higher memory and IPC |\n\n- **Common combination:** replicas for I/O scale, plus a worker pool inside each replica for CPU steps.\n\n### What workers are not for\n- **I/O-bound work gains nothing:** a worker waiting on an HTTP call is a wasted thread.\n- **Native code already off the loop** (the async `crypto` and `zlib` APIs use libuv's pool) does not need a\n  worker.\n- **Multi-machine or crash-durable work** belongs in a job queue (see the BullMQ lesson), not in threads.\n- **Observability gets harder:** logs, traces and metrics from inside a worker need to be forwarded or tagged,\n  because the worker has its own globals.","example":"### A minimal pool for top-k similarity\nA minimal pool that dispatches top-k cosine-similarity jobs to workers, transferring the query vector instead\nof cloning it.\n\nThe embedding matrix lives in a `SharedArrayBuffer`: passing an ordinary `Float32Array` as\n`workerData` would structured-clone the whole ~1.2 GB matrix into every worker. The worker file and the pool\nlive in separate modules; this listing shows both with the worker guarded by `isMainThread`.\n\n```ts\nimport { Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData } from \"node:worker_threads\";\nimport { availableParallelism } from \"node:os\";\n\ntype Job = { query: Float32Array; k: number };\ntype Hit = { index: number; score: number };\n\nif (!isMainThread) {\n  const vectors = workerData.vectors as Float32Array;      // flattened [n, dim], L2-normalised, shared\n  const dim = workerData.dim as number;\n  parentPort!.on(\"message\", ({ query, k }: Job) => {\n    const hits: Hit[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0, n = vectors.length / dim; i < n; i++) {\n      let dot = 0;                                          // dot of unit vectors = cosine\n      for (let d = 0; d < dim; d++) dot += query[d]! * vectors[i * dim + d]!;\n      hits.push({ index: i, score: dot });\n    }\n    hits.sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);\n    parentPort!.postMessage(hits.slice(0, k));             // small result: cloning is fine\n  });\n} else {\n  class Pool {                                              // minimal: no crash replacement\n    private idle: Worker[] = []; private queue: Array<() => void> = [];\n    constructor(file: URL, data: unknown, size = Math.max(1, availableParallelism() - 1)) {\n      for (let i = 0; i < size; i++) this.idle.push(new Worker(file, { workerData: data }));\n    }\n    run(job: Job): Promise<Hit[]> {\n      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n        const start = () => {\n          const w = this.idle.pop()!;\n          const onError = (e: Error) => { w.off(\"message\", onMsg); reject(e); };\n          const onMsg = (hits: Hit[]) => {\n            w.off(\"error\", onError); this.idle.push(w); this.queue.shift()?.(); resolve(hits);\n          };\n          w.once(\"message\", onMsg).once(\"error\", onError);\n          w.postMessage(job, [job.query.buffer as ArrayBuffer]); // transfer: O(1), query detached here\n        };\n        this.idle.length ? start() : this.queue.push(start);\n      });\n    }\n  }\n  const dim = 1536, n = 200_000;                            // 200k x 1536 x 4 B = ~1.2 GB\n  const vectors = new Float32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(n * dim * 4)); // shared, not cloned per worker\n  const pool = new Pool(new URL(import.meta.url), { vectors, dim });\n  const hits = await pool.run({ query: new Float32Array(dim).fill(0.01), k: 5 });\n  console.log(hits[0]);                                    // main loop stayed free to serve streams\n}\n```\n\n### Cooperative cancellation with a shared flag\nSave as `cancel.mjs` and run `node cancel.mjs`. The worker polls a shared `Int32Array` every 100,000\niterations, so the main thread can stop it after 200 ms without `terminate()` and without a worker restart.\n\n```js\nimport { Worker, isMainThread, workerData, parentPort } from \"node:worker_threads\";\n\nif (!isMainThread) {\n  const flag = new Int32Array(workerData.flag);\n  let i = 0;\n  while (true) {\n    i++;                                                   // stand-in for a long scoring or parse loop\n    if (i % 100_000 === 0 && Atomics.load(flag, 0) === 1) break;   // cheap poll, not every iteration\n  }\n  parentPort.postMessage(`stopped after ${i} iterations`);\n} else {\n  const flag = new Int32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(4));\n  const w = new Worker(new URL(import.meta.url), { workerData: { flag: flag.buffer } });\n  w.once(\"message\", (m) => console.log(m));\n  setTimeout(() => Atomics.store(flag, 0, 1), 200);       // user abandoned the request after 200 ms\n}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing a pool in a 4-CPU, 4 GB container\nIllustrative numbers for an ingestion service that also answers chat.\n\n| Item | Choice | Memory |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main thread heap (HTTP, streaming) | 1 thread | ~0.5 GB |\n| Worker pool | 4 CPUs − 1 = 3 workers, `maxOldGenerationSizeMb: 512` each | ≤ 1.5 GB |\n| Shared embedding matrix | 200k × 1536 × 4 B in a `SharedArrayBuffer` | ~1.2 GB, once |\n| Total budget | main + 3 × worker + shared | ~3.2 GB, leaving headroom under 4 GB |\n| If the pool were sized from 64 host cores | 63 workers | Thrashing, and up to ~32 GB of worker heap allowed |","patterns":["**Use a maintained pool library.** `piscina` with `maxThreads` set from the container CPU quota, a bounded `maxQueue`, an `idleTimeout`, and `run(task, { signal })` for cancellation. Monitor queue size, wait time and run time per job.","**Size from the quota, not the host.** Read the CPU limit from an environment variable set by the deploy (or verify `availableParallelism()` inside the container) and keep one core for the main loop. Monitor event-loop lag on the main thread and CPU throttling on the container.","**Cap every worker's heap.** Pass `resourceLimits: { maxOldGenerationSizeMb }` so one malformed document kills a worker, not the pod; replace workers on `'error'` and `'exit'`. Alert on worker restart rate and pool size dropping below target.","**Transfer in, clone out.** Send file bytes and vectors in the transfer list and return small results; keep read-mostly matrices in a `SharedArrayBuffer`. Watch main-thread lag around `postMessage` calls for accidental large clones.","**Tag worker telemetry.** Include the worker's `threadId` and the job id in every log line, and propagate the trace id in the job message, since workers have their own globals."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"HTTP request on main loop\"] --> Q[\"Pool queue (bounded)\"]\n    Q --> I{\"Idle worker?\"}\n    I -->|\"no\"| Q\n    I -->|\"yes\"| W[\"Worker isolate on another core\"]\n    R -->|\"transfer ArrayBuffer, O(1)\"| W\n    S[(\"SharedArrayBuffer matrix\")] -.->|\"read, no copy\"| W\n    W -->|\"clone small top-k result\"| M[\"Main thread resolves promise\"]\n    M --> O[\"Stream reply, loop never blocked\"]\n    W -.->|\"error or exit\"| X[\"Pool replaces worker\"]","caption":"The main thread keeps serving I/O while jobs queue for a fixed pool; large inputs are transferred or shared,\nand only small results are cloned back."},"interview":[{"q":"How do worker threads differ from threads in Java or Go?","a":"- **No shared heap:** each worker is a separate V8 isolate with its own heap, globals and module instances,\n  because V8's heap is not thread-safe.\n- **Communication is by message:** structured clone copies, transfer moves `ArrayBuffer` ownership, and only\n  `SharedArrayBuffer` gives shared memory, guarded by `Atomics`."},{"q":"After adding a worker pool, the main thread still lags during big uploads. What do you check?","a":"- **Accidental clones:** a large `Buffer` or string posted without the transfer list is copied on the main\n  thread, blocking it for the copy.\n- **Work still inline:** parsing or `JSON.parse` of the result may still run on the main thread; profile with\n  `--cpu-prof`.\n- **Oversized pool:** more workers than container CPUs starves the main thread of a core."},{"q":"When would you choose a child process or a separate service over a worker thread?","a":"- **Isolation:** native modules that can segfault, or untrusted code, are safer in a process whose crash does\n  not take down the server.\n- **Durability and scale:** work that must survive restarts or exceed one machine belongs in a job queue with\n  separate worker processes."},{"q":"How would you size a worker pool and its memory for a 4-CPU container?","a":"- **Threads:** about CPU quota minus one, so 3 workers, verified against what the runtime reports inside the\n  container.\n- **Memory:** main heap + 3 × per-worker `maxOldGenerationSizeMb` + shared buffers must fit under the\n  container limit with headroom.\n- **Validate under load:** watch queue depth, per-job time and RSS, and adjust."},{"q":"How do you cancel a job that is already running in a worker?","a":"- **`terminate()`** is reliable but discards the worker, so the pool pays startup for a replacement.\n- **A shared cancel flag** polled with `Atomics.load` keeps the worker alive but needs the job to cooperate;\n  prefer it when users routinely abandon requests."}],"tradeoffs":["**Parallelism vs memory.** Workers give true parallelism for JavaScript on multi-core machines, but every worker is a separate heap, so memory scales with pool size; on a small container two workers may be all you can afford.","**Clone vs transfer.** Structured clone is convenient and safe; transfer is O(1) but detaches the sender's buffer. Choose transfer for large payloads and accept the discipline of not touching them afterwards.","**Shared memory vs races.** `SharedArrayBuffer` avoids copying entirely and suits read-mostly data like an embedding matrix, but reintroduces data races JavaScript otherwise never has; keep it to numeric arrays with `Atomics` guarding writes.","**Bounded pool vs slot blocking.** A pool bounds concurrency and amortizes startup, but a job that blocks a worker for seconds blocks that slot; give long jobs their own pool or move them to a queue.","**`terminate()` vs cancel flag.** `terminate()` cancels instantly but costs a worker restart; a shared-memory flag is cheap but needs the job to poll it. Terminate wins for rare cancellations of untrusted or third-party code; the flag wins when cancellation is routine (users abandoning requests).","**Workers vs a separate service.** Workers are simpler to deploy and share the process's memory, yet they die with the process and cannot scale beyond one machine; durable or horizontally scaled work belongs in a job queue."],"pitfalls":["**A new `Worker` per request.** Symptom: latency dominated by worker startup and memory spikes under load, often worse than running the job inline.","**Large buffers without the transfer list.** Symptom: the main thread stalls on the copy, the very problem the worker was meant to solve.","**Reading a `TypedArray` after transferring its buffer.** Symptom: zero-length arrays or `TypeError`s about detached buffers far from the `postMessage` call.","**Workers for I/O-bound steps** like calling the model API. Symptom: no throughput gain plus extra memory and harder debugging.","**Forgetting workers have their own globals.** Symptom: missing logs and traces, or a database client initialized twice because the module was loaded again in the worker.","**Not handling `'error'` and `'exit'`.** Symptom: a pool that silently shrinks after a worker crashes and eventually hangs with all jobs queued.","**Expecting a timeout or abort to stop a worker mid-computation.** Symptom: the request returns an error while the worker's CPU stays at 100% and its pool slot stays busy until the job finishes."],"when":"- **Use when** a code path does sustained synchronous computation (more than a few tens of milliseconds,\n  repeatedly) that must not stall the event loop and fits in one machine's memory: tokenization at scale,\n  document parsing, embedding math in JS, compression and hashing of large payloads.\n- **Prefer instead:** `setImmediate` chunking for rare, medium-sized jobs; native or WASM libraries that\n  release the thread when they exist; a job queue for anything durable or spread across machines.\n- **Measure:** event-loop lag before and after, per-job time inside the worker, pool queue depth, and process\n  RSS.\n- **Interviews:** why `async` does not help, how data crosses to a worker, and when a worker is the wrong tool;\n  be ready to compare with Python's multiprocessing and the GIL.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Moves tokenization and parsing into a pooled worker, transfers buffers instead of cloning them, and forwards worker logs and errors so failures are visible.","architect":"Sets the rule for what runs on the loop, in workers, or in a separate job service, and sizes pools against container CPU and memory limits.","fde":"Spots a customer's chunking pipeline parsing PDFs on the request thread, demonstrates the loop-lag fix with a pool, and explains why their 2-core container caps the gain."},"check":{"q":"A document-ingestion endpoint parses uploaded PDFs and tokenizes them in the request handler.\nDuring bulk uploads the chat endpoint in the same process becomes unresponsive. A colleague proposes\nwrapping the parsing in an `async` function and calling it without `await`. Evaluate the proposal and\ngive a better design.","a":"The proposal does nothing: `async` only yields at `await` points, and parsing is a synchronous\nloop, so it still runs to completion on the event-loop thread and the chat streams still freeze. The\nreal fix is to move parsing and tokenization into a pool of worker threads sized to the container's\nCPUs, send each job the file bytes as a transferred `ArrayBuffer` (not a clone), and return the chunked\ntext as the result. The pool also bounds concurrency so a bulk upload becomes a queue rather than a\nthundering herd. If uploads can exceed the memory of one process or must survive restarts, push jobs\ninto a durable queue with separate worker processes instead, and keep the API process for I/O only.\nMeasure event-loop lag to confirm the chat path no longer stalls."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does moving a CPU-heavy function into an `async` function not stop it from blocking the event loop?","options":["`async` functions run on the libuv thread pool but return to the main thread for results","`async` only yields control at `await`, and a synchronous loop has no await, so it still runs to completion on the main thread","`async` functions are compiled to generators that are slower, making the block worse","The event loop pauses microtasks while an async function runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`async` changes the return type to a promise and allows `await` inside; it does not move code to another thread. Only an actual `await` on a pending promise suspends the function. True parallelism for JavaScript requires a worker thread."},{"q":"What happens to a `Float32Array` on the main thread after its buffer is listed in the transfer list of `postMessage`?","options":["It is copied, and both sides hold independent data","It becomes read-only on the main thread","Its buffer is detached, so the main-thread view has length 0 and the worker owns the memory","It is shared, so writes on either side are visible to both"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Transfer moves ownership without copying, which is why it is O(1), and the sender's view is detached to prevent two threads touching the same memory. Shared visibility requires a `SharedArrayBuffer`; structured clone without a transfer list copies the data."},{"q":"Which workload benefits least from a worker-thread pool?","options":["Tokenizing thousands of documents for chunking","Making concurrent HTTP requests to a model provider","Computing cosine similarity across an in-memory embedding matrix","Parsing large PDF uploads into text"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP requests are I/O-bound and already non-blocking on the main thread; a worker waiting on a socket adds memory and complexity with no throughput gain. Workers pay off for sustained synchronous computation that would otherwise stall the loop."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — AbortController, AbortSignal, events.once, timers/promises","Official docs: WHATWG DOM Standard — AbortController and AbortSignal"],"why":"- **Model calls are the most expensive thing a Node service does:** seconds of latency, tokens billed as they\n  are produced, and a connection held open for the duration.\n- **Work gets abandoned constantly.** Users close a tab, hit stop or re-send a prompt; agent loops decide\n  half-way a tool result is no longer needed; timeouts must give up on a slow provider and fall back. The\n  question is always how to make work *stop*, not just stop waiting for it.\n- **Promises offer no answer.** A promise is a handle to a result, not to the work: `Promise.race` against a\n  timer settles your await, but the HTTP request keeps streaming, the provider keeps generating, and the\n  tokens keep costing money.\n- **The old way was ad hoc.** Every API had its own `req.destroy()`, `stream.destroy()`, `clearTimeout()`, and\n  composing them across a call tree meant threading callbacks by hand. `AbortController` and `AbortSignal`,\n  borrowed from the browser, give one object every cancellable API understands.\n- **Cooperative *because* JavaScript cannot preempt.** The runtime cannot safely unwind a function holding a\n  database transaction or a half-written file. A signal is a broadcast each layer checks or subscribes to, so\n  cancellation flows down the request's path and cleanup happens where resources were acquired.","how":"### The two objects\n`const ac = new AbortController()` creates a controller with one `ac.signal`.\n\n| Signal member | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `aborted` | Boolean |\n| `reason` | Whatever `abort(reason)` was given; defaults to a `DOMException` named `AbortError` |\n| `throwIfAborted()` | Throws the reason if already aborted |\n| `'abort'` event | Fires once on abort |\n\n- **The split is deliberate.** The controller stays with the owner who may cancel; the signal, which can only\n  be observed, is handed to everyone else, so a callee cannot abort its caller's work by accident.\n\n### How built-in APIs consume it\n- **`fetch(url, { signal })`** rejects with `signal.reason` and closes the connection; with a streaming body,\n  reading from `response.body` also rejects. The closed connection is how the provider learns to stop; most\n  stop generating on disconnect, but confirm for yours.\n- **Timers:** `setTimeout` from `node:timers/promises` takes `{ signal }` and rejects early when aborted (with\n  an `AbortError` whose `cause` is the signal's reason).\n- **Events:** `events.once(emitter, 'name', { signal })` stops waiting.\n- **Streams:** `stream.addAbortSignal(signal, stream)` and `pipeline(..., { signal })` destroy the chain.\n- **Others:** child processes, `fs` promises, `readline` and most newer APIs accept a signal.\n- **Provider SDKs** pass it to the request: the OpenAI and Anthropic Node SDKs take a `signal` request option,\n  the Vercel AI SDK calls it `abortSignal`.\n\n### Composing signals\n- **`AbortSignal.timeout(ms)`** returns a signal that aborts with a `TimeoutError` after the delay.\n- **`AbortSignal.any([a, b])`** (Node 20.3 and later) aborts when any input aborts, carrying that input's\n  reason.\n- **Together, one line:** \"stop when the client disconnects *or* after 30 s *or* when the parent agent step is\n  cancelled\". Before `any` existed, this was done by hand with listeners forwarding one abort to another\n  controller.\n\n### Making your own code cancellable\nThree idioms cover nearly everything:\n\n1. **Check at loop boundaries.** `signal.throwIfAborted()` at the top of each iteration of an agent loop or\n   chunk-processing loop stops a long computation at the next safe point.\n   - **It only works if the loop *yields*** between iterations (an `await` on real I/O, or `setImmediate`).\n     `abort()` is itself called from a callback — the socket `'close'` handler, the timeout's timer — and no\n     callback runs while a synchronous loop holds the thread, so a loop that never awaits sees\n     `signal.aborted === false` until it finishes.\n   - **CPU-bound work** that must be cancellable either yields in slices or runs in a worker that can be\n     terminated.\n2. **Subscribe for cleanup.** `signal.addEventListener('abort', () => conn.release(), { once: true })` when you\n   hold a resource with no signal-aware API. Remove the listener when work finishes normally, otherwise a\n   long-lived signal accumulates listeners for every call made with it and leaks.\n3. **Pass it down.** Every async function that does I/O takes `signal` as a parameter and forwards it. The call\n   tree becomes a cancellation tree, with one controller at the root per request.\n\n### Distinguishing abort from failure\n- **How an abort surfaces:** a rejection whose `name` is `'AbortError'` (or `'TimeoutError'` for the timeout\n  signal), and `signal.reason` tells you *why*.\n- **Only with the right reason.** That holds if you abort with the default reason or an `AbortError`-named\n  `DOMException`: `fetch` and streams reject with whatever reason you pass, so `abort(new Error(\"bye\"))`\n  produces a rejection named plain `Error`.\n\n| Outcome | Retry? |\n|---|---|\n| Provider 503 | Yes |\n| Client disconnect | Never |\n| Timeout | Maybe, against a different provider |\n\n- **Why it matters.** Code that treats every rejection as a failure retries cancelled calls and bills tokens\n  for a user who already left. Check `signal.aborted` (or `error.name`) before classifying.\n\n### Deadlines across hops\n- **A timeout is really a *deadline*:** the request must finish by time T.\n- **Each hop gets the time remaining,** not its own fresh 30 s, or the inner call keeps working (and billing)\n  after the outer caller has given up.\n- **Mechanics:** compute `remaining = deadline - Date.now()` at each hop, create\n  `AbortSignal.timeout(remaining - margin)` combined with the parent signal, and pass the deadline downstream\n  explicitly (a header or a field such as gRPC's deadline).\n- **Because a signal does not cross a process boundary** — only the closed connection does.\n\n### Where the request-level controller comes from\n1. **The root signal comes from the connection:** `res.on('close')` (or the framework's request abort event;\n   in `fetch`-style handlers it is `request.signal`) fires when the client goes away.\n2. **Create one controller per request** and abort it on close.\n3. **Combine with a timeout.**\n4. **Pass the result into** the model call, the tool calls and any streams. Cancelling the parent cancels\n   everything underneath because they all share one signal.","example":"### Request-scoped signal through a streaming model call\nA request-scoped signal combining client disconnect, a hard timeout, and an agent-step cancellation, passed\nthrough a streaming model call, with the abort reason used to decide whether the error is retryable. The\nlistener cleanup in `withResource` shows the pattern for resources that have no signal-aware API.\n\n```ts\nimport { setTimeout as sleep } from \"node:timers/promises\";\nimport type { IncomingMessage, ServerResponse } from \"node:http\";\n\nfunction requestSignal(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse, timeoutMs: number) {\n  const client = new AbortController();\n  // fetch rejects with this exact reason, so keep its name \"AbortError\" (not a plain Error)\n  res.once(\"close\", () => client.abort(new DOMException(\"client disconnected\", \"AbortError\")));\n  return AbortSignal.any([client.signal, AbortSignal.timeout(timeoutMs)]);\n}\n\nasync function streamModel(prompt: string, signal: AbortSignal, onToken: (t: string) => void) {\n  const res = await fetch(\"https://provider.example/v1/stream\", {\n    method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ prompt, stream: true }), signal,\n  });\n  const dec = new TextDecoder();                  // stream: true keeps split UTF-8 characters intact\n  for await (const chunk of res.body!) {          // rejects with signal.reason when the signal fires\n    signal.throwIfAborted();                       // also stop between chunks if parsing is slow\n    onToken(dec.decode(chunk, { stream: true }));\n  }\n}\n\nasync function withResource<T>(signal: AbortSignal, fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  const conn = { release() { console.log(\"released\"); } };\n  let released = false;                          // abort and finally both fire on cancellation\n  const release = () => { if (!released) { released = true; conn.release(); } };\n  signal.addEventListener(\"abort\", release, { once: true });\n  try { return await fn(); }\n  finally { signal.removeEventListener(\"abort\", release); release(); }\n}\n\nexport async function handler(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse) {\n  const signal = requestSignal(req, res, 30_000);\n  try {\n    await withResource(signal, () => streamModel(\"Explain backpressure\", signal, (t) => res.write(t)));\n    res.end();\n  } catch (e: unknown) {\n    const name = (e as { name?: string }).name;\n    if (name === \"AbortError\") return;                       // user left: no retry, no error log\n    if (name === \"TimeoutError\") {                          // headers may be sent once tokens flowed\n      if (!res.headersSent) res.statusCode = 504;\n      return res.end(\"\\n[provider timeout]\");\n    }\n    throw e;                                                 // genuine failure: let the error layer classify it\n  }\n}\nvoid sleep;                                                  // sleep(ms, undefined, { signal }) for cancellable waits\n```\n\n### `Promise.race` keeps billing; a signal stops it\nSave as `race.mjs` and run `node race.mjs` (about 7 seconds). A fake provider bills one token every 100 ms.\nThe race \"times out\" at 1 s but the work runs on to all 50 tokens; the signal version stops at 9.\n\n```js\nimport { setTimeout as sleep } from \"node:timers/promises\";\n\nasync function fakeProvider(signal, stats) {      // bills one token every 100 ms until stopped\n  for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) {\n    await sleep(100, undefined, { signal });      // rejects once the signal aborts\n    stats.tokens++;\n  }\n}\n\nconst raced = { tokens: 0 };\nawait Promise.race([fakeProvider(undefined, raced), sleep(1000)]);\nconsole.log(\"race returned at 1 s, tokens:\", raced.tokens);      // 9\nawait sleep(4500);\nconsole.log(\"4.5 s later, still billing:\", raced.tokens);        // 50\n\nconst stopped = { tokens: 0 };\nawait fakeProvider(AbortSignal.timeout(1000), stopped)\n  .catch((e) => console.log(\"rejected:\", e.name, \"cause:\", e.cause?.name)); // AbortError, cause TimeoutError\nawait sleep(1000);\nconsole.log(\"signal version, 1 s later:\", stopped.tokens);       // 9, no growth\n```\n\n- **Note the rejection shape.** `timers/promises` rejects with an `AbortError` whose `cause` is the\n  `TimeoutError`, while `fetch` rejects with the `TimeoutError` itself. Classify from `signal.reason` when you\n  have the signal, rather than trusting one error name.\n\n### Worked scenario: one deadline across three hops\nGateway deadline 30 s; each hop subtracts elapsed time and a 500 ms margin (illustrative).\n\n| Hop | Elapsed on arrival | Timeout it sets | Without propagation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gateway → agent service | 0 s | 30 − 0.5 = 29.5 s | 30 s |\n| Agent → tool service (after planning) | 6 s | 24 − 0.5 = 23.5 s | Fresh 30 s: could run to 36 s |\n| Tool → model provider (after retrieval) | 10 s | 20 − 0.5 = 19.5 s | Fresh 30 s: could run to 40 s, billed after the user got a 504 |","patterns":["**One root controller per request.** In middleware, create the controller, abort it on `res` `'close'` (or use `request.signal` in `fetch`-style frameworks), combine with `AbortSignal.timeout`, and attach the result to the request context. Monitor sockets to providers still open after request end.","**Signal parameter as a library contract.** Every internal client takes `{ signal }` and forwards it to `fetch` or the SDK's `signal`/`abortSignal` option; reviews reject I/O functions without one. Track aborts by reason (client, timeout, parent) as separate counters.","**Propagate deadlines, not timeouts.** Send the absolute deadline downstream in a header or a gRPC deadline, and at each hop build `AbortSignal.timeout(remaining - margin)`. Monitor work completed after the caller's deadline.","**Timeouts from measured latency.** Set per-route timeouts from provider p99 plus headroom, not round numbers, and review them when the model or prompt size changes. Watch the timeout rate per provider.","**Abort-aware retry and logging.** The retry layer skips `AbortError`, may fall back on `TimeoutError`, and logs aborts at info level, not as errors. Monitor token spend on aborted requests."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"res close: client left\"] --> CL[\"client AbortController\"]\n    T[\"AbortSignal.timeout 30 s\"] --> ANY[\"AbortSignal.any\"]\n    CL --> ANY\n    P[\"parent agent step signal\"] --> ANY\n    ANY --> F[\"fetch to model provider\"]\n    ANY --> TL[\"tool calls\"]\n    ANY --> S[\"stream pipeline\"]\n    F -->|\"socket closed\"| PR[\"Provider stops generating\"]\n    ANY -.->|\"reason\"| R{\"AbortError or TimeoutError?\"}\n    R -->|\"AbortError\"| N[\"No retry, not an error\"]\n    R -->|\"TimeoutError\"| FB[\"Maybe fall back\"]","caption":"One root signal per request, built from client disconnect and a deadline, fans out to every operation;\nwhichever fires first stops them all."},"interview":[{"q":"Why are `AbortController` and `AbortSignal` separate objects?","a":"- **Capability split:** the controller can cancel, the signal can only observe, so the owner keeps the\n  controller and hands signals to callees.\n- **Why it matters:** a library cannot cancel its caller's other work by accident, and one signal can be\n  shared safely across a whole call tree."},{"q":"Users hit stop, but provider usage keeps growing for minutes. How do you find and fix it?","a":"- **Confirm:** count sockets to the provider that stay open after the request ended, and compare billed\n  tokens with delivered tokens.\n- **Find the gap:** usually `res.on('close')` is not wired to a controller, or one layer (an SDK wrapper, a\n  retry helper) does not forward the signal.\n- **Fix:** root controller per request, aborted on close and passed to every call; add a metric so it stays\n  fixed."},{"q":"When should you not abort an in-flight operation?","a":"- **Side-effecting tools mid-call:** cutting off an email send or a database write can leave external state\n  half-done; cancel before the call, or make the tool idempotent and let it finish.\n- **Must-complete work** such as audit logging should not take the request's signal at all."},{"q":"How do you set and propagate timeouts in a three-hop agent call chain?","a":"- **Use one deadline:** set it at the edge from measured p99, then give each hop `remaining - margin`, not a\n  fresh timeout.\n- **Cross process boundaries explicitly:** send the deadline in a header or gRPC deadline, because a signal\n  does not cross a process; only the closed connection does."},{"q":"How does your retry layer tell a client cancellation from a timeout?","a":"- **Read the reason:** `signal.reason` (or the rejection's `name`) is `AbortError` for a client disconnect and\n  `TimeoutError` for `AbortSignal.timeout`.\n- **Act differently:** never retry the first; the second may fall back to another provider within the\n  remaining deadline. Beware wrappers such as `timers/promises`, which put the reason in `cause`."}],"tradeoffs":["**Safe cleanup vs weakest link.** Cooperative cancellation is safe (cleanup runs where resources live) but one function that ignores the signal keeps the work alive, so coverage is a discipline, not a feature.","**Output savings vs sunk cost.** Aborting a model stream stops further output tokens on providers that cancel on disconnect, but the full prompt and every token already generated are still billed; cancelling very early saves the most, very late saves almost nothing.","**Stopping vs half-done side effects.** Aborting mid-tool-call can leave external state half-done (an email sent, a row inserted); for side-effecting tools cancel *before* the call or make the tool idempotent rather than cutting it off.","**One signal vs per-branch control.** A single request-level signal is simple, but an agent may want to cancel one branch and keep the rest; child controllers combining the parent via `AbortSignal.any` give that granularity at the cost of more plumbing.","**Real stop vs premature stop.** Timeouts as signals stop the work, unlike `Promise.race`, but a too-short timeout on a slow-but-fine provider turns success into waste; set them from measured p99 latency."],"pitfalls":["**`Promise.race` with a timer as a \"timeout\".** Symptom: the request returns 504 while the provider keeps streaming and the token bill keeps growing.","**Not wiring `res.on('close')` to a controller.** Symptom: upstream connections and token spend continue for minutes after users closed their tabs, visible as open sockets outliving requests.","**Adding `'abort'` listeners to a long-lived signal without removing them.** Symptom: `MaxListenersExceededWarning` and memory growth proportional to calls made under that signal.","**Retrying on every rejection, including `AbortError`.** Symptom: duplicate model calls after cancellation and bills that do not match request counts.","**Using the response stream after `abort()`.** Symptom: confusing `ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE` errors reported as failures rather than expected cancellations.","**Forgetting `AbortSignal.timeout` counts from creation.** Symptom: a timeout that fires early because the signal was created long before the request it guards.","**`throwIfAborted()` in a synchronous loop that never awaits.** Symptom: cancellation \"works\" in tests with I/O but does nothing for a CPU-heavy step, which runs to completion after the client has left."],"when":"- **Always:** one controller per inbound request, its signal passed into every model call, tool call, stream\n  and timer of that request, combined with a timeout.\n- **In agent loops:** add a per-step controller so a planner can abandon a branch, and check\n  `throwIfAborted` at the top of loops that do real work.\n- **Treat `AbortError` as normal** in logs and metrics, separate from failures, and never retry it.\n- **Skip the machinery** only for fire-and-forget work that must complete regardless, such as audit logging.\n- **Measure:** aborts by reason (client, timeout, parent), token spend on aborted requests, and sockets open\n  after request end.\n- **Interviews:** usually probe why promises cannot be cancelled and how a signal propagates through a call\n  tree.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Threads a `signal` parameter through every I/O function, wires client disconnect and timeouts into it, and classifies `AbortError` separately from provider failures in the retry layer.","architect":"Sets the timeout budget per hop and the policy for cancelling side-effecting tools, and makes \"accepts an AbortSignal\" a requirement for internal client libraries.","fde":"Finds the customer's gateway that keeps streaming after users hit stop, quantifies the wasted token spend, and adds disconnect-to-abort wiring as the first-day fix."},"check":{"q":"A team wraps every model call in `Promise.race([call(), timeoutAfter(20_000)])` and reports that\ntimeouts \"work\" because requests return in 20 seconds. Their token bill is higher than their request\nvolume predicts. Explain what is happening and how to fix it properly.","a":"`Promise.race` settles the await when the timer fires, but it does nothing to the underlying HTTP\nrequest: the socket stays open, the provider keeps generating, and every token is billed until the\nresponse finishes on its own. The same applies to retries: the retry starts while the original call is\nstill running, so two calls are billed. The fix is to create an `AbortSignal.timeout(20_000)`, combine\nit with the client-disconnect signal using `AbortSignal.any`, and pass the result to `fetch` or the SDK's\n`signal` option, so the socket is destroyed when the deadline passes and generation stops. The error\nsurfaces as a `TimeoutError`, which the retry layer can handle deliberately. Measuring sockets open\nafter the deadline and token usage per request before and after will show the difference."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does JavaScript use cooperative cancellation through `AbortSignal` instead of letting a promise be cancelled directly?","options":["Promises are immutable, so adding a cancel method would break the specification","The runtime cannot safely preempt running code that holds resources, so each layer must observe the signal and clean up where the resource was acquired","Cancellation is handled by the operating system, not the language","Direct cancellation would require every promise to run on a separate thread"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A promise is a handle to a result, not to the work. Forcibly unwinding a function mid-transaction or mid-write would corrupt state, so the signal is a broadcast that cooperating code checks or subscribes to. The cost is that an ignoring layer keeps work alive."},{"q":"What is the difference between `Promise.race` with a timer and `AbortSignal.timeout` passed to `fetch`?","options":["There is none; both stop the request after the delay","`Promise.race` is faster because it avoids creating a signal","`Promise.race` only stops waiting while the request continues; the signal destroys the request so upstream generation stops","`AbortSignal.timeout` resolves with partial data instead of rejecting"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The race settles your promise but leaves the socket open and the provider generating billed tokens. A signal passed to `fetch` aborts the request, which closes the connection and makes the promise reject with a `TimeoutError` that can be handled explicitly."},{"q":"An `AbortError` reaches a retry wrapper that retries any rejection. What is the correct behaviour?","options":["Retry once, since the abort may have been spurious","Retry against a different provider, because the first was cancelled","Do not retry; the abort means the caller no longer wants the result","Convert it to a 500 error so the client can decide"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An abort is a decision by the owner of the request (client disconnect, parent cancellation) that the work is no longer wanted. Retrying it spends tokens for nobody. Only a timeout signal, identified by its `TimeoutError` reason, may justify a fallback attempt."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","sources":["Official docs: Zod","Official docs: JSON Schema specification"],"why":"- **Every interesting input is untrusted.** A request body can be anything the client sent. A model's \"JSON\"\n  can be a markdown-fenced blob with a trailing comment, a number where a string was asked for, or a field\n  the tool never defined. An environment variable is a string even when the code needs a port number.\n- **A compile-time type here is a comment, not a check.** `JSON.parse(body) as ChatRequest` tells the\n  compiler to trust you; the first missing field becomes `undefined` deep in business logic, usually a\n  `TypeError` after the model call was billed.\n- **Hand-written checks drift.** `if (typeof x.messages !== \"object\")` checks diverge from the type the rest of\n  the code uses and are never complete.\n- **Two definitions disagree.** A TypeScript interface *and* a separate JSON Schema for the model doubles the\n  surface, so the model is told one shape and the code checks another. Validating only at HTTP misses queue\n  messages, model outputs and tool results.\n- **One definition, three uses.** The static type (`z.infer`), the runtime parser (`schema.parse`) and the\n  model contract (JSON Schema). The principle is \"parse, don't validate\": a parse returns a *new* value of a\n  narrower type, so later code cannot forget the check happened.","how":"### Schema as a value\n- **Built by composition:** `z.object({ model: z.string(), temperature: z.number().min(0).max(2).default(1),\n  messages: z.array(Message).min(1) })`.\n- **Each combinator carries both** the runtime checks and a phantom static type, which\n  `z.infer<typeof Schema>` extracts.\n- **Type from schema, not the reverse,** *because* a type cannot be inspected at runtime, while a value can be\n  both executed and reflected on.\n\n### Input type vs output type\n| Type | What it describes | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `z.input<typeof S>` | What the producer may send | Whoever constructs the payload, including the model |\n| `z.output<typeof S>` | What `parse` gives back | The code that consumes it |\n\n- **`z.infer` is the output side:** `z.output<typeof S>` (which is what `z.infer` returns) is what `parse`\n  gives back.\n- **They differ** whenever a field has a `.default()` (optional on input, required on output) or a\n  `.transform()` (a string in, a `Date` out).\n\n### `parse` versus `safeParse`\n| Call | Returns | Use where |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Schema.parse(input)` | The typed value, or throws a `ZodError` | Boundaries where failure becomes an HTTP 400 through a central error handler |\n| `Schema.safeParse(input)` | `{ success: true, data }` or `{ success: false, error }`, never throws | Failure is an expected branch, such as a model output you intend to repair |\n\n- **`error.issues`** is an array of `{ path, code, message }`: what you return to a client and what you feed\n  back to a model.\n\n### Transform, refine, coerce\n- **Change the value while parsing:** `z.string().transform((s) => s.trim())`, `z.coerce.number()` for a port\n  read from `process.env`, `.refine((v) => v.end > v.start, { message })` for cross-field rules, `.pipe()` to\n  chain a transform into a further schema.\n- **Transforms are the point of `parse`:** the output type differs from the input type, so the result is a\n  domain value, not the wire shape.\n- **Async refinements cost a round trip.** Checks that need I/O (does this `customerId` exist?) can be async,\n  but then only `parseAsync`/`safeParseAsync` run them; keep them few, because they turn a cheap pure parse\n  into a database round trip at every boundary.\n- **Brand the output.** `.brand<\"CustomerId\">()` makes it a branded type, so code requiring a `CustomerId`\n  cannot be handed an unparsed string.\n\n### Objects: strict, strip, passthrough\n| Mode | Unknown keys | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Default (*strip*) | Silently removed | General parsing |\n| `.strict()` | Rejected | Tool arguments (an unexpected key usually means the model misread the schema) and request bodies, where dropping a typo'd field hides a client bug |\n| `.passthrough()` | Kept | Forwarding provider payloads you do not fully model |\n\n- **Zod 4 naming.** It keeps these methods but prefers the equivalent `z.strictObject()` and `z.looseObject()`\n  constructors.\n- **Tagged variants.** `z.discriminatedUnion(\"type\", [...])` validates stream events and similar, and gives\n  the compiler the same narrowing as a hand-written union.\n\n### From schema to the model, and back\nThe round trip is: schema → JSON Schema → model → JSON text → `safeParse` → typed value.\n\n1. **Generate the JSON Schema from Zod.** Zod 4 ships `z.toJSONSchema`; earlier versions use the\n   `zod-to-json-schema` package; the OpenAI Node SDK has Zod helpers and the Vercel AI SDK accepts Zod schemas\n   directly. `.describe()` strings become the field descriptions the model reads.\n2. **Generate the *input* schema** (`{ io: \"input\" }` in Zod 4), because a field with `.default()` is optional\n   on input but present on output.\n3. **Check your provider's subset.** Strict structured-output modes accept only part of JSON Schema: some\n   reject or ignore keywords such as `format` or length bounds, and some require every property to be listed\n   as required.\n4. **Always parse the reply,** even with provider-side constrained decoding: it is the only check for\n   `refine` rules, it guards against provider bugs, and a failed parse becomes a repair prompt (\"the field\n   `due_date` must be ISO 8601; you returned `tomorrow`\").\n\n### Alternatives in the ecosystem\n| Library | Style | Suits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Valibot | Same model as Zod | Smaller bundles |\n| TypeBox, Ajv | JSON-Schema-first | Teams whose source of truth is an OpenAPI document |\n| ArkType | A type-syntax string instead of chaining | Preference for TS-like syntax |\n\n- **The discipline matters more than the library:** one schema per boundary, the type derived from it, and no\n  `as` casts on untrusted data.","example":"### Tool schema, JSON Schema and a repair loop\nA tool definition whose Zod schema produces both the TypeScript argument type and the JSON Schema sent to the\nmodel, with a repair loop that feeds validation issues back when the model's arguments do not parse. Unknown\nkeys are rejected because an extra key from a model usually means it misread the schema.\n\n```ts\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ScheduleArgs = z.object({\n  title: z.string().min(1).describe(\"Short title for the calendar event\"),\n  start: z.iso.datetime({ offset: true })                 // without offset: only a trailing \"Z\"\n    .describe(\"ISO 8601 start time with timezone offset\"),\n  durationMinutes: z.number().int().min(5).max(480).default(30),\n  attendees: z.array(z.email()).max(20).default([]),\n}).strict();\ntype ScheduleArgs = z.infer<typeof ScheduleArgs>;       // output: durationMinutes: number (required)\ntype ScheduleInput = z.input<typeof ScheduleArgs>;      // input: durationMinutes?: number\n\nexport const scheduleTool = {\n  name: \"schedule_event\",\n  description: \"Create a calendar event\",\n  // Zod 4 APIs throughout (on Zod 3: z.string().datetime(), z.string().email(), zod-to-json-schema).\n  // io \"input\" describes ScheduleInput: defaulted fields are optional for the model.\n  parameters: z.toJSONSchema(ScheduleArgs, { io: \"input\" }),\n  run: async (args: ScheduleArgs) => ({ id: \"evt_123\", ...args }),\n};\n\ntype ModelCall = (messages: Array<{ role: string; content: string }>) => Promise<string>;\n\nexport async function callToolWithRepair(rawArgs: string, model: ModelCall, maxRepairs = 2) {\n  let text = rawArgs;\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRepairs; attempt++) {\n    let json: unknown = undefined;\n    try { json = JSON.parse(text); } catch { /* reported as a syntax problem below */ }\n    const result = ScheduleArgs.safeParse(json);\n    if (result.success) return scheduleTool.run(result.data);   // result.data is typed and trusted\n    const issues = json === undefined ? \"(root): not valid JSON\" : result.error.issues\n      .map((i) => `${i.path.join(\".\") || \"(root)\"}: ${i.message}`).join(\"\\n\");\n    if (attempt === maxRepairs) throw new Error(`tool args invalid after repairs:\\n${issues}`);\n    text = await model([\n      { role: \"system\", content: \"Return only JSON matching the schema; fix the listed problems.\" },\n      { role: \"user\", content: `Previous arguments:\\n${text}\\n\\nProblems:\\n${issues}` },\n    ]);\n  }\n  throw new Error(\"unreachable\");\n}\n\nconst port = z.coerce.number().int().min(1).max(65535).parse(process.env.PORT ?? \"3000\");\nvoid port;\n```\n\n### Boot-time config and a request body that returns 400\nZod 4. Config is parsed once at startup so a bad deploy crashes immediately, not on the first request. The\nrequest parser returns path and message only, never the raw input, so client responses cannot leak payloads.\n\n```ts\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst Env = z.object({\n  PORT: z.coerce.number().int().min(1).max(65535).default(3000),\n  MODEL_API_KEY: z.string().min(1),\n  ENABLE_TOOLS: z.stringbool().default(false),           // \"false\" -> false, unlike z.coerce.boolean()\n  LOG_LEVEL: z.enum([\"debug\", \"info\", \"warn\", \"error\"]).default(\"info\"),\n});\nexport const env = Env.parse(process.env);                // throws at boot with every bad variable listed\n\nconst ChatRequest = z.strictObject({\n  model: z.enum([\"fast\", \"smart\"]),\n  messages: z.array(z.strictObject({\n    role: z.enum([\"user\", \"assistant\"]),\n    content: z.string().min(1).max(32_000),\n  })).min(1).max(100),\n  temperature: z.number().min(0).max(2).default(1),\n});\nexport type ChatRequest = z.output<typeof ChatRequest>;\n\nexport function parseChatRequest(body: unknown) {\n  const r = ChatRequest.safeParse(body);\n  if (r.success) return { ok: true as const, data: r.data };   // temperature filled in: 1\n  return {\n    ok: false as const,\n    status: 400,\n    issues: r.error.issues.map((i) => ({ path: i.path.join(\".\") || \"(root)\", message: i.message })),\n  };\n}\n// parseChatRequest({ model: \"gpt-x\", messages: [], temprature: 0.2 }) yields three issues:\n// model (invalid option), messages (too small), (root) (unrecognized key \"temprature\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a model upgrade raises tool-argument failures\nA team tracks `safeParse` failures per tool after switching model versions (illustrative numbers).\n\n| Metric for `schedule_event` | Before | After upgrade | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Argument parse failure rate | 0.5% | 6% | The model's behaviour changed, not the code |\n| Top issue path | mixed | `start` (invalid ISO datetime) | It now sends local times without an offset |\n| Repair success within 2 attempts | 90% | 70% | Repairs cost an extra call each; fix the root cause |\n| Fix | — | `.describe()` text gives an example with offset; failures back near baseline | The schema description is part of the prompt |","patterns":["**Validate config at boot.** Parse `process.env` with one schema in the entry module, using `z.coerce.number()` for ports and `z.stringbool()` or an enum transform for flags, so a bad deploy fails its readiness check instead of serving errors. Monitor crash-on-start in deploy dashboards.","**Central `ZodError` → 400 mapping.** Route handlers call `parse`; one error handler converts `ZodError` to a 400 with `path` and `message` only (Zod 4's `z.flattenError` helps), never raw input. Monitor 400 rate per route and per issue path to spot client bugs.","**Shared contracts package.** Tool, request and event schemas live in one package consumed by every service, with types derived via `z.infer`, so producers and consumers cannot drift. Track schema changes in review like API changes.","**Additive schema evolution.** Add new fields as optional with `.default()`, deploy all producers (including queued messages and prompts), then tighten. Monitor validation failures by producer version during rollouts.","**Bounded repair with metrics.** `safeParse` model output, send `issues` back at most two times, then fail explicitly. Emit failure rate, repair success rate and extra tokens per tool; a rising failure rate usually means a prompt or model change."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Z[\"Zod schema\"] --> T[\"z.infer: static type\"]\n    Z --> J[\"z.toJSONSchema, io input\"]\n    J --> M[\"Model tool call\"]\n    M --> X[\"JSON text arguments\"]\n    X --> P{\"safeParse\"}\n    P -->|\"success\"| R[\"Typed value: run tool\"]\n    P -->|\"issues\"| B{\"Repairs left?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Repair prompt with issues\"]\n    F --> M\n    B -->|\"no\"| E[\"Fail the call\"]","caption":"One schema feeds the model's contract and the parser that checks the model's reply; invalid replies loop back\nas repair prompts, a bounded number of times."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"parse, don't validate\" mean, and why does it matter for AI services?","a":"- **Definition:** a parse turns `unknown` input into a new value of a narrower type, or fails; a validator only\n  answers yes or no and leaves the original untyped value in circulation.\n- **Why here:** model outputs, tool arguments and webhooks are untrusted, and a parsed value means every later\n  line can rely on the shape without re-checking or casting."},{"q":"A tool keeps running with missing parameters although the model \"sent them\". How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect stripping:** the default object mode drops unknown keys, so a misspelled or extra key from the\n  model vanishes silently; log the raw arguments next to the parsed ones.\n- **Fix:** make tool-argument schemas `.strict()` so the mismatch becomes an issue fed back into a repair\n  prompt, and improve `.describe()` text if the model keeps misnaming the field."},{"q":"When would you not validate with a runtime schema?","a":"- **Between already-typed internal modules:** the compiler already checks those calls, and re-parsing adds CPU\n  and code with no new safety.\n- **On hot paths with large arrays you produced yourself,** validate once at the edge and trust inside."},{"q":"How do you change a schema that queued messages and prompts already depend on?","a":"- **Additive first:** add the new field as optional with `.default()`, so old producers still parse.\n- **Then migrate producers:** deploy writers, drain or reprocess queued messages, update prompts and tool\n  descriptions.\n- **Tighten last,** and watch validation failure rates by producer version during the rollout."},{"q":"What do you monitor around schema validation in production?","a":"- **Failure rate per boundary and per issue path:** a jump on model outputs after a deploy usually means a\n  prompt or model change; on HTTP, a client bug.\n- **Repair metrics:** repair success within the attempt bound and extra tokens spent, since each repair is\n  another model call."}],"tradeoffs":["**One schema vs coupling.** One schema gives type, parser and model contract together, but couples the wire format to the domain type; when they must diverge, add a `transform` or a second schema rather than casting.","**Safety vs CPU.** Validating every boundary costs CPU proportional to payload size, negligible against a model call but noticeable on hot internal paths with large arrays; validate at the edge, trust inside.","**`.strict()` vs forward compatibility.** Strict catches model and client mistakes early, yet breaks when a provider adds a harmless field; use `strip` for payloads you forward and `strict` for arguments you act on.","**Repair vs latency and cost.** A repair prompt improves success rates at the cost of another model call and latency; bound the attempts and prefer provider structured-output modes that reduce failures in the first place.","**JSON-Schema-first vs TypeScript-first.** TypeBox and Ajv align with OpenAPI-driven teams; Zod is more ergonomic for application code. Mixing both in one service is where contracts drift.","**Tighter schemas vs old producers.** A new required field or narrower enum protects consumers but breaks producers still sending the old shape (queued messages written before a deploy, a prompt not yet updated). Add fields as optional with a default first, and tighten only once every producer has moved."],"pitfalls":["**Casting untrusted JSON with `as` instead of parsing.** Symptom: a `TypeError` on an `undefined` field deep in business logic, after the model call was billed.","**A TypeScript interface and a hand-written JSON Schema kept separately.** Symptom: the model returns a field the code rejects, or the code expects a field the model was never told about.","**Default `strip` on tool arguments.** Symptom: a hallucinated extra parameter is silently dropped, so the tool runs with half of what the model intended.","**Returning raw `ZodError` messages to end users or logging full payloads in issues.** Symptom: leaked internal field names or PII in client responses and logs.","**Skipping the parse because the provider \"guarantees\" the schema.** Symptom: `refine` rules never run, and a provider-side change or bug reaches production unchecked.","**Reading `process.env` without coercion.** Symptom: `\"3000\" + 1` style bugs and a port compared as a string.","**`z.coerce.boolean()` for env flags.** It applies JavaScript truthiness, so `FEATURE_X=false` turns the feature *on* because `\"false\"` is a non-empty string. Parse with an enum (`z.enum([\"true\", \"false\"]).transform((v) => v === \"true\")`) or Zod 4's `z.stringbool()` instead."],"when":"- **Parse at every boundary where data enters the process:** HTTP bodies and query strings, webhook payloads,\n  queue messages, environment configuration, model outputs and tool-call arguments, and responses from\n  third-party APIs you depend on.\n- **Derive types from schemas,** generate the model's JSON Schema from the same source, and use `safeParse`\n  with a bounded repair loop where the model is the producer.\n- **Avoid when** calls are internal between already-typed modules; that is what the compiler is for.\n- **Measure:** the validation failure rate per boundary (a rising rate on model outputs usually means a prompt\n  or model change) and the repair success rate.\n- **Interviews:** why types alone are insufficient, how a Zod schema becomes a tool definition, and how to\n  handle invalid model output.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines one Zod schema per tool and per endpoint, derives types with `z.infer`, generates the tool's JSON Schema from it, and routes `ZodError` issues into 400 responses or repair prompts.","architect":"Decides where the schemas live (a shared contracts package), which boundaries are strict versus permissive, and how schema versions evolve alongside prompts and tool definitions.","fde":"Audits a customer's agent for `as` casts on model output, replaces them with parsed schemas, and uses the validation failure rate to show which tools the model misuses most."},"check":{"q":"A team argues that since their provider supports structured outputs with constrained decoding,\nvalidating the model's JSON with Zod on their side is redundant work. What do you tell them?","a":"Constrained decoding makes the output syntactically match the JSON Schema, which removes most\nfailures, but it does not remove the need to parse. First, the JSON Schema cannot express everything the\nZod schema checks: cross-field `refine` rules, date validity, semantic ranges; and providers' strict\nmodes support only a subset of JSON Schema, so some keywords you did write may not be enforced. Output\ncut off by the max-token limit, or a refusal, can still arrive as invalid JSON. Second, the parse is what\nproduces a typed value; without it the code is still operating on `unknown` or on an `as` cast, so the\ncompiler cannot help. Third, the provider is an external system that can change or have bugs, and the\nparse is the only place that failure is caught before a tool runs with bad arguments. Finally, the\nschema is already written, so the validation is a single `safeParse` call whose cost is negligible next\nto the model call. The constrained mode reduces repairs; it does not replace the boundary check."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why derive the TypeScript type from the Zod schema with `z.infer` rather than writing the interface first and the schema to match?","options":["`z.infer` produces faster code because it skips type checking","A schema is a runtime value that can both validate and be reflected into JSON Schema, while a type is erased, so the schema must be the single source of truth","Interfaces cannot describe optional fields, but schemas can","Zod refuses to compile when an interface exists for the same shape"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Types exist only at compile time; nothing can execute or inspect them. Making the schema primary means the type, the parser and the model-facing JSON Schema cannot drift apart. Interfaces describe optional fields fine; they simply cannot check anything at runtime."},{"q":"When should a tool-argument schema use `.strict()` instead of Zod's default of stripping unknown keys?","options":["Never; stripping is always safer because it avoids rejecting valid calls","Only when the tool has no optional parameters","When an unexpected key likely means the model misread the contract, so rejecting and repairing is better than silently dropping it","When the JSON Schema is generated by the provider instead of the code"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A model that adds an undefined parameter has misunderstood the tool, and stripping it would run the tool with a different intent than the model had. Strict mode surfaces the mismatch so the issue can be fed back for repair. Stripping remains appropriate for payloads you merely forward."},{"q":"A model's tool arguments fail `safeParse`. What is the most robust next step?","options":["Run the tool with whatever fields parsed correctly","Cast the raw JSON to the argument type and continue","Send the parse issues back to the model in a bounded repair loop, and fail the call if it still does not parse","Retry the identical prompt until the output parses"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The issues carry the path and reason for each failure, which is exactly the feedback a model needs to correct its output. Bounding the attempts prevents runaway cost, and failing explicitly beats running a side-effecting tool with partial or unchecked arguments."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","sources":["Fowler, 2004 — Inversion of Control Containers and the Dependency Injection pattern","Official docs: NestJS","Official docs: Awilix"],"why":"- **AI handlers are glue around external calls.** A typical handler does five things: load a tenant's\n  config, fetch context from Postgres and a vector store, call an LLM through an SDK client, write the\n  result plus usage numbers, and emit a trace. Those calls are expensive, non-deterministic and\n  credentialed.\n- **The obvious wiring hurts by day ten.** `import { openai } from './clients'`, where `clients.ts` does\n  `export const openai = new OpenAI()`, works on day one. Then tests either call the real API (slow,\n  paid, flaky, secrets in CI) or use module mocking (`vi.mock('./clients')`), which couples tests to\n  file paths and silently diverges from the real wiring.\n- **Singletons cannot vary.** An enterprise customer wants an Azure endpoint with their own key, but a\n  module-level singleton has exactly one key. \"Use Anthropic when OpenAI keeps returning 429 or 5xx\"\n  cannot be expressed when the dependency is a fixed import. The singleton is also built at import\n  time, before config is validated or the process knows it is a server, worker or script, so startup\n  order becomes accidental.\n- **The naive fix is global mutable state.** \"Export a `setClient()` setter\" lets concurrent requests\n  from different tenants race on it.\n- **DI makes the dependency an explicit parameter.** It is decided once at the edge of the program, so\n  core logic is pure with respect to its collaborators. The key word is *explicit*: a function's\n  signature tells you everything it touches.","how":"### The shape of the pattern\n1. **Define the dependency by capability, not library.** An interface\n   `LLM { complete(req): Promise<Completion> }` is far narrower than the OpenAI SDK's surface. The\n   handler depends on it *because* then a fake, a caching wrapper or another vendor can satisfy it.\n   Structural typing means no `implements` keyword is required: any object with the right shape works.\n2. **Inject through the constructor or a factory argument.** `createChatService({ llm, db, clock })` or\n   `class ChatService { constructor(private deps: Deps) {} }`. Setter and field injection are avoided\n   *because* an object that can exist half-wired is a bug waiting for a code path.\n3. **Compose at one root.** A `main.ts` (or `createApp()`) reads env, constructs the pool, the Redis\n   client and the SDK client, then the services, then the HTTP routes. All `new` calls for\n   infrastructure live here and nowhere else.\n4. **Let the root own lifecycle.** It starts things in dependency order and shuts them down in reverse.\n5. **Tests build a different graph.** `createChatService({ llm: fakeLLM, db: testDb, clock: fixed })`.\n   No module mocks, no monkey-patching, no environment variables.\n\n### Composing behaviour at the root\nEvery adapter satisfies the same `LLM` interface, so cross-cutting behaviour is added by wrapping one\nimplementation in another (the decorator pattern), only at the root:\n\n```\nllm = withCache(redis,                       // outermost: a hit skips everything below\n        withFallback(                        // on failure or open breaker, try the next provider\n          withBreaker(openaiAdapter),        // per provider, so one outage does not trip the other\n          withBreaker(anthropicAdapter)))\n```\n\n| Layer | Position | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cache | outermost | a hit should cost neither a retry nor a breaker slot |\n| Breaker | wraps exactly one provider | its state describes that provider's health |\n| Vendor SDK retries | innermost | a fallback is taken only after a provider has genuinely failed |\n\n- **The handler sees none of it.** It still calls `llm.complete()`.\n- **Tests can prove the fallback.** Wire the same stack around a fake that fails on demand.\n- **Without injection** each of these behaviours ends up as an `if` inside business logic.\n\n### Scopes\n| Scope | Examples | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Singleton | pool, Redis, SDK client | expensive to construct and they own sockets |\n| Request | tenant id, trace context, remaining budget, per-tenant model routing | built per request from singletons plus the request |\n\n- **Request context in Node.** Create a `RequestContext` in middleware and pass it down, or use\n  `AsyncLocalStorage` for ambient values such as the trace id.\n- **`AsyncLocalStorage` is complementary, not a replacement.** Good for cross-cutting context you would\n  otherwise thread through every signature; bad for real dependencies, *because* hidden ambient\n  dependencies recreate the testability problem DI solves.\n- **Containers differ on scope mismatches.** NestJS \"bubbles\" request scope up the chain: a provider\n  that injects a request-scoped one silently becomes request-scoped itself (correct, but rebuilt on\n  every request). A hand-wired root, or a container without that rule, lets a singleton capture the\n  first request's object and keep it.\n\n### Containers and decorators\n- **NestJS** uses class decorators (`@Injectable()`) and constructor parameter types, resolved through\n  `reflect-metadata`. It needs the `experimentalDecorators` and `emitDecoratorMetadata` compiler\n  options.\n- **Interfaces are erased at compile time,** so anything that is not a class must be registered under an\n  explicit token (a string or a `Symbol`).\n- **tsyringe and InversifyJS** work the same way. **Awilix** resolves by parameter name or an explicit\n  registration map and needs no decorators.\n- **A container buys** automatic graph construction, scoped lifetimes (request scope in NestJS,\n  `createScope()` in Awilix) and lazy construction.\n- **It costs** a runtime resolution step, errors that surface at boot instead of compile time, and a\n  learning curve.\n- **Rule of thumb:** for a dozen dependencies a hand-written root is clearer; past a few dozen with\n  request scopes, a container pays for itself.\n\n### What it is not\n- **Not \"make everything an interface\".** Pure functions and value types are not injected.\n- **Inject what is slow, stateful, non-deterministic or credentialed:** network clients, databases,\n  caches, clocks, randomness, the file system.","example":"### Interface, service, root and test\nThree files compressed into one listing: a capability interface, a service that depends only on it, the\ncomposition root that knows about vendors, and a test that swaps in a fake. The service never imports a\nvendor SDK, so the test runs with no network and no API key.\n\n```ts\n// llm.ts — the capability, not the vendor\nexport interface LLM { complete(p: { system: string; user: string; maxTokens: number }): Promise<{ text: string; inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number }> }\n\n// chat-service.ts — depends only on interfaces\nexport interface Deps { llm: LLM; db: { saveUsage(tenant: string, tokens: number): Promise<void> }; now: () => Date }\nexport function createChatService({ llm, db, now }: Deps) {\n  return {\n    async answer(tenant: string, question: string) {\n      const r = await llm.complete({ system: `Today is ${now().toISOString()}`, user: question, maxTokens: 512 });\n      await db.saveUsage(tenant, r.inputTokens + r.outputTokens);\n      return r.text;\n    },\n  };\n}\n\n// main.ts — the composition root: the only place that knows about vendors\nimport OpenAI from \"openai\";\nimport { Pool } from \"pg\";\nconst pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });\nconst openai = new OpenAI();                                  // reads OPENAI_API_KEY here, nowhere else\nconst llm: LLM = {\n  async complete({ system, user, maxTokens }) {\n    const res = await openai.chat.completions.create({ model: process.env.MODEL ?? \"gpt-4o-mini\", max_completion_tokens: maxTokens,\n      messages: [{ role: \"system\", content: system }, { role: \"user\", content: user }] });\n    return { text: res.choices[0].message.content ?? \"\", inputTokens: res.usage?.prompt_tokens ?? 0, outputTokens: res.usage?.completion_tokens ?? 0 };\n  },\n};\nconst db = { saveUsage: async (t: string, n: number) => { await pool.query(\"insert into usage(tenant, tokens) values ($1, $2)\", [t, n]); } };\nexport const chat = createChatService({ llm, db, now: () => new Date() });\n\n// chat-service.test.ts — no network, no mocking of module paths\nconst calls: string[] = [];\nconst svc = createChatService({\n  llm: { complete: async (p) => { calls.push(p.user); return { text: \"42\", inputTokens: 10, outputTokens: 1 }; } },\n  db: { saveUsage: async () => {} },\n  now: () => new Date(\"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\"),\n});\n// expect(await svc.answer(\"acme\", \"meaning?\")).toBe(\"42\"); expect(calls).toEqual([\"meaning?\"]);\n```\n\n### A fallback decorator and the test that proves it\nThe decorator satisfies `LLM`, so the service cannot tell it apart from a single provider. Only\nrate limits and server errors fall back; a 400 is the caller's bug and must surface.\n\n```ts\n// with-fallback.ts — composes two LLMs behind one interface\nimport type { LLM } from \"./llm\";\n\nconst retryable = (e: unknown) => {\n  const status = (e as { status?: number }).status;   // OpenAI and Anthropic SDK errors carry .status\n  return status === 429 || (status !== undefined && status >= 500);\n};\n\nexport function withFallback(primary: LLM, secondary: LLM, shouldFallBack = retryable): LLM {\n  return {\n    async complete(p) {\n      try {\n        return await primary.complete(p);\n      } catch (e) {\n        if (!shouldFallBack(e)) throw e;               // 400s, auth errors: do not hide them\n        return secondary.complete(p);\n      }\n    },\n  };\n}\n\n// with-fallback.test.ts — fakes that fail on demand, no network\nconst rateLimited: LLM = { complete: async () => { throw Object.assign(new Error(\"rate limited\"), { status: 429 }); } };\nconst badRequest: LLM = { complete: async () => { throw Object.assign(new Error(\"bad\"), { status: 400 }); } };\nconst backup: LLM = { complete: async () => ({ text: \"from backup\", inputTokens: 5, outputTokens: 2 }) };\nconst req = { system: \"\", user: \"hi\", maxTokens: 16 };\n// expect((await withFallback(rateLimited, backup).complete(req)).text).toBe(\"from backup\");\n// await expect(withFallback(badRequest, backup).complete(req)).rejects.toThrow(\"bad\");\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one enterprise tenant needs Azure\nThe same change request, with and without injection.\n\n| Step | Module singleton | Injected `LLM` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Add Azure endpoint and key for tenant `acme` | a second global client plus an `if (tenant === \"acme\")` in the handler | a new adapter built in the root |\n| Route per tenant | branch inside business logic | a router chosen from tenant config at request time |\n| Test the routing | mock `./clients` by path, hope it matches real wiring | pass two fakes, assert which one was called |\n| Add Anthropic fallback | another `try/catch` in every handler | wrap adapters with `withFallback` once |\n| Files touched in business code | every handler that calls the LLM | none |","patterns":["**Validate config before building the graph.** When the root starts, parse env with a schema (for example zod) before constructing any client, so a missing key fails boot with a clear message instead of on the first request. Monitor boot failures in deploys.","**Per-tenant adapter router.** When tenants bring their own endpoint or key, build adapters lazily from tenant config and cache them in a `Map` keyed by tenant id, rebuilding on config change. Monitor the number of cached adapters and error rate per tenant.","**One contract suite for fakes and real adapters.** Run the same interface tests against the fake on every commit and against the real SDK (sandbox key or recorded responses) nightly, so the fake cannot drift. Monitor the nightly job; a red run means the fake lies.","**Reverse-order shutdown from the root.** The root keeps a list of disposers registered as it builds the graph; on `SIGTERM` it closes the HTTP server, then workers, then `pool.end()` and the Redis client. Monitor shutdown duration against the orchestrator's grace period.","**Ban infrastructure imports outside the root.** An ESLint `no-restricted-imports` rule that forbids `openai`, `@anthropic-ai/sdk` and `pg` in service folders keeps the seam honest. Monitor CI lint failures."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    ENV[\"Env and config\"] --> ROOT[\"Composition root: main.ts\"]\n    ROOT --> POOL[(\"Postgres pool\")]\n    ROOT --> SDK[\"Vendor SDK clients\"]\n    SDK --> AD[\"LLM adapters\"]\n    AD --> DEC[\"Cache, fallback, breaker wrappers\"]\n    DEC -->|\"LLM interface\"| SVC[\"ChatService(Deps)\"]\n    POOL -->|\"db interface\"| SVC\n    MW[\"Request middleware\"] -->|\"tenant, trace\"| CTX[\"RequestContext\"]\n    CTX --> SVC\n    SVC --> R[\"HTTP routes\"]\n    T[\"Unit test\"] -.->|\"fake LLM, fixed clock\"| SVC","caption":"Only the composition root knows vendors and env; services see interfaces, requests add scoped context,\nand tests build a different graph around the same service."},"interview":[{"q":"What is dependency injection, and why does it matter more in AI services than in CRUD apps?","a":"- **Definition:** a unit receives its collaborators as parameters instead of importing or constructing\n  them, and one composition root builds the real graph.\n- **Why AI makes it urgent:** most of the work is slow, paid, non-deterministic, credentialed calls.\n  Hard-wired clients mean tests hit the network, tenants cannot get their own endpoint, and provider\n  fallback needs a rewrite."},{"q":"Tests pass locally but one tenant's requests occasionally use another tenant's model. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect a scope mismatch first:** a request-scoped object (tenant config, API key, span) captured\n  by a singleton at first use, which only shows up under concurrency.\n- **Confirm:** log tenant id alongside the adapter or config identity per request, and replay two\n  tenants concurrently in a test.\n- **Fix:** build tenant-specific objects per request from singletons, or let the container make the\n  consumer request-scoped (NestJS bubbling), and add a concurrency test."},{"q":"When would you not use a DI container in a TypeScript service?","a":"- **Small services and libraries:** a dozen dependencies read more clearly as a hand-written root, and\n  missing wiring stays a compile error instead of a boot-time error.\n- **When decorators are a cost:** containers like NestJS need `emitDecoratorMetadata` and tokens for\n  interfaces, because interfaces are erased at runtime.\n- **Flip point:** a few dozen services with request-scoped lifetimes, or a team already on NestJS."},{"q":"How would you order cache, retries, circuit breaker and fallback around an LLM client, and why?","a":"- **Cache outermost,** because a hit should cost neither a retry nor a breaker slot.\n- **Fallback next, breakers per provider inside it,** because each breaker's state should describe one\n  provider's health and an outage at one must not trip the other.\n- **SDK retries innermost,** so fallback happens only after a provider has genuinely failed."},{"q":"What exactly should and should not be injected?","a":"- **Inject** network clients, databases, caches, clocks, randomness and the file system: anything slow,\n  stateful, non-deterministic or credentialed.\n- **Do not inject** pure functions and value types such as a prompt formatter; it adds indirection with\n  no test or tenancy benefit.\n- **Use `AsyncLocalStorage` only for ambient context** like trace ids, never for real dependencies,\n  because hidden dependencies undo testability."}],"tradeoffs":["**Explicit wiring vs boilerplate.** Every dependency is visible and testable, but signatures and the composition root grow; past a few dozen services with request scopes, a container (NestJS, Awilix) becomes cheaper than hand-wiring.","**Decorator containers vs compile-time safety.** They give automatic resolution and scoped lifetimes, but rely on `emitDecoratorMetadata`, need tokens for anything that is not a class, and move \"missing dependency\" errors from the compiler to boot time. In small services and libraries the trade flips back to manual wiring.","**Narrow interfaces vs vendor features.** You can swap vendors and wrap caching or retry decorators around them, but every vendor feature you need (tool calls, streaming, prompt caching) must be surfaced through the interface, so over-abstracting early costs real functionality.","**Fakes vs realism.** Fakes in unit tests are fast and deterministic, but only prove the service calls the interface correctly; a thin layer of integration tests against the real SDK (recorded or sandboxed) is still needed or the fake drifts from reality."],"pitfalls":["**Module-level singletons created at import time** (`export const openai = new OpenAI()`). Symptom: tests that fail with \"missing API key\" or make real calls, and startup crashes whose stack trace points at an import line. Fix: construct in the root.","**Service locator in disguise.** Passing one giant `container` object and calling `container.get('llm')` inside business logic. Symptom: a signature no longer tells you what a function touches, and every test must build the whole container.","**Mixing scopes.** Injecting a request-scoped object (tenant config, trace span) into a singleton. Symptom: one tenant's model or API key leaking into another tenant's request under concurrency.","**Mocking by file path** (`vi.mock('../clients')`) instead of injecting. Symptom: tests that break when files move, and mocks that stay green after the real wiring changed.","**Leaky capability interfaces** that return vendor types (`OpenAI.ChatCompletion`, `Anthropic.Message`). Symptom: fakes must construct vendor objects, and adding a second provider still forces edits in business code. Fix: return your own small result type.","**Circular dependencies between services.** Some containers paper over them with lazy proxies (NestJS `forwardRef`); hand-wiring cannot construct them at all. Symptom: `undefined` at runtime or a NestJS circular-dependency boot error. Fix: usually split an interface."],"when":"- **Use when:** a service talks to an LLM, a database or a queue, which is nearly all AI backend code.\n  Ask \"would I want to replace this in a test or per tenant?\" If yes, inject it.\n- **Avoid when:** it is a pure function or value type; injecting it only adds indirection.\n- **Start simple:** a hand-written composition root and a `Deps` type. Move to a container when\n  request-scoped lifetimes and the number of services make the root hard to read, or when the team has\n  standardised on NestJS.\n- **Measure:** unit tests run with no network and no env vars, and adding a second LLM provider touches\n  only the root.\n- **Interviews:** explain why module singletons break tests and multi-tenancy, how interface erasure\n  affects containers, and where request scope lives.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes services against `LLM`, `Store` and `Clock` interfaces, wires concrete clients in one `createApp()`, and keeps unit tests free of network calls by passing fakes.","architect":"Owns the capability interfaces and the composition root as the seam where provider fallback, per-tenant routing, caching and credential isolation are decided without touching business logic.","fde":"Swaps the customer's private endpoint, proxy or on-prem model in at the root, and demonstrates the service against a fake LLM in the customer's CI before credentials are issued."},"check":{"q":"Your service imports a single OpenAI client at module scope. A customer now requires their\nrequests to go to an Azure OpenAI deployment with their own key, and the team wants automatic fallback\nto Anthropic when OpenAI is rate-limiting or failing (429s and 5xx after retries). How would you restructure, and where does each decision live?","a":"Introduce a narrow `LLM` interface covering only the operations the service uses, and make the\nservice take it as a constructor or factory argument. At the composition root build concrete adapters:\none wrapping the OpenAI SDK, one for Azure with the customer's endpoint and key, one for Anthropic.\nCompose them: a `FallbackLLM` that tries the primary and, on a 429 or 5xx that survives the SDK's own\nretries, calls the secondary, and a\nrouter that picks a per-tenant adapter from config at request time. The business logic never changes;\ntests inject a fake `LLM` and a fixed clock. Credentials stay in the root, tenant routing is\nrequest-scoped, and the fallback policy is a decorator around the interface, which keeps retry and\ncircuit-breaker logic out of the handler."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does TypeScript's type erasure matter for decorator-based DI containers?","options":["Because interfaces cannot be exported from modules that use decorators","Because an interface does not exist at runtime, so the container needs an explicit token or a class to know what to inject","Because erased types make constructor parameters optional","Because `emitDecoratorMetadata` only works with JavaScript files"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Containers resolve dependencies at runtime, and `emitDecoratorMetadata` can only record types that exist as values; an interface compiles to nothing, so a string or `Symbol` token (or a class) must stand in for it."},{"q":"Which dependency is usually NOT worth injecting?","options":["A function returning the current time","A Postgres connection pool","A pure function that formats a prompt template from its arguments","A client for a vector database"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Injection pays off for collaborators that are slow, stateful, non-deterministic or credentialed. A pure function is deterministic and free to call, so injecting it only adds indirection; clocks look harmless but are non-deterministic and matter in tests."},{"q":"A request-scoped tenant config object gets injected into a singleton LLM service. What is the likely symptom?","options":["Compile errors because scopes are checked by the TypeScript compiler","Slower startup because the singleton is now rebuilt per request","Under concurrent traffic, one tenant's model routing or API key is used for another tenant's request","The container refuses to start and logs a circular-dependency error"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A singleton is constructed once and shared, so it captures whichever request-scoped object it was first given; later requests from other tenants reuse that state, a correctness and security failure that only appears under concurrency. (NestJS avoids this by making the consumer request-scoped too, which trades the leak for per-request construction.)"}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","sources":["Official docs: PostgreSQL","Official docs: node-postgres (pg)","Official docs: Drizzle ORM","Official docs: Prisma"],"why":"- **Agent bookkeeping changes in groups.** Finishing one step means: insert the tool result, append the\n  assistant message, decrement the tenant's monthly token budget, enqueue the next step. If the process\n  dies between the second and third statement, the budget is never charged.\n- **Concurrent runs race.** Two parallel runs for one tenant both check \"1,200 tokens left\", both pass,\n  and both run `remaining = remaining - 1000`: the tenant ends at minus 800. If each instead writes back\n  a value computed in JavaScript (`remaining = 200`), one charge is silently lost. Without transactions\n  you write compensating code for every interleaving and still miss some.\n- **The Node pool trap is silent.** `pg`'s `Pool.query()` borrows a connection per call, so\n  `await pool.query('BEGIN'); await pool.query('INSERT ...'); await pool.query('COMMIT')` can use three\n  different connections. It passes single-user tests (the pool often returns the same connection) and\n  is non-atomic in production, with no error.\n- **Hand-run schema changes drift.** Running `ALTER TABLE` by hand on production means staging and\n  local disagree, nobody can reproduce last month's schema for a bug report, and a replica starting\n  mid-deploy sees columns its code does not know.\n- **Migrations make schema a versioned artifact.** It moves through environments with the code, and\n  each change's operational cost (locks, rewrites, index builds on million-row embedding tables) is\n  explicit before it hits production.","how":"### What goes wrong with `pool.query('BEGIN')`\n1. `BEGIN` runs on connection A, which returns to the pool still inside an open transaction.\n2. The INSERT autocommits on connection B.\n3. Connection A either sits idle in transaction or silently absorbs another request's statements until\n   a stray `COMMIT` lands on it.\n4. The idle-in-transaction connection holds its locks until it blocks a migration.\n\n### Transactions on one client\n- **Pin, run, finish, release.** Check a client out of the pool, run `BEGIN`, execute every statement\n  on that same client, then `COMMIT` or `ROLLBACK`, and release the client in `finally`, *because* a\n  leaked client is a leaked connection: the pool silently shrinks until requests hang on `connect()`.\n- **Libraries wrap this.** `pg` leaves it to you; Drizzle exposes `db.transaction(async (tx) => { ... })`;\n  Prisma has interactive `$transaction(async (tx) => { ... })`; Kysely and Knex have equivalents. The\n  callback form exists *because* it makes rollback-on-throw and release impossible to forget.\n- **Use `tx`, never the outer `db`, inside the callback.** The outer handle goes back to the pool and\n  escapes the transaction.\n- **No true nesting.** A nested `tx.transaction(...)` in Drizzle or Knex becomes a `SAVEPOINT`, so an\n  inner failure can roll back to the savepoint while the outer transaction continues.\n- **Destroy broken clients.** On a socket-level error, release with `client.release(err)` so the pool\n  destroys it instead of handing a broken connection to the next caller.\n\n### Isolation and contention\nPostgres defaults to READ COMMITTED: each statement sees data committed before it started. Enough for\nmost inserts, not for read-modify-write on a shared row such as a budget counter.\n\n| Tool | How it works | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` | locks the row, concurrent transactions queue behind you | counters, \"claim this job\" |\n| Atomic predicate update | condition and write in one statement, check row count | hot counters, no lock to reason about |\n| REPEATABLE READ | one snapshot per transaction; updating a row changed after the snapshot aborts with `40001` | preventing lost updates (not write skew) |\n| SERIALIZABLE | database detects anomalies and aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE `40001` | invariants spanning several rows |\n\n- **Why the predicate update is safe at READ COMMITTED.** `UPDATE budgets SET remaining = remaining - $1 WHERE tenant = $2 AND\n  remaining >= $1`: when two hit the same row, the second blocks on the row lock until the first\n  commits, then re-evaluates its `WHERE` against the newly committed row version.\n- **Worked through.** With 1,200 remaining and two charges of 1,000, the first leaves 200; the second\n  re-checks `200 >= 1000`, matches zero rows, and the caller sees \"exhausted\".\n- **The buggy version has no re-check.** A `SELECT`, a JavaScript check and an\n  `UPDATE ... SET remaining = $computed` is exactly the lost-update bug.\n- **Retry is the mechanism.** Retry the whole transaction on `40001` (and on `40P01`, deadlock) in a\n  small bounded loop, *because* the abort is how the database protects you, not a bug.\n\n### Keep transactions short\n- **Never hold one open across an LLM call.** A 20-second completion inside a transaction pins a\n  connection, holds locks and blocks the next migration. Do the model call, then open a transaction for\n  the writes.\n- **Server-side safety nets.** `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` kills a session sitting inside an\n  open transaction doing nothing (the leaked-client case); `statement_timeout` bounds any single query.\n- **Set them per role or connection,** so the application role can be strict while the migration role\n  is not.\n\n### Idempotency and the outbox\n- **Retries deliver the same write twice.** A unique constraint plus `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`\n  (or `DO UPDATE`) turns the second arrival into a no-op inside the same transaction.\n- **Side effects go through an outbox.** Insert an outbox row in the same transaction and let a relay\n  worker publish it, *because* \"commit, then enqueue\" can lose the enqueue and \"enqueue, then commit\" can\n  run the job before the data exists.\n\n### Migrations\nA migration tool (Drizzle Kit, Prisma Migrate, Knex, node-pg-migrate, or SQL-first tools such as dbmate\nand Flyway) keeps ordered files plus a table of applied ones. `migrate` applies the missing ones in\norder, usually inside a transaction; tools differ: some wrap each file, some the whole batch, and some\nneed an explicit opt-out per file.\n\n- **Forward-only, expand/contract.** Renaming a column: add the new column; deploy code that writes both\n  and reads new-or-old; backfill in batches; deploy code that uses only the new one; drop the old. Each\n  step is compatible with the versions before and after *because* both run at once during a rolling\n  deploy.\n- **Down migrations are rarely the rollback.** Once new code has written data in the new shape,\n  reversing the schema destroys it, so roll back the *code* (which the expand step keeps compatible) and\n  fix forward.\n- **Backfill in batches, outside the DDL.** `UPDATE ... WHERE id BETWEEN $1 AND $2` in chunks of a few\n  thousand rows, each its own short transaction, *because* one giant `UPDATE` holds row locks on every\n  touched row until commit, bursts WAL that replicas must replay, and leaves dead tuples vacuum cannot\n  reclaim while it is open.\n- **Indexes concurrently.** `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` does not block writes but cannot run inside a\n  transaction block, so the file must opt out of the tool's wrapping transaction. This matters for\n  pgvector HNSW or IVFFlat indexes on large embedding tables, which can take minutes to hours depending\n  on row count, dimensions and `maintenance_work_mem`.\n- **`lock_timeout`.** Put `SET lock_timeout = '5s'` at the top of a migration: a DDL statement waiting\n  for a lock queues *every* later query behind it, so failing fast and retrying off-peak is safer.\n\n### Which statements lock\n| Change | Cost | Safer path |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ADD COLUMN` with a constant (non-volatile) default | metadata-only since Postgres 11 | just do it |\n| `ALTER COLUMN ... SET NOT NULL` on an existing column | scans the table under a write-blocking lock | add `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID`, validate, then `SET NOT NULL` (since Postgres 12 it uses the validated check instead of rescanning) |\n| Adding a CHECK or foreign key | scans under a write-blocking lock | `ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID`, then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` in a later step under a weaker lock |\n| Index on a live table | plain `CREATE INDEX` blocks writes | `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, outside a transaction |\n\n### Running migrations\n- **A separate step,** a CI job or init container, not inside every replica's startup.\n- **Guarded by `pg_advisory_lock`,** so two runners cannot apply the same file concurrently. Many tools\n  take one automatically; check yours rather than assuming.\n- **Directly to Postgres, not through a transaction-mode pooler** such as PgBouncer: session-level\n  advisory locks and `SET lock_timeout` belong to a server connection the pooler may swap between\n  statements.","example":"### Transaction helper and idempotent step recorder\nA `withTransaction` helper on raw `pg` that pins one client, releases it on every path and retries\ntransient aborts, plus a step recorder that is idempotent (unique step id) and charges the budget with\nan atomic predicate update so no explicit row lock is needed.\n\nThe SQL file shows an expand step with a concurrent index, which must run outside the tool's\ntransaction.\n\n```ts\nimport { Pool, PoolClient } from \"pg\";\nconst pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL, max: 10 });\n\nexport async function withTransaction<T>(fn: (tx: PoolClient) => Promise<T>, retries = 3): Promise<T> {\n  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {\n    const client = await pool.connect();                // one connection for the whole transaction\n    let broken = false;\n    try {\n      await client.query(\"BEGIN\");                      // add \"ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE\" if you rely on it\n      const result = await fn(client);\n      await client.query(\"COMMIT\");\n      return result;\n    } catch (err: any) {\n      await client.query(\"ROLLBACK\").catch(() => { broken = true; });   // socket gone: do not reuse\n      const transient = err.code === \"40001\" || err.code === \"40P01\";   // serialization failure / deadlock\n      if (!transient || attempt >= retries) throw err;\n    } finally {\n      client.release(broken);                           // always release; `true` destroys a broken client\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport async function recordStep(tenant: string, runId: string, stepId: string, tokens: number, content: string) {\n  return withTransaction(async (tx) => {\n    const inserted = await tx.query(                    // idempotent: a redelivered job hits the unique key\n      \"INSERT INTO run_steps (run_id, step_id, content) VALUES ($1, $2, $3) ON CONFLICT (step_id) DO NOTHING\",\n      [runId, stepId, content]);\n    if (inserted.rowCount === 0) return \"duplicate\";\n    const charged = await tx.query(                     // atomic read-modify-write, no FOR UPDATE needed\n      \"UPDATE budgets SET remaining = remaining - $1 WHERE tenant = $2 AND remaining >= $1\", [tokens, tenant]);\n    if (charged.rowCount === 0) throw new Error(\"budget exhausted\");    // rolls the insert back too\n    await tx.query(\"INSERT INTO outbox (topic, payload) VALUES ('step.completed', $1)\", [{ runId, stepId }]);\n    return \"recorded\";\n  });\n}\n```\n\n```sql\n-- 0007_add_embedding.sql  (expand step; tool configured NOT to wrap this file in a transaction)\nSET lock_timeout = '5s';\nALTER TABLE documents ADD COLUMN embedding vector(1536);        -- nullable: metadata-only change\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY documents_embedding_hnsw ON documents USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: three ways to charge 1,000 tokens twice\nTwo runs for the same tenant charge 1,000 tokens each, at the same moment, with 1,200 remaining.\n\n| Approach | Run 1 | Run 2 | Final `remaining` | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `SELECT`, check in JS, `SET remaining = remaining - 1000` | reads 1,200, passes | reads 1,200, passes | -800 | overspent |\n| `SELECT`, check in JS, `SET remaining = $computed` | writes 200 | writes 200 | 200 | one charge lost |\n| Predicate update `... AND remaining >= $1` | 1 row, leaves 200 | waits, re-checks `200 >= 1000`, 0 rows | 200 | run 2 sees \"exhausted\" |\n\n### Production snippet: NOT NULL on a live table, in steps\nEach file is its own migration, so the brief strong lock of `ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID` is not held\nduring the validation scan. The backfill runs as a batched job between the first two files.\n\n```sql\n-- 0010_expand.sql: nullable column, metadata-only\nALTER TABLE messages ADD COLUMN tenant_id uuid;\n\n-- backfill job (not a migration): repeat per id range, each batch its own short transaction\nUPDATE messages m SET tenant_id = c.tenant_id FROM conversations c\n WHERE m.conversation_id = c.id AND m.id BETWEEN $1 AND $2 AND m.tenant_id IS NULL;\n\n-- 0011_add_check.sql: brief lock, no scan\nSET lock_timeout = '5s';\nALTER TABLE messages ADD CONSTRAINT messages_tenant_nn CHECK (tenant_id IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;\n\n-- 0012_validate.sql: scans under a lock that still allows writes\nALTER TABLE messages VALIDATE CONSTRAINT messages_tenant_nn;\n\n-- 0013_set_not_null.sql: Postgres 12+ uses the validated check instead of rescanning\nSET lock_timeout = '5s';\nALTER TABLE messages ALTER COLUMN tenant_id SET NOT NULL;\nALTER TABLE messages DROP CONSTRAINT messages_tenant_nn;\n```","patterns":["**Strict timeouts on the application role.** When any code path might leak a client, set `ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '30s'` and a `statement_timeout`, leaving the migration role looser (values illustrative). Monitor `pg_stat_activity` for sessions in state `idle in transaction` and their age.","**Pool saturation alerts.** node-postgres exposes `pool.totalCount`, `pool.idleCount` and `pool.waitingCount`; export them as gauges. A sustained non-zero waiting count with total at `max` means leaked clients or transactions held across slow calls.","**Outbox relay with `SKIP LOCKED`.** When writes must trigger jobs, a relay polls `SELECT ... FROM outbox ORDER BY id LIMIT 100 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, publishes, then deletes or marks the rows, so several relays never double-claim. Monitor the age of the oldest unpublished row.","**Fail-fast migrations, retried by the pipeline.** Every migration starts with `SET lock_timeout`, runs as a gated CI or init-container step before rollout, and the job retries off-peak on timeout. Monitor lock waits and migration duration per deploy.","**Post-build index check.** After any `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, query `SELECT indexrelid::regclass FROM pg_index WHERE NOT indisvalid` and drop or rebuild what it returns, because a failed concurrent build leaves an INVALID index that still slows writes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"pool.connect: pin one client\"] --> B[\"BEGIN\"]\n    B --> C[\"INSERT step ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING\"]\n    C -->|\"0 rows\"| CM[\"COMMIT\"]\n    C -->|\"1 row\"| D[\"UPDATE budget WHERE remaining >= tokens\"]\n    D -->|\"0 rows: throw\"| RB[\"ROLLBACK\"]\n    D -->|\"1 row\"| E[\"INSERT outbox row\"]\n    E --> CM\n    CM -.->|\"error\"| RB\n    RB -->|\"40001 or 40P01, retries left\"| A\n    RB -->|\"other error\"| X[\"Rethrow\"]\n    CM --> REL[\"client.release\"]","caption":"One pinned client carries the whole step: idempotent insert, atomic budget charge, outbox row. Any\nerror rolls everything back, and only serialization failures or deadlocks are retried."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you run a transaction with `pool.query` in node-postgres, and what do you do instead?","a":"- **Transactions belong to one connection,** and `Pool.query()` borrows a connection per call, so\n  `BEGIN`, the writes and `COMMIT` can land on different connections with no error.\n- **Instead,** check out one client with `pool.connect()`, run every statement on it, and release it in\n  `finally`, or use a library callback such as Drizzle's `db.transaction` and only touch `tx` inside."},{"q":"An agent step holds a transaction open while it calls the model, and migrations now time out. What is happening and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** a 20-second completion inside a transaction pins a connection and holds row locks; a\n  migration's DDL waits for those locks, and every later query queues behind the DDL.\n- **Fix:** call the model first, then open a short transaction for the writes.\n- **Safety nets:** `SET lock_timeout` in migrations, and `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` plus\n  `statement_timeout` on the application role."},{"q":"When would you choose SERIALIZABLE over `FOR UPDATE` or a predicate update?","a":"- **SERIALIZABLE** when an invariant spans several rows (write skew) and hand-placing locks is error\n  prone; it requires retrying on `40001`.\n- **Predicate update or `FOR UPDATE`** for a single hot row such as a budget counter, because under\n  contention SERIALIZABLE aborts often and retry loops cost throughput."},{"q":"How do you add a NOT NULL column to a 50-million-row table without downtime?","a":"- **Expand:** add the column nullable (metadata-only), deploy code that writes it, and backfill in\n  batches of a few thousand rows, each in its own transaction.\n- **Constrain cheaply:** add `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID`, then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` in a later\n  migration under a weaker lock, then `SET NOT NULL`, which since Postgres 12 skips the rescan.\n- **Guard each step** with `SET lock_timeout` so a blocked DDL fails fast instead of queueing traffic."},{"q":"Why put migrations in a separate pipeline step instead of running them at app startup?","a":"- **Several replicas would race** to apply the same file unless an advisory lock guards the run.\n- **Startup would block** on long index builds, and a failed migration would crash-loop every pod.\n- **The cost:** code and schema deploy separately, so each migration must work with the previous code\n  version, which expand/contract guarantees."}],"tradeoffs":["**Atomicity vs pool capacity.** Pinning a client per transaction holds a pooled connection for its lifetime; with `max: 10` and 20-second LLM calls inside transactions the pool becomes the bottleneck, so keep model calls outside.","**SERIALIZABLE vs retries.** It removes whole classes of anomalies without hand-placed locks, but aborts under contention and forces retry loops. At low contention it is nearly free; on hot rows `FOR UPDATE` or atomic predicate updates perform better.","**ORM convenience vs lock awareness.** Prisma, Drizzle and Kysely make transaction scoping hard to get wrong and generate migrations from a schema file, but generated migrations know nothing about locks, concurrency or backfills; large tables need hand-edited SQL.","**Expand/contract safety vs deploy count.** Safe under rolling deploys but takes several deploys instead of one. For a table of a few thousand rows on a single replica a direct change is fine; the discipline pays off once the table is large or replicas overlap.","**Separate migration step vs ordering.** It avoids races between replicas, but code and schema can then deploy out of order, so every migration must be compatible with the previous code version."],"pitfalls":["**Using `pool.query('BEGIN')`.** Statements land on different connections. Symptom: non-atomic data with no error, plus connections stuck `idle in transaction` in `pg_stat_activity`.","**Outer `db` instead of `tx` inside a Drizzle or Prisma transaction callback.** Symptom: a write that persists although the transaction rolled back, or a deadlock when the outer query waits on a lock the transaction holds.","**Forgetting `client.release()` on an error path.** Symptom: the service hangs on `pool.connect()` after exactly `max` failures.","**`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` inside the tool's transaction.** Symptom: `cannot run inside a transaction block`. A failed concurrent build leaves an INVALID index that must be dropped by hand.","**NOT NULL column with an inline backfill in one statement on a large table.** Symptom: a deploy that holds an exclusive lock for minutes while every API request queues behind it.","**Migrations through PgBouncer in transaction mode.** Symptom: `lock_timeout` apparently ignored or an advisory lock that never seems held, because consecutive statements ran on different server connections.","**Migrations on container start with several replicas and no advisory lock.** Symptom: duplicate-apply errors or two runners racing on the same `ALTER`."],"when":"- **Use a transaction when:** two or more writes must succeed together or a read must be consistent with\n  a following write: run bookkeeping, budget charges, message plus embedding inserts, outbox rows.\n- **Avoid:** wrapping LLM or HTTP calls in one.\n- **Pick the contention tool:** atomic predicate updates and `FOR UPDATE` for hot counters; SERIALIZABLE\n  for invariants that span several rows.\n- **Schema:** every change goes through a migration file reviewed for its lock footprint; tables beyond a\n  few hundred thousand rows get expand/contract and concurrent index builds.\n- **Measure:** idle-in-transaction sessions in `pg_stat_activity`, transaction duration and lock waits.\n- **Interviews:** expect the pool-per-statement trap, the retry on `40001`, and how to add a NOT NULL\n  column to a live table.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `withTransaction` helper, keeps model calls outside it, handles `40001` retries, and reviews each generated migration for lock and backfill behaviour before merging.","architect":"Sets the policy: which invariants are transactional, where idempotency keys and the outbox live, the default isolation level, and that migrations run as a gated pipeline step with expand/contract.","fde":"Applies migrations inside a customer's VPC where a DBA controls maintenance windows, and explains why an embedding index must be built concurrently rather than during a deploy."},"check":{"q":"A worker charges a tenant's token budget and records an agent step. Under load, budgets\noccasionally go negative and some steps appear twice. Diagnose the two bugs and fix them without\nchanging the isolation level.","a":"Negative budgets mean a check-then-act race: the code reads `remaining`, checks it in JavaScript,\nthen decrements, so two transactions both pass the check against the same value and both subtract.\n(Writing back a value computed in JavaScript would instead lose a charge, which is the same race with a\ndifferent symptom.) Replace it with a single predicate\nupdate, `UPDATE budgets SET remaining = remaining - $1 WHERE tenant = $2 AND remaining >= $1`, and treat\na zero row count as \"exhausted\"; or lock with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` first. Duplicate steps mean\nat-least-once delivery from the queue with no idempotency: add a unique constraint on the step id, use\n`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, and only charge when the insert actually inserted a row. Both must\nrun on one pinned client inside one transaction so a failure after the charge rolls the charge back.\nFinally confirm the code is not calling `pool.query('BEGIN')`, which would scatter the statements across\nconnections."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `await pool.query('BEGIN'); await pool.query('INSERT ...'); await pool.query('COMMIT')` wrong with node-postgres?","options":["`BEGIN` must be uppercase and parameterised to be recognised","Each `pool.query` may use a different connection, so the INSERT can run outside the transaction","The pool rejects `COMMIT` unless autocommit is disabled","Transactions require the `pg-transaction` extension"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transactions are a property of a single connection. The pool borrows a connection per call, so the INSERT can autocommit on another connection while the first sits idle in transaction, and no error is raised."},{"q":"You need an HNSW index on a 5-million-row embeddings table in production. Which approach avoids blocking writes?","options":["`CREATE INDEX` inside the migration's transaction so it can roll back","Drop and recreate the table with the index defined inline","`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` in a migration configured to run outside a transaction block","Add the index at application startup with a retry loop"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concurrent index builds allow reads and writes during the build but cannot run inside a transaction block, so the migration tool's wrapping transaction must be disabled for that file; a failed build leaves an invalid index to drop."},{"q":"Your transaction retry helper catches SQLSTATE 40001. When does that error actually occur?","options":["Whenever a unique constraint is violated","When the pool has no free connections","When Postgres aborts a transaction under REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE because a concurrent commit made it unserializable","When a statement exceeds `statement_timeout`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`40001` is `serialization_failure`: the database detects that the transaction's reads are inconsistent with a concurrent commit and aborts it, expecting the application to retry; unique violations are `23505` and timeouts are `57014`."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/redis","sources":["Official docs: Redis","Official docs: ioredis","Official docs: node-redis"],"why":"- **In-process state breaks at two replicas.** Every `Map` becomes a bug: the rate limiter counts half\n  the traffic, the response cache misses whenever the balancer picks the other replica, the \"run already\n  in progress\" flag is wrong half the time, and a reconnecting streaming client lands on a replica that\n  knows nothing about its stream.\n- **Postgres is the wrong tool for it.** A token-bucket check on every request, with disk-backed\n  transactions and MVCC overhead, costs milliseconds and connections you want for real queries, and\n  TTL-based expiry needs a cron job.\n- **Redis fills the gap.** It is a network-attached data-structure library: `INCR`, `SET ... NX PX`,\n  `ZADD` and `XADD` are single atomic operations measured in microseconds server-side, keys expire on\n  their own, and memory is the only limit.\n- **Single-threaded execution is the design choice.** No locks, no partial updates, and a Lua script or\n  `MULTI` block runs as one unit *because* nothing else can interleave.\n- **Its cost is equally clear.** One slow command (a `KEYS *`, a huge `SMEMBERS`, a long script) stalls\n  every client, and data that must survive a crash needs persistence configured deliberately.\n- **Scope of this lesson.** The fundamentals live in the backend module; this covers Node usage and what\n  goes wrong.","how":"### Why one thread is enough\n- **Data is in RAM** and almost every command is O(1) or O(log n).\n- **An event loop multiplexes** thousands of client sockets, so the server spends its time executing\n  commands rather than waiting.\n- **Redis 6 added optional I/O threads** for reading and writing sockets, but command execution itself\n  stays serial.\n\n### Data structures, picked by access pattern\n| Structure | Commands | AI-service use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Strings | `SET key value EX ttl`, `SET ... NX` | cached completions, feature flags, idempotency markers |\n| Hashes | `HSET` field by field | one conversation's metadata or a session, expired as a whole |\n| Lists | `LPUSH` plus `LTRIM` | simple queues, recent-messages windows |\n| Sets, sorted sets | `ZADD`, `ZREMRANGEBYSCORE`, `ZCARD` | membership, ranking, sliding-window rate limits (timestamps as scores) |\n| Streams | `XADD`, consumer groups | relaying token streams between replicas, BullMQ's event log |\n| Pub/Sub | publish, subscribe | fire-and-forget broadcast of streamed tokens to the replica holding the SSE connection |\n\n- **Per-field TTLs are new.** Redis 7.4 added `HEXPIRE`; older servers and many managed offerings only\n  expire whole keys.\n- **Pub/Sub stores nothing.** An offline subscriber misses messages; use Streams when that matters.\n\n### Atomicity: single thread, MULTI, Lua\n- **Each command is atomic.** Compound logic (\"increment, and if first hit set the expiry\") needs more.\n- **`MULTI`/`EXEC`** queues commands and executes them without interleaving, but has no server-side\n  branching.\n- **Lua via `EVAL`/`EVALSHA`** runs atomically and can branch.\n- **Rate limiters, token buckets and lock release are Lua scripts** (\"delete only if the value is still\n  my token\") *because* a get-then-set from the client is a race between two round trips.\n\n### Persistence and eviction\n| Role | Persistence | Eviction | Because |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cache | none, or RDB snapshots (periodic, cheap, can lose the last minutes) | `maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru` | everything is rebuildable |\n| Queue or lock store | AOF with `appendfsync everysec` (loses at most about a second) | `noeviction` | evicting a job key under memory pressure silently loses work |\n\n- **Decide per instance,** and do not share one instance between the two roles.\n\n### From Node\n- **Two mainstream clients.** `ioredis` (long the de-facto standard and what BullMQ uses) and `redis`\n  (node-redis, the official client). Both are promise-based and support Lua scripts, Cluster and\n  Sentinel.\n- **Pipelining.** node-redis batches un-awaited commands automatically; ioredis does it with\n  `enableAutoPipelining` or an explicit `pipeline()`.\n- **Dedicated connections for subscribers and blocking commands.** A connection in subscriber mode or\n  running `BLPOP` / `XREAD BLOCK` cannot issue other commands. BullMQ workers open a separate blocking\n  connection for the same reason and require `maxRetriesPerRequest: null` on it.\n- **Configure failure behaviour explicitly.** ioredis's `maxRetriesPerRequest` and `enableOfflineQueue`\n  decide whether a command waits for reconnection or fails fast. On a request path you usually want\n  fail-fast *because* a hung Redis should degrade the cache, not take down the API.\n- **Namespace keys per tenant and purpose** (`t:{tenantId}:cache:{hash}`). Hash tags in braces keep\n  related keys on one Cluster slot. That matters for atomicity: in Redis Cluster a `MULTI` block or Lua\n  script may touch only one slot and fails with `CROSSSLOT` otherwise, which is why BullMQ asks you to\n  wrap a queue prefix in braces on Cluster.\n- **Keep values small.** Never block the event loop building huge payloads; values beyond a few hundred\n  kilobytes belong in object storage with a Redis pointer.\n\n### Typical AI-service uses\n- **Completion cache.** Key = SHA-256 of (model, system prompt, messages, parameters), serialised with\n  stable key order. Cache only requests where one canonical answer is acceptable (temperature 0 or\n  explicitly marked cacheable), and store usage alongside so cost dashboards stay honest.\n- **Temperature 0 is not deterministic.** On hosted APIs it is not bit-for-bit reproducible, so the cache\n  *chooses* an answer rather than reproducing one.\n- **Stampede protection.** When a hot entry expires, or after a deploy with a cold cache, many identical\n  requests miss at once and each calls the model: one expiry can cost N paid completions.\n- **Single-flight fixes it.** The first miss takes `SET llm:fill:{hash} 1 NX PX 30000` and calls the\n  model; the others poll the cache key briefly (or wait on a pub/sub message) before falling back to\n  their own call. Add random TTL jitter so entries written together do not expire together. Within one\n  replica, a `Map` of in-flight promises keyed by hash deduplicates for free.\n- **Provider rate limit / semaphore.** A token bucket in Lua keyed by provider and model, so N replicas\n  together stay under the vendor's requests-per-minute and tokens-per-minute limits.\n- **Run state and locks.** `SET run:{id}:lock token NX PX 30000` ensures one worker per run, released by\n  a check-and-delete script; refresh the TTL while working. A TTL lock can still expire under a paused\n  process, so writes that must never happen twice also need a fencing token or a unique constraint\n  downstream.\n- **Short-term memory.** Last-N turns for a conversation with a TTL; long-term memory lives in Postgres\n  or a vector store.","example":"### Completion cache and fixed-window rate limiter\nA completion cache and a fixed-window rate limiter using ioredis. The limiter is a Lua script so the\nincrement and the expiry are set atomically; the cache key hashes the fields that affect the output in a\nfixed order.\n\nEvery Redis failure on the hot path degrades rather than failing the request: the limiter fails open,\nthe cache falls through to the provider. Fixed windows allow up to twice the limit across a window\nboundary; use a sliding window or token bucket when that matters.\n\n```ts\nimport Redis from \"ioredis\";\nimport { createHash } from \"node:crypto\";\n\nconst redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL!, { maxRetriesPerRequest: 1, enableOfflineQueue: false });\nredis.on(\"error\", (e) => console.error(\"redis\", e.message));   // an unhandled 'error' event would crash the process\n\n// Atomic fixed-window limiter: the first hit in a window sets the TTL.\nredis.defineCommand(\"hitWindow\", {\n  numberOfKeys: 1,\n  lua: `local n = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])\n        if n == 1 then redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end\n        return n`,\n});\nexport async function allow(tenant: string, limit = 60, windowMs = 60_000): Promise<boolean> {\n  const bucket = Math.floor(Date.now() / windowMs);\n  try {\n    const n: number = await (redis as any).hitWindow(`rl:${tenant}:${bucket}`, windowMs);\n    return n <= limit;\n  } catch { return true; }                                          // Redis down: fail open (a policy choice)\n}\n\n// Cache-aside for deterministic completions.\nexport async function cachedComplete(req: { model: string; system: string; messages: unknown[]; temperature: number; maxTokens: number },\n                                     call: () => Promise<string>, ttlSec = 3600): Promise<string> {\n  if (req.temperature !== 0) return call();                      // sampled output: do not cache\n  const material = JSON.stringify([req.model, req.system, req.messages, req.temperature, req.maxTokens]);  // fixed field order\n  const key = \"llm:\" + createHash(\"sha256\").update(material).digest(\"hex\");\n  try {\n    const hit = await redis.get(key);\n    if (hit !== null) return hit;\n  } catch { /* Redis down: fall through to the provider */ }\n  const out = await call();\n  redis.set(key, out, \"EX\", ttlSec).catch(() => {});             // best effort, never awaited on the hot path\n  return out;\n}\n```\n\n### A run lock with safe release and refresh\nOne worker per agent run. The token proves ownership, so a worker whose lock expired during a long LLM\ncall cannot delete or extend the lock another worker now holds.\n\n```ts\nimport Redis from \"ioredis\";\nimport { randomUUID } from \"node:crypto\";\n\nconst redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL!);\nconst RELEASE = `if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1]) else return 0 end`;\nconst REFRESH = `if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[2]) else return 0 end`;\n\nexport async function withRunLock<T>(runId: string, work: () => Promise<T>, ttlMs = 30_000): Promise<T | \"busy\"> {\n  const key = `run:${runId}:lock`;\n  const token = randomUUID();\n  const ok = await redis.set(key, token, \"PX\", ttlMs, \"NX\");       // \"OK\" or null\n  if (ok !== \"OK\") return \"busy\";\n  const timer = setInterval(() => { redis.eval(REFRESH, 1, key, token, ttlMs).catch(() => {}); }, ttlMs / 3);\n  try {\n    return await work();\n  } finally {\n    clearInterval(timer);\n    await redis.eval(RELEASE, 1, key, token);                      // never a plain DEL\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing and a stampede\nIllustrative numbers for a completion cache, using the entry size from the trade-offs below.\n\n| Question | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Memory for a million cached prompts at 20 KB each | 1,000,000 × 20 KB | ~20 GB of RAM, before overhead |\n| Same traffic with a 1-hour TTL, if only 100,000 distinct prompts arrive per hour | 100,000 × 20 KB | ~2 GB resident |\n| A popular entry expires while 200 identical requests are in flight | no single-flight: each misses | 200 paid completions |\n| Same expiry with the `NX` fill key | 1 caller fills, 199 poll | 1 paid completion |","patterns":["**Separate instances by role.** Run the cache with `maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru` and no persistence, and the BullMQ / lock instance with `noeviction` and `appendonly yes` plus `appendfsync everysec`. Monitor `evicted_keys` from `INFO stats`: it must stay zero on the queue instance.","**Fail-fast clients on request paths.** Set `maxRetriesPerRequest: 1`, `enableOfflineQueue: false` and ioredis's `commandTimeout`, wrap calls in `try/catch`, and count fall-throughs. Monitor the fall-through rate and provider spend, which jumps when the cache is down.","**Watch the slow log.** Set `slowlog-log-slower-than` (microseconds) and read `SLOWLOG GET` from a dashboard job, because one slow command stalls every client. Alert on new entries and on p99 command latency.","**Find big keys without blocking.** Use `redis-cli --bigkeys` (it walks the keyspace with `SCAN`) and `MEMORY USAGE <key>` instead of `KEYS`. Monitor `used_memory` against `maxmemory`.","**Jittered TTLs plus single-flight on expensive entries.** Set the TTL to the base plus a random fraction (for example up to 10%) and guard fills with an `NX` key. Monitor provider calls per minute right after deploys and cache flushes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Incoming request\"] --> L{\"Lua limiter: under limit?\"}\n    L -->|\"no\"| E[\"Reject with 429\"]\n    L -->|\"yes or Redis down\"| C{\"Cache hit?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Return cached text\"]\n    C -->|\"no or Redis down\"| F{\"Won fill key NX PX?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes\"| M[\"Call the model\"]\n    M --> S[\"SET key EX ttl plus jitter\"]\n    S --> H\n    F -->|\"no\"| P[\"Poll cache briefly\"]\n    P -->|\"filled\"| H\n    P -->|\"timed out\"| M","caption":"One request through the limiter and a single-flight completion cache. Only the request that wins the\n`NX` fill key calls the model; every Redis failure falls through rather than failing the request."},"interview":[{"q":"Redis is single-threaded. Why is it still fast, and what does that design cost you?","a":"- **Fast because** data is in RAM, nearly every command is O(1) or O(log n), and one event loop\n  multiplexes thousands of sockets; Redis 6 I/O threads help with socket work only.\n- **The benefit:** each command, `MULTI` block and Lua script is atomic with no locks.\n- **The cost:** one slow command such as `KEYS *` or a long script stalls every client."},{"q":"A cache hit rate looks healthy, yet provider spend spikes after every deploy. What is going on?","a":"- **A stampede:** a cold or flushed cache makes many identical requests miss at once, and each pays for\n  its own completion.\n- **Fix:** single-flight with `SET ... NX PX` on a fill key so one caller fills while others poll, an\n  in-process `Map` of in-flight promises per replica, and jittered TTLs.\n- **Verify** by graphing provider calls per minute around deploys."},{"q":"Would you put BullMQ on the same Redis instance as your completion cache?","a":"- **No:** the cache wants `allkeys-lru` and no persistence; the queue needs `noeviction` and AOF,\n  because an evicted job key is silently lost work.\n- **Also,** a cache scan or big value can stall the queue's commands on the shared thread.\n- **When it is fine:** tiny deployments that run `noeviction` everywhere and accept that the cache can\n  fill memory and start erroring writes."},{"q":"Why do Lua scripts and `MULTI` fail with `CROSSSLOT` on Redis Cluster, and how do you design keys for it?","a":"- **Cluster shards keys across hash slots,** and an atomic block or script may only touch keys in one\n  slot.\n- **Hash tags** in braces, such as `t:{tenantId}:...`, make Redis hash only the braced part, so a\n  tenant's related keys share a slot.\n- **That is why BullMQ asks** for a braced queue prefix on Cluster."},{"q":"How do you make a Redis lock safe for an agent run that may take minutes?","a":"- **Acquire** with `SET key token NX PX ttl` using a random token, and refresh the TTL periodically\n  with a script that checks the token.\n- **Release** with a check-and-delete Lua script, never a plain `DEL`, so an expired holder cannot\n  delete someone else's lock.\n- **Still add a fencing token or unique constraint** downstream, because a paused process can outlive\n  its TTL."}],"tradeoffs":["**Speed and expiry vs RAM.** Microsecond atomic operations and built-in expiry, but everything lives in RAM; a completion cache holding 20 KB responses for a million prompts is 20 GB, so entry size and TTL must be budgeted, not assumed.","**Free atomicity vs head-of-line stalls.** Single-threaded execution gives atomicity for free, but one slow command stalls all clients; once anyone needs scans over large keyspaces or long Lua loops, split the instance by role.","**Pub/Sub simplicity vs durability.** The simplest way to relay streamed tokens across replicas, but it drops messages for absent subscribers; when a reconnecting client must resume, Streams with consumer groups cost more code and keep the data.","**Cache config vs queue config.** `allkeys-lru` with no persistence is cheap and safe to lose for a cache; under a queue or lock store it silently discards work, so durability costs money (AOF, replicas) and a separate instance.","**Managed vs latency.** Managed Redis (or Valkey, the Linux Foundation fork) removes operations work but adds network hops; another availability zone turns sub-millisecond calls into milliseconds on every request."],"pitfalls":["**One connection for pub/sub or blocking commands and normal commands.** Symptom: an error that only subscriber commands may be used, or requests that hang behind a `BLPOP`.","**No `error` listener on the client.** Symptom: the whole Node process exits on the first network blip with an unhandled `'error'` event.","**Caching non-deterministic completions, or hashing an object with unstable key order.** Symptom: stale or wrong answers served as hits, or a hit rate far below what the traffic suggests.","**No stampede protection on an expensive cache.** Symptom: a burst of identical provider calls (and a token-cost spike) when a popular entry expires or after a cache flush, though steady-state hit rate looks healthy.","**`KEYS pattern` in production to find a tenant's keys.** Symptom: latency spikes on every client while it runs. Use `SCAN` with a cursor or maintain an index set.","**Lock release with a plain `DEL`.** Symptom: worker A, whose lock expired during a long LLM call, deletes the lock worker B now holds, so two workers process one run.","**BullMQ or locks on an `allkeys-lru` instance.** Symptom: jobs vanish under memory pressure with no error anywhere."],"when":"- **Use when:** state must be shared across replicas, must be fast, and is either rebuildable (cache) or\n  short-lived (rate windows, locks, run state, recent turns).\n- **Avoid when:** you cannot afford to lose it, unless you have configured AOF, replicas and\n  `noeviction` and understand what each costs.\n- **Not the primary store** for conversations or evaluation results; that is Postgres.\n- **Measure:** hit rate, p99 command latency, memory against `maxmemory`, evicted keys and connected\n  clients per replica.\n- **Interviews:** explain why rate limiting needs a Lua script, why pub/sub loses messages, and why a\n  cache and a queue should not share an instance.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the cache-aside and token-bucket helpers, keeps separate connections for subscribers, and makes every Redis call on the request path fail open or fail fast.","architect":"Decides which state is allowed in Redis, the persistence and eviction policy per instance, key namespacing for tenants, and whether Streams or a queue carry cross-replica events.","fde":"Works with whatever managed Redis or Valkey the customer runs, checks eviction policy and version before wiring BullMQ to it, and sizes memory from entry size and TTL."},"check":{"q":"Your agent API runs four replicas. A streaming response is produced by the replica that called\nthe model, but the client's SSE connection may be on another replica, and clients sometimes reconnect\nmid-stream. Design the Redis part and explain the trade-offs.","a":"The producing replica appends each token chunk to a Redis Stream keyed by run id with a TTL, and\nthe replica holding the SSE connection reads it with `XREAD BLOCK` from the last id the client\nacknowledged, which makes reconnects resumable because the chunks are stored, not merely broadcast.\nPub/Sub would be simpler and lower latency, but a client reconnecting would miss everything published\nwhile it was away. Each blocking reader needs its own connection, and the stream must be trimmed or\nexpired so memory does not grow with every run. The instance should run `noeviction` so chunks are not\ndropped under pressure, and the final entry should be a sentinel so the consumer knows the stream is\ncomplete rather than stalled."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a rate limiter implemented as a Lua script instead of `GET` then `SET` from Node?","options":["Lua runs faster than JavaScript on the Redis server","The read and the write from Node are separate round trips, so two replicas can both read the same count and both allow a request past the limit","Redis rejects `SET` from clients that have recently called `GET`","Lua scripts are the only way to set a TTL on a key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Redis executes each command atomically, but a client-side get-then-set is two commands with a gap; a script (or `MULTI`) runs as one unit on the single-threaded server, which closes the race."},{"q":"BullMQ jobs are disappearing from a shared Redis instance under load, with no errors logged. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The jobs exceeded Redis's maximum value size","Workers are running with too much concurrency","The instance uses an `allkeys-lru` eviction policy and job keys are evicted when memory fills","Pub/Sub messages were dropped because no subscriber was connected"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cache-style eviction policy treats job state like any other key and silently removes it at `maxmemory`; queue and lock workloads need `noeviction` and ideally their own instance."},{"q":"Which client setup is correct for a Node service that both caches completions and subscribes to a channel?","options":["One connection for everything, because ioredis multiplexes commands","Two connections: one for normal commands and a dedicated one in subscriber mode","A new connection per request to avoid head-of-line blocking","A connection pool of at least one per CPU core"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A connection that has issued `SUBSCRIBE` can only run subscriber commands, so `GET`/`SET` on it fail; a separate subscriber connection is required, while normal commands pipeline fine on one shared connection."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","sources":["Official docs: BullMQ","Official docs: Redis"],"why":"- **A request is the wrong unit for long work.** An agent run takes two minutes, calls the model eight\n  times and calls three tools. Inside an HTTP handler, the client holds a connection for two minutes and\n  a load balancer idle timeout (60 s is a common default) can kill it first.\n- **Restarts and bursts hurt.** A deploy that restarts the process loses the run with no record, and a\n  burst of 200 runs starts 200 concurrent model calls that hit the provider's rate limit together. The\n  same shape appears for a 10,000-document embedding batch, nightly evals, document ingestion, webhook\n  fan-out and scheduled re-indexing.\n- **In-process queues fail on every axis.** `setImmediate(() => runAgent(...))` with a `Map` of\n  in-flight runs dies with the process, cannot be shared across replicas, and has no retry policy and no\n  visibility.\n- **Postgres-as-queue is legitimate but DIY.** `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` keeps everything in\n  one transactional store, but you write the polling, locking, retry, backoff, delay and dead-letter\n  machinery yourself.\n- **BullMQ packages that machinery on Redis.** Redis fits *because* the hot path (claim a job, renew its\n  lock, move it between states) is a handful of atomic operations on lists, sorted sets and hashes,\n  done in microseconds as Lua scripts so a claim is never half-done. The trade: snapshot-only\n  persistence or an LRU eviction policy means lost jobs.","how":"### Objects\n| Object | Role | Key detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| Queue | producer handle | `queue.add(name, data, opts)` writes a job hash and pushes its id onto the waiting list (or a delayed or prioritised sorted set) |\n| Worker | consumer | takes a processor `(job) => Promise<result>` and a `concurrency`; each slot atomically moves a job from waiting to active. Many Workers on many replicas share one queue, which is how you scale |\n| Job | the unit | `id`, `name`, `data` (JSON), `opts`, `attemptsMade`, `progress`, `returnvalue`, timestamps |\n| QueueEvents | listener | Redis-streams based `completed`, `failed`, `progress`, so an API replica can stream progress without being the worker |\n| FlowProducer | parent and children | the parent runs only after all children complete: \"embed 500 chunks, then write the document summary\" |\n\n### Custom job ids and dedup\n- **A custom `jobId` deduplicates.** Adding a job whose id already exists is a no-op while the earlier\n  job is still stored: the simplest exactly-once-enqueue tool you have.\n- **Format rules.** Custom ids must not be plain integers or contain `:` (BullMQ uses it as its key\n  separator and throws on `add`), so use `embed-<docId>`, not `embed:<docId>`.\n- **The dedup window is the job's lifetime in Redis.** Once `removeOnComplete` deletes the job, the same\n  id can be added again; while a completed job is retained, a deliberate re-run with that id is silently\n  ignored.\n- **Explicit windows.** BullMQ 5 also offers a `deduplication` option with its own id and TTL.\n\n### Job lifecycle\nEach state is a Redis structure, and a job is in exactly one:\n\n| State | Structure | Meaning when it grows |\n|---|---|---|\n| `waiting` | list | too few workers or a limiter that is too tight |\n| `prioritized`, `delayed` | sorted sets by priority or due time | scheduled work piling up |\n| `active` | list plus a per-job lock key | non-empty with nobody working means stalls |\n| `waiting-children` | parent blocked on its flow | children are slow or failing |\n| `completed`, `failed` | terminal sets | a growing `failed` means a bug or a dependency that is down |\n\n- **Every transition is one Lua script** (claim, complete, fail and reschedule, promote a delayed job)\n  *because* it touches several keys, and a crash between \"remove from waiting\" and \"add to active\"\n  would otherwise lose or duplicate the job.\n\n### Retries, backoff, delays\n- **Exponential backoff.** `opts.attempts` with `opts.backoff: { type: 'exponential', delay: 1000 }`\n  re-schedules a failed job after 1 s, 2 s, 4 s and so on until `attempts` is exhausted, then leaves it\n  in the failed set for inspection or manual retry.\n- **Delayed jobs** sit in a sorted set keyed by timestamp and are promoted when due, *because* polling a\n  list for \"is it time yet\" would waste Redis round trips.\n- **`priority`** orders waiting jobs.\n- **Retention.** `removeOnComplete` and `removeOnFail` with a count or an age keep Redis from filling\n  with history.\n- **Recurring work** (nightly evals, hourly re-index) uses a job scheduler:\n  `queue.upsertJobScheduler(id, { pattern: cron } or { every: ms }, template)` in recent BullMQ 5\n  releases, which supersedes the older repeatable-job API. It keeps one upcoming delayed job per\n  schedule, so several producer replicas do not multiply the runs.\n\n### Locks and stalled jobs\n1. **Claim:** the worker sets a lock with `lockDuration` and renews it on an interval while the processor\n   runs.\n2. **Failure:** the process dies, or the event loop is blocked long enough that renewal stops; the lock\n   expires.\n3. **Recovery:** a stall checker sees an active job with no lock and moves it back to waiting, up to\n   `maxStalledCount` times before marking it failed.\n\n- **This is the durability mechanism,** and also why a CPU-bound processor that blocks the event loop can\n  get a job processed twice: the renewal timer never fires.\n- **Sandboxed processors exist for that failure mode.** Pass a file path instead of a function; the job\n  runs in a child process or a worker thread.\n\n### Rate limiting for LLM providers\n- **Fleet-wide cap.** `Worker` takes `limiter: { max, duration }` to cap jobs started per window across\n  all workers of a queue.\n- **Reacting to 429s.** A processor calls `await queue.rateLimit(ms)` (in BullMQ 5 this replaces the\n  deprecated `worker.rateLimit`) and throws `Worker.RateLimitError()`; the job returns to the queue\n  without consuming an attempt while the queue pauses for that window.\n- **Per-tenant limits.** Per-group limits are a BullMQ Pro feature; the open-source approach is a queue\n  per tenant tier, or a Redis token bucket checked inside the processor.\n\n### Delivery semantics, and what BullMQ is not\n- **At-least-once everywhere.** A job can run twice after a stall, after a crash between finishing the\n  work and moving the job to completed, or after a manual retry.\n- **So processors must be idempotent,** usually by keying side effects on `job.id` (a Postgres unique\n  constraint, an idempotency key on a downstream API).\n- **Not a workflow engine.** It does not checkpoint the middle of a processor. Split a ten-step agent run\n  into one job per step with state in Postgres, or use a durable-execution engine (Temporal, Inngest,\n  Restate and similar) for replayable history, long timers and human-in-the-loop waits.","example":"### Embedding pipeline with dedup, limiter and checkpoints\nThe API enqueues one job per document with a deterministic id (a double-submit is harmless), a worker\nwith bounded concurrency and a provider rate limit processes it, and the processor is idempotent\nbecause the vector write keys on document id and chunk index.\n\nEach BullMQ object gets its own ioredis connection from a factory here; BullMQ also duplicates a\nWorker's connection internally for its blocking commands, and refuses a Worker connection whose\n`maxRetriesPerRequest` is not `null`.\n\n```ts\nimport IORedis from \"ioredis\";\nimport { Queue, Worker, QueueEvents } from \"bullmq\";\nconst conn = () => new IORedis(process.env.REDIS_URL!, { maxRetriesPerRequest: null });  // required for Workers\n\nexport const embedQueue = new Queue(\"embed\", { connection: conn(),\n  defaultJobOptions: { attempts: 5, backoff: { type: \"exponential\", delay: 2_000 },\n                       removeOnComplete: { count: 1_000 }, removeOnFail: { age: 7 * 24 * 3600 } } });\n\nexport async function enqueueEmbedding(docId: string, tenant: string) {\n  await embedQueue.add(\"embed-doc\", { docId, tenant }, { jobId: `embed-${docId}` });   // dedup by id (no ':' allowed)\n}\n\n// worker.ts — its own process; several replicas share the queue\nconst worker = new Worker(\"embed\", async (job) => {\n  const { docId } = job.data as { docId: string; tenant: string };\n  const chunks = await loadChunks(docId);\n  for (let i = 0; i < chunks.length; i++) {\n    if (await embeddingExists(docId, i)) continue;                   // resume, do not redo, after a retry\n    try {\n      const vec = await embed(chunks[i].text);                       // provider call\n      await upsertEmbedding(docId, i, vec);                           // idempotent: (docId, i) is unique\n    } catch (e: any) {\n      if (e.status === 429) { await embedQueue.rateLimit(30_000); throw Worker.RateLimitError(); }\n      throw e;                                                       // anything else: retry with backoff\n    }\n    await job.updateProgress(Math.round(((i + 1) / chunks.length) * 100));\n  }\n  return { chunks: chunks.length };\n}, { connection: conn(), concurrency: 4, limiter: { max: 50, duration: 60_000 } });\n\nworker.on(\"failed\", (job, err) => console.error(\"embed failed\", job?.id, job?.attemptsMade, err.message));\n\n// api.ts — stream progress to a client without being the worker\nconst events = new QueueEvents(\"embed\", { connection: conn() });\nevents.on(\"progress\", ({ jobId, data }) => notifyClient(jobId, data));\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what the settings above mean in time\nUsing the queue defaults above (`attempts: 5`, exponential backoff with a 2-second base delay) and the\nworker limiter of 50 jobs per 60 seconds.\n\n| Question | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Waits between the 5 attempts | 2 s, 4 s, 8 s, 16 s | 4 retries, ~30 s of backoff before the job lands in `failed` |\n| Time to start 10,000 document jobs | 10,000 ÷ 50 per minute | ~200 minutes, however many workers you add |\n| Jobs in flight per replica | `concurrency: 4` | 4 documents at once; 3 replicas give 12 |\n| A 429 at chunk 300 of a 500-chunk document | `rateLimit(30_000)` then retry | queue pauses 30 s, job resumes at chunk 300 thanks to `embeddingExists` |\n\n### Production snippet: nightly evals and clean shutdown\nA scheduler registered by every producer replica still yields one run per night, and the worker drains\nin-flight jobs on `SIGTERM` instead of leaving them to the stall checker.\n\n```ts\nimport { Queue, Worker } from \"bullmq\";\n\nconst evalQueue = new Queue(\"evals\", { connection: conn() });\nawait evalQueue.upsertJobScheduler(\"nightly-regression\",\n  { pattern: \"0 2 * * *\" },                                         // 02:00 every day\n  { name: \"run-suite\", data: { suite: \"regression\" }, opts: { attempts: 2 } });\n\nconst evalWorker = new Worker(\"evals\", runSuite, { connection: conn(), concurrency: 1 });\n\nprocess.on(\"SIGTERM\", async () => {\n  await evalWorker.close();                                         // stop claiming, wait for active jobs\n  process.exit(0);\n});\n```","patterns":["**Drain workers on deploy.** Handle `SIGTERM` with `await worker.close()` and set the orchestrator's grace period above your typical job duration, or make jobs resumable so a cut-off job restarts cheaply. Monitor `stalled` events and duplicate side effects right after deploys.","**Alert on depth and age, not just failures.** Poll `queue.getJobCounts('waiting', 'active', 'delayed', 'failed')` into metrics, and record start delay as `job.processedOn - job.timestamp`. A rising start delay with idle workers points at the limiter; with busy workers, at capacity.","**Enqueue through an outbox.** Write the job request into a Postgres outbox row in the business transaction; a relay calls `queue.add` with the outbox row id as `jobId`, so relay retries dedupe. Monitor the age of the oldest unrelayed outbox row.","**Sandbox CPU-heavy processors.** Pass a processor file path to `Worker`, optionally with `useWorkerThreads: true`, for parsing, chunking or vector math, so the main event loop keeps renewing locks. Monitor event-loop lag and stalled counts per queue.","**Run a dead-letter review loop.** Keep failures with `removeOnFail: { age: ... }`, inspect them in a dashboard such as Bull Board, and re-run fixed ones with `job.retry()`. Monitor failed counts grouped by error message and attempt."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    state \"waiting\" as W\n    state \"delayed\" as D\n    state \"prioritized\" as PR\n    state \"active, locked\" as A\n    state \"waiting-children\" as WC\n    state \"completed\" as C\n    state \"failed\" as F\n    [*] --> W : queue.add\n    [*] --> D : add with delay\n    [*] --> PR : add with priority\n    [*] --> WC : flow parent\n    WC --> W : all children done\n    D --> W : due time reached\n    W --> A : claim and lock\n    PR --> A : claim and lock\n    A --> C : processor resolves\n    A --> D : throws, attempts left, backoff\n    A --> W : lock expired, stall checker\n    A --> F : attempts or stalls exhausted\n    C --> [*] : removeOnComplete\n    F --> [*] : removeOnFail","caption":"The job lifecycle: every arrow is one atomic Lua script, and the stall arrow from active back to\nwaiting is what makes a crashed worker's job run again."},"interview":[{"q":"Why use a job queue for an agent run instead of doing the work in the HTTP handler?","a":"- **Lifetime:** a two-minute run outlives load-balancer idle timeouts and dies with the process on a\n  deploy, leaving no record.\n- **Control:** a queue adds retries with backoff, a fleet-wide rate limit against the provider, and\n  visibility into waiting, active and failed work.\n- **The API then returns a run id** and streams progress via `QueueEvents` or polling."},{"q":"Jobs are being processed twice even though no worker crashed. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect stalls:** a processor blocking the event loop stops lock renewal, so the stall checker\n  hands the job to another worker while the first is still running.\n- **Confirm** with `stalled` events, event-loop lag metrics and overlapping start times for one job id.\n- **Fix** by moving CPU work to a sandboxed processor, and make side effects idempotent on `job.id`\n  regardless, because at-least-once delivery will duplicate eventually."},{"q":"When would you not use BullMQ?","a":"- **Low volume with Postgres already present:** `SKIP LOCKED` polling keeps jobs transactional with your\n  data and removes a Redis dependency.\n- **Long multi-step workflows:** runs needing durable mid-run state, timers of days or human approval\n  belong in a durable-execution engine such as Temporal.\n- **Redis you cannot make durable:** if the only instance is `allkeys-lru` with snapshots, important\n  jobs will be lost."},{"q":"How do you keep a fleet of embedding workers under a provider quota of 50 requests per minute?","a":"- **Use the Worker `limiter`,** `{ max: 50, duration: 60_000 }`, which is shared across all workers of\n  the queue; adding replicas then adds no extra load on the provider.\n- **On a 429 anyway,** call `queue.rateLimit(ms)` and throw `Worker.RateLimitError()` so the job waits\n  without burning an attempt.\n- **Caveat:** the limiter counts jobs, not provider calls; if one job makes many calls, size jobs or add\n  a token bucket inside the processor."},{"q":"What does a deterministic `jobId` buy you, and where does it stop protecting you?","a":"- **It makes enqueue idempotent:** a double-submit of `embed-<docId>` is a no-op while the first job is\n  still stored.\n- **It stops at retention:** once `removeOnComplete` deletes the job the id can be reused, so a late\n  duplicate runs again; while it is retained, an intended re-run is silently ignored.\n- **So** keep processor-side idempotency, or use BullMQ 5's `deduplication` option with an explicit TTL."}],"tradeoffs":["**Speed and sharing vs durability.** Redis makes claim, renew and move microsecond-fast and lets any number of replicas share a queue, but durability equals Redis's: AOF and `noeviction` are mandatory for work you cannot re-enqueue; a cache-style instance is fine for best-effort jobs.","**Free retries vs repeated work.** Exponential backoff absorbs transient provider failures without code, but each retry re-runs the whole processor; a 500-chunk job failing at chunk 499 restarts at chunk 0 unless the processor checkpoints per chunk.","**One limiter vs fairness.** A worker-level `limiter` keeps a fleet under a provider's quota with one setting, but it is per queue, not per tenant or model; fairness needs separate queues, BullMQ Pro groups, or an in-processor token bucket.","**At-least-once vs exactly-once.** At-least-once is simple and robust and forces idempotent processors; the queue does not offer exactly-once, and chasing it with locks inside the processor usually costs more than a unique constraint.","**Lightweight vs workflow engine.** BullMQ needs only Redis, but has no durable mid-job state; once runs need long waits, signals or replay, Temporal-style engines earn their operational cost."],"pitfalls":["**A processor that blocks the event loop** (large synchronous JSON parse, vector math). Symptom: `stalled` events and jobs processed twice because the lock renewal timer could not fire. Fix: a sandboxed processor or a worker thread.","**Non-idempotent side effects keyed on nothing** (send email, charge budget, append message). Symptom: duplicates after a deploy or a stall, with every attempt logging success.","**An ioredis connection with `maxRetriesPerRequest` at its default (20).** Symptom: a Worker throwing at construction when handed that instance (BullMQ only warns and overrides it when given plain options), or blocking waits erroring out during a Redis reconnect.","**Relying on `jobId` for dedup while also using `removeOnComplete`.** Symptom: the \"duplicate\" document is embedded again a few minutes after the first job finished, because the completed job (and its id) was already deleted.","**`removeOnComplete` left off in a high-volume queue.** Symptom: Redis memory grows until eviction or out-of-memory, weeks after launch.","**Treating `queue.add` as part of a database transaction.** Symptom: jobs for rows that rolled back, or committed rows with no job. Fix: an outbox table and a relay worker.","**Exiting with `process.exit()` instead of `await worker.close()`.** Symptom: active jobs re-run after every deploy once the stall checker catches them."],"when":"- **Use when:** work outlives a request, must survive restarts, needs retries with backoff, or must be\n  throttled against an external quota: agent runs, ingestion, embedding, evaluation batches, webhook\n  delivery, scheduled refreshes.\n- **Avoid when:** work is synchronous and sub-second; keep it in the request.\n- **Alternatives:** with Postgres already there and low volume, `SKIP LOCKED` polling can be enough; for\n  durable multi-step state, timers of hours or days, or human approval steps, use a durable-execution\n  engine and keep BullMQ for simple, idempotent units.\n- **Measure:** waiting-queue depth, job age at start, failure rate by attempt, stalled count and worker\n  concurrency utilisation.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"what happens when a worker dies mid-job\" and \"how do you keep the side effect\n  from happening twice\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs job payloads and ids, writes idempotent processors with per-chunk checkpoints, configures attempts, backoff, concurrency and limiter, and closes workers gracefully on SIGTERM.","architect":"Decides queue topology (per workload, per tenant tier), the Redis durability policy behind it, where the outbox sits, and when a workflow engine replaces the queue for long-running agent state.","fde":"Deploys workers inside the customer's environment, checks their Redis eviction and persistence settings, and tunes limiter values to the customer's negotiated provider quotas."},"check":{"q":"A worker embedding 2,000-chunk documents dies during a deploy. On restart, some documents are\nembedded twice and one job is marked failed after being \"stalled\" repeatedly. Explain what happened and\nwhat you change.","a":"When the process died, its jobs' locks expired, the stall checker moved them back to waiting and\nother workers re-ran them from the first chunk; because the upsert was not keyed on (document, chunk\nindex), the second pass wrote duplicate vectors. The repeatedly stalled job is a processor that blocks\nthe event loop, probably synchronous parsing or vector math, so lock renewal stops even though the\nprocess is alive; after `maxStalledCount` stalls BullMQ marks it failed. Changes: make the upsert\nidempotent with a unique key, check for existing chunks at the start of each iteration so a retry\nresumes instead of restarting, move CPU-heavy work to a sandboxed processor, and close workers with\n`await worker.close()` on SIGTERM so in-flight jobs finish before the pod is killed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes a job survive a worker process crash in BullMQ?","options":["The processor's promise is serialised to disk by Node","Job state lives in Redis and the lock expires, so the stall checker moves the active job back to waiting","Workers replicate in-flight jobs to each other over pub/sub","BullMQ writes a write-ahead log in the worker's filesystem"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Durability comes from Redis holding the job and from the lock-and-stall mechanism: a dead worker stops renewing its lock, and once it expires the job is returned to the queue for another worker, which is why handlers must be idempotent."},{"q":"Why does BullMQ require `maxRetriesPerRequest: null` on a worker's ioredis connection?","options":["To disable job retries, which BullMQ manages itself","Because Redis Cluster does not support per-request retries","So that commands, including the worker's long blocking waits, queue until Redis reconnects instead of failing after a fixed number of reconnection attempts","To allow the connection to be shared with pub/sub subscribers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ioredis's `maxRetriesPerRequest` fails pending commands after N reconnection attempts (20 by default); a worker's loop is built around long blocking commands that must survive a reconnect rather than reject, so BullMQ insists on `null` (wait indefinitely) for worker connections."},{"q":"Which design keeps a 500-chunk embedding job from restarting at chunk 0 after a transient failure at chunk 499?","options":["Increase `attempts` so the job eventually gets through","Lower `concurrency` so fewer jobs fail at once","Make each chunk's write idempotent and check for existing chunks so a retry skips work already stored","Use `priority: 1` so the job is retried first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"BullMQ retries re-run the whole processor; only application-level checkpoints (idempotent upserts plus an existence check at the start of each chunk) turn a retry into a resume. More attempts just repeat the same 499 calls."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Brooker, 2015 — Exponential Backoff And Jitter (AWS Architecture Blog)","Official docs: Anthropic TypeScript SDK","Official docs: OpenAI Node SDK"],"why":"- **Unbounded latency.** A naive client does `await fetch(url)`. A provider that accepts the TCP\n  connection and then stalls leaves the handler waiting until the socket gives up; for Node's built-in\n  `fetch` (undici, with 300-second header and body timeouts) that is minutes by default. A few hundred of\n  those hold every worker slot, pooled DB connection and queue lock.\n- **Transient errors become user-facing failures.** A 429 from a shared quota or a 529\n  `overloaded_error` from Anthropic often clears within seconds, so not retrying throws away a request\n  that would have succeeded.\n- **\"Retry everything, immediately, five times\" is worse.** During an incident a thousand clients\n  retrying in lockstep multiply load five-fold at the wrong moment, synchronised by the same schedule,\n  and non-idempotent calls (a tool that sends an email, a POST that creates a resource) execute more than\n  once.\n- **Each layer fixes one problem.** Timeouts fix the first; backoff with jitter fixes the second and\n  defuses the third; idempotency keys make retries safe.\n- **The breaker handles what backoff cannot.** When the dependency is down for minutes, the right\n  behaviour is not \"wait 30 s then fail\" per request but \"fail in a millisecond and try later\", which\n  protects your latency budget, frees resources and gives the provider room to recover.","how":"### Timeouts first\n- **Every attempt gets an `AbortSignal`** with a per-attempt timeout (`AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` in\n  current Node, or an `AbortController` you cancel), *because* a retry policy without a timeout retries\n  nothing while the first attempt hangs.\n- **Streaming needs two numbers.** Time-to-first-token differs from total completion time: time out the\n  connection and first chunk, then use an idle timeout between chunks, not one giant budget that must\n  cover a 90-second answer.\n- **Carry a request-level deadline** through the call chain so attempt timeouts plus backoff never\n  exceed what the caller can wait for.\n\n### Classify before retrying\n| Outcome | Retry? | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, Anthropic's 529 | yes | transient: a later attempt can succeed |\n| 400, 401, 403, 404, 422 | never | the request is wrong and will be wrong again |\n| `ECONNREFUSED`, `UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT` | always | the request never reached the server |\n| `ECONNRESET`, read timeout, socket closed mid-body | only if idempotent | the server may have processed it and the reply was lost |\n\n- **Ambiguous failures cost money.** The first attempt may have succeeded server-side after you stopped\n  listening, and an LLM call that completed is billed whether or not you received it.\n\n### Exponential backoff with full jitter\n```\nsleep(n) = random(0, min(cap, base × 2^n))      // attempt n\n```\n- **The exponential term** backs off fast enough to help a recovering provider.\n- **The cap** keeps the wait sane.\n- **The jitter** spreads clients out, *because* identical schedules re-synchronise the thundering herd on\n  every attempt.\n- **Honour `Retry-After`** (seconds or an HTTP date; the Anthropic and OpenAI SDKs also read a\n  millisecond variant): the server knows its own recovery window better than your formula.\n- **Bound both** attempt count and total elapsed time.\n\n### Idempotency\n- **A retried POST must be safe** when it creates a run, sends a message or charges a budget.\n- **Pass an idempotency key** the server deduplicates on (many APIs accept an `Idempotency-Key` header),\n  or make the operation naturally idempotent (PUT by id, upsert).\n- **If neither is possible,** do not retry automatically; surface the ambiguity to the caller.\n\n### The vendor SDKs\n- **What the Anthropic TypeScript SDK retries:** connection errors, 408, 409, 429 and 5xx, with backoff,\n  honouring `Retry-After`. The OpenAI Node SDK follows the same design with the same defaults.\n- **Options:** `maxRetries` (default 2) and `timeout` (default 10 minutes, in milliseconds). For a\n  non-streaming call whose `max_tokens` implies a longer generation, the SDK refuses and asks you to\n  stream unless you set `timeout` explicitly.\n- **Deadline arithmetic.** A timed-out attempt is itself retried, so wall-clock time can reach roughly\n  `timeout × (maxRetries + 1)` plus backoff.\n- **Typed errors.** `Anthropic.RateLimitError` for 429, `Anthropic.InternalServerError` for 5xx\n  including 529, `Anthropic.APIConnectionError`, all subclasses of `Anthropic.APIError` with `.status`,\n  `.headers`, the parsed body on `.error` and `.type` such as `overloaded_error`. Classification is an\n  `instanceof` chain plus a status check, not string matching.\n- **Configure, do not wrap.** Set those options rather than adding a second retry loop, *because* nested\n  retries multiply: 3 × 3 = 9 attempts against a provider that is already overloaded.\n\n### Circuit breaker\n1. **Closed** (normal): calls pass through; failures are counted in a rolling window.\n2. **Open**: once failures exceed a threshold (say, over half of at least 20 calls in 10 s), the breaker\n   rejects calls immediately for a `resetTimeout` without touching the dependency.\n3. **Half-open**: after the timeout, one trial call goes through; success closes the breaker, failure\n   re-opens it.\n\n- **Trips on rate over a minimum volume,** not one failure, *because* one bad response is noise.\n- **Rejects fast** *because* a queued request that will fail anyway costs latency and a connection slot.\n- **Probes instead of flipping back blindly** *because* you want evidence of recovery before releasing\n  the full load.\n- **Needs a fallback:** a smaller model, a cached answer, a degraded response, or a clear error with a\n  `Retry-After` for your own callers.\n- **Libraries:** `opossum` is the common Node breaker; `cockatiel` composes retry, timeout, breaker and\n  bulkhead policies; `p-retry` covers retry alone.\n- **State is per process.** With 20 replicas, each counts its own failures, trips on its own schedule and\n  sends its own probe, so the provider sees up to 20 probes per reset period. Usually acceptable; sharing\n  state through Redis makes the fleet trip together but puts a network call in front of every request\n  to protect against a network failure.\n\n### Bulkheads and retry budgets\n- **Bulkhead:** limit concurrent in-flight calls per dependency (`p-limit` or a semaphore) so one slow\n  provider cannot consume every worker slot. For LLM providers this also respects concurrency-based\n  quotas, and backed by Redis it works across replicas.\n- **Why a budget:** per-request limits do not bound fleet-wide amplification; if every request may make\n  3 attempts, an outage can triple traffic.\n- **Retry budget:** cap retries as a fraction of recent requests (for example, at most one retry per ten\n  requests over the last minute, an illustrative ratio to tune). During a blip almost every failure gets\n  its retry; during an outage the budget empties and failures surface immediately.\n- **Prior art:** it is the per-client analogue of a breaker; some service meshes and gRPC clients\n  implement it as retry throttling.\n\n### Composing the layers\nFrom the outside in:\n\n| Order | Layer | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | request deadline | it is the caller's promise; every inner wait must fit inside it |\n| 2 | bulkhead | caps concurrency before any work starts |\n| 3 | breaker | one exhausted retry sequence is one logical failure; inside, it would count each attempt and trip three times faster, and an open breaker should stop retries from starting |\n| 4 | retry with backoff | acts on classified errors |\n| 5 | per-attempt timeout | bounds a single attempt, so a hung attempt becomes a classifiable error |","example":"### `fetchWithRetry` and a minimal breaker\nA per-attempt timeout (combined with the caller's own signal), classification that retries ambiguous\nfailures (timeouts, resets) only for idempotent requests, full-jitter backoff and `Retry-After`, plus a\nconsecutive-failure breaker that lets exactly one probe through when half-open.\n\nProduction code uses the SDK's built-in retries and `opossum` or `cockatiel`, but the mechanics are\nthese. After the last retryable status the response is returned, not thrown, so the breaker's wrapped\nfunction must turn 5xx and 429 into failures itself, as the usage line shows.\n\n```ts\nconst RETRYABLE = new Set([408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529]);\nconst NEVER_SENT = [\"ECONNREFUSED\", \"UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT\"];           // safe for any method\nconst MAYBE_SENT = [\"ECONNRESET\", \"ETIMEDOUT\", \"UND_ERR_SOCKET\"];         // idempotent requests only\nconst sleep = (ms: number) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));\nconst jitter = (n: number, base: number, cap: number) => Math.random() * Math.min(cap, base * 2 ** n);\n\nexport async function fetchWithRetry(url: string, init: RequestInit,\n                                     o = { attempts: 4, baseMs: 500, capMs: 8_000, attemptTimeoutMs: 20_000 }) {\n  const idempotent = [\"GET\", \"HEAD\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\"].includes(init.method ?? \"GET\")\n    || new Headers(init.headers).has(\"idempotency-key\");\n  for (let n = 0; ; n++) {\n    const timeout = AbortSignal.timeout(o.attemptTimeoutMs);\n    try {\n      const res = await fetch(url, { ...init, signal: init.signal ? AbortSignal.any([init.signal, timeout]) : timeout });\n      if (!RETRYABLE.has(res.status) || n + 1 >= o.attempts) return res;     // success, non-retryable, or out of attempts\n      await res.body?.cancel();                                               // free the socket before retrying\n      const retryAfter = Number(res.headers.get(\"retry-after\"));             // seconds form; an HTTP date falls back to jitter\n      await sleep(retryAfter > 0 ? retryAfter * 1000 : jitter(n, o.baseMs, o.capMs));\n    } catch (err: any) {\n      const code = err?.cause?.code;                                          // undici wraps socket errors in err.cause\n      const transient = NEVER_SENT.includes(code) ||\n        (idempotent && (err?.name === \"TimeoutError\" || MAYBE_SENT.includes(code)));\n      if (!transient || n + 1 >= o.attempts) throw err;                       // caller aborts (AbortError) are never retried\n      await sleep(jitter(n, o.baseMs, o.capMs));\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport class CircuitBreaker {\n  private failures = 0; private openedAt = 0; private state: \"closed\" | \"open\" | \"half\" = \"closed\";\n  constructor(private threshold = 5, private resetMs = 30_000) {}\n  async call<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, fallback: () => T): Promise<T> {\n    if (this.state !== \"closed\") {\n      if (this.state === \"half\" || Date.now() - this.openedAt < this.resetMs) return fallback();  // fail fast; one probe at a time\n      this.state = \"half\";                                                      // this caller is the probe\n    }\n    try {\n      const out = await fn();\n      this.failures = 0; this.state = \"closed\"; return out;\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (++this.failures >= this.threshold || this.state === \"half\") { this.state = \"open\"; this.openedAt = Date.now(); }\n      throw e;\n    }\n  }\n}\n// await breaker.call(async () => { const r = await fetchWithRetry(url, init);\n//   if (r.status >= 500 || r.status === 429) throw new Error(`upstream ${r.status}`); return r; }, () => degraded());\n```\n\n### Production snippet: SDK as the single retry owner, `opossum` above it\nThe SDK owns retries and per-attempt timeouts; the breaker counts one outcome per logical call and\nignores 400s, which reflect our bug rather than provider health.\n\n```ts\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\nimport CircuitBreaker from \"opossum\";\n\nconst anthropic = new Anthropic({ maxRetries: 2, timeout: 20_000 });   // worst case ~60 s plus backoff\n\nasync function complete(prompt: string) {\n  const msg = await anthropic.messages.create({\n    model: process.env.MODEL!, max_tokens: 512,\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: prompt }],\n  });\n  return msg.content.flatMap((b) => (b.type === \"text\" ? [b.text] : [])).join(\"\");\n}\n\nconst breaker = new CircuitBreaker(complete, {\n  timeout: false,                     // the SDK already bounds each attempt\n  errorThresholdPercentage: 50,       // open when over half of calls fail...\n  volumeThreshold: 20,                // ...once at least 20 calls were seen...\n  rollingCountTimeout: 10_000,        // ...in a 10 s rolling window\n  resetTimeout: 30_000,               // then allow a probe after 30 s\n  errorFilter: (e: unknown) => e instanceof Anthropic.BadRequestError,   // 400s do not count\n});\nbreaker.fallback(() => \"The assistant is busy right now. Please try again in a minute.\");\nbreaker.on(\"open\", () => console.warn(\"breaker open: anthropic\"));\n\nexport const answer = (prompt: string) => breaker.fire(prompt);\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: how nested retries amplify an outage\nOne user action during a provider incident, where every attempt fails slowly.\n\n| Retry layout | Attempts per user action | Worst-case wait with 20 s attempt timeouts |\n|---|---|---|\n| SDK `maxRetries: 2` only | 3 | ~60 s plus backoff |\n| SDK 3 attempts × handler loop of 4 | 12 | ~240 s plus backoff |\n| Same, plus a queue that re-runs the job 3 times | 36 | ~12 minutes, much of it billed if attempts completed server-side |\n| SDK only, under an open breaker | 0 after the trip | about a millisecond, served by the fallback |\n\nFull-jitter sleeps for `fetchWithRetry` defaults (`baseMs: 500`, `capMs: 8_000`, 4 attempts) are drawn\nfrom 0-500 ms, 0-1,000 ms and 0-2,000 ms before attempts 2, 3 and 4.","patterns":["**One retry owner per call path.** Decide whether the SDK, the handler or the queue retries, and set the others to fail fast (for example SDK `maxRetries: 2` and a queue job with `attempts: 1` for that step). Monitor attempts per logical request and provider calls per user action during incidents.","**Propagate the deadline.** Compute the remaining budget at each hop and pass it down as an `AbortSignal.timeout(remaining)` or a per-request `timeout` option, so inner retries never outlive the caller. Monitor deadline-exceeded counts per dependency.","**Rate-based breaker with a fallback per provider.** Configure `opossum` with `errorThresholdPercentage`, `volumeThreshold` and `resetTimeout`, and route the fallback to a smaller model or cached answer. Alert on `open` events and track time spent open.","**Bulkhead per dependency.** Wrap each provider in its own `p-limit(n)` sized to the quota; export `limit.activeCount` and `limit.pendingCount` as gauges. A growing pending count means the dependency, not your service, is the bottleneck.","**Fault-injection tests.** Point the client at a stub server, or undici's `MockAgent`, that returns 429 with `Retry-After`, 529, connection resets and stalls, and assert attempt counts, waits and fallbacks in CI. Monitor that the suite runs on every change to client code."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Caller with deadline\"] --> B[\"Bulkhead: take a slot\"]\n    B --> CB{\"Breaker open?\"}\n    CB -->|\"yes\"| FB[\"Fallback or fast error\"]\n    CB -->|\"no, or half-open probe\"| T[\"Attempt with own timeout\"]\n    T --> P[(\"Provider API\")]\n    T -->|\"success or non-retryable\"| OK[\"Return to caller\"]\n    T -->|\"429, 5xx, safe network error\"| K{\"Attempts and deadline left?\"}\n    K -->|\"yes: jitter or Retry-After\"| T\n    K -->|\"no\"| F[\"One logical failure\"]\n    F -->|\"counts toward\"| CB","caption":"The composed call path: the breaker sees one logical outcome per call, while the retry loop inside it\nhandles individual attempts within the deadline."},"interview":[{"q":"Which errors should an LLM client retry, and which should it never retry?","a":"- **Retry** 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, Anthropic's 529, and connection errors where the request\n  never left (`ECONNREFUSED`, connect timeouts).\n- **Never retry** 400, 401, 403, 404 or 422, because the same request fails the same way.\n- **Retry only if idempotent:** resets and read timeouts after sending, because the server may have\n  completed and billed the call."},{"q":"Why put the circuit breaker outside the retry loop rather than inside it?","a":"- **Outside,** one exhausted retry sequence counts as one logical failure, and an open breaker stops\n  retries from starting at all.\n- **Inside,** the breaker counts every attempt and trips about three times faster than its threshold\n  implies, while the retry loop keeps hitting a breaker that is already open.\n- **The deadline still wraps both,** so total time stays inside the caller's budget."},{"q":"A breaker keeps opening at night when traffic is low. What is wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"- **It trips on counts without a minimum volume:** two failures out of three requests looks like a 67%\n  error rate.\n- **Fix:** trip on rate over a minimum call volume, for example `volumeThreshold` in `opossum`, and\n  exclude caller bugs such as 400s from the failure count.\n- **Then verify** with the history of breaker `open` events against request volume."},{"q":"When would you not add a circuit breaker to an outbound dependency?","a":"- **No meaningful fallback and low traffic:** failing fast and failing slowly give the user the same\n  error, and the breaker adds tuning burden.\n- **A queue already owns failure handling:** background jobs can back off at the queue level instead.\n- **Very low call volume,** where a rate threshold cannot gather enough signal to trip correctly."},{"q":"Your SDK has `maxRetries: 2` and `timeout: 20_000`. What is the worst-case wall-clock time, and how do you budget it?","a":"- **Each timed-out attempt is retried,** so the worst case is about 20 s × 3 = 60 s plus backoff sleeps.\n- **Budget from the caller's deadline:** if the endpoint promises 30 s, lower `timeout`, lower\n  `maxRetries`, or pass a per-request signal derived from the remaining deadline.\n- **For streaming,** use a first-token timeout plus an idle timeout instead of one total budget."}],"tradeoffs":["**Success rate vs outage load.** Retries raise success during blips but multiply load during outages; with jitter and a total-time cap the amplification is bounded, without them a fleet-wide retry storm can keep a provider down.","**Predictable latency vs long completions.** Aggressive per-attempt timeouts cut off slow-yet-successful long completions; for large outputs use a time-to-first-token timeout plus an idle timeout rather than one short total budget.","**Protection vs false trips.** A breaker protects your latency and the provider, but a mis-tuned threshold trips on normal error noise and takes a healthy dependency offline; thresholds should be rate-based over a minimum call volume.","**Availability vs quality.** Falling back to a cheaper model or cached answer keeps the product up at a quality cost users may notice; a chat assistant can degrade, a compliance extraction probably cannot.","**Retry budget vs individual requests.** A budget bounds fleet-wide amplification, but denies retries to some requests during a genuine blip that a per-request policy would have saved; it becomes overhead when traffic is too low for amplification to hurt the provider.","**SDK retry vs your policy.** The built-in retry is simpler and correct for its own error classes, but knows nothing about your deadline or breaker; integrate through `maxRetries` and `timeout`, not a wrapper loop."],"pitfalls":["**Nested retry loops** (SDK retries inside your retries inside the queue's retries). Symptom: a single user action producing dozens of provider calls during an incident, visible as a cost spike in the same hour as the outage.","**Retrying non-idempotent POSTs on timeout or `ECONNRESET`.** Symptom: duplicate runs, messages or charges that each carry a different request id but trace back to one user click.","**No jitter.** Symptom: provider error rates oscillating in neat waves at your backoff intervals, as every client retries at the same instant.","**Breaker without a minimum volume.** Symptom: the breaker opening at 03:00 because two of the night's three requests failed.","**Ignoring `Retry-After`.** Symptom: 429s keep coming despite backoff, because the server told you its window and you retried earlier.","**Replaying a stream that failed after the first tokens into the same response.** Symptom: clients receive an answer that restarts in the middle."],"when":"- **Baseline, always:** every outbound call to an LLM, embedding, vector, search or SaaS API gets a\n  timeout and a classified retry policy; this is not an optimisation.\n- **Add a breaker** per dependency when the service has a meaningful fallback or when the dependency's\n  failure would otherwise exhaust worker capacity.\n- **Add a bulkhead** when one slow dependency can starve the others.\n- **Avoid** retrying inside a request path when a queue above it already retries; pick the layer that\n  owns retries and make the others fail fast.\n- **Measure:** attempts per request, retry success rate, breaker state transitions, p99 latency with and\n  without retries, and duplicate side effects.\n- **Interviews:** derive the full-jitter formula, explain why breakers trip on rate, and say which layer\n  should own retries.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures SDK `maxRetries` and `timeout`, wraps tool calls with `cockatiel` or `opossum`, passes `AbortSignal`s and idempotency keys, and writes tests that simulate 429s, 529s and timeouts.","architect":"Assigns retry ownership per layer (client, service, queue), defines breaker thresholds and fallbacks per provider, and sets the latency budget each dependency is allowed to consume.","fde":"Tunes timeouts and limits to a customer's negotiated quotas and their egress proxy's behaviour, and explains why their incident dashboard shows retry waves."},"check":{"q":"During a provider incident your service's p99 latency went from 3 s to 90 s and your bill\ntripled for the hour, even though the provider was mostly returning errors. Explain the mechanics and\nthe fix.","a":"Each attempt hung for tens of seconds on a provider that accepted connections but failed slowly\n(the SDK's 10-minute default timeout never fired, so nothing cut the wait short), then the SDK retried\ntwice, then the handler's own loop retried three times, and the queue above re-enqueued failures: a\nsingle user action became a dozen or more slow attempts (3 SDK attempts × 4 handler attempts before the\nqueue even acts), which explains the latency. The cost comes from the attempts that did complete\nserver-side, and are billed, after your client had already timed out or given up, plus work the queue\nre-ran after it had in fact succeeded. The fix is layered: a\nper-attempt timeout sized for the call, a single owner of retries (the SDK with `maxRetries` set and\nnothing above it), full-jitter backoff honouring `Retry-After`, a total deadline per request, and a\ncircuit breaker per provider that opens on a high failure rate and fails fast with a fallback or a clear\nerror. Idempotency keys make any remaining retries safe to repeat."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why add random jitter to exponential backoff rather than retrying at fixed exponential intervals?","options":["Jitter makes individual retries succeed more often","Without jitter, clients that failed together retry together, so each wave of retries hits the provider at the same instant","Jitter is required by the HTTP specification for 429 responses","It reduces the total number of attempts per request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Backoff alone keeps the schedule synchronised across clients; randomising each sleep spreads the retries over the window so the provider sees a smooth load instead of spikes that re-trigger the overload."},{"q":"A circuit breaker is in the open state. What happens to a new call?","options":["It is queued until the dependency recovers","It is retried with backoff until the reset timeout elapses","It is rejected immediately (or served a fallback) without contacting the dependency","It is sent, and the breaker closes if it succeeds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Open means \"known bad\": the breaker fails fast to protect latency and the dependency, and only after the reset timeout does it move to half-open and allow a single probe call."},{"q":"Which response should a well-behaved client NOT retry automatically?","options":["503 Service Unavailable with a `Retry-After` header","A connection reset before any response bytes arrived","400 Bad Request caused by an over-long prompt","429 Too Many Requests from a shared rate limit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A 400 means the request itself is invalid and will fail identically on every attempt; retrying only adds load and latency, whereas 503, 429 and a reset before any response bytes are transient signals that a later attempt can succeed."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","sources":["Official docs: OpenTelemetry JavaScript","OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions — Generative AI","Official docs: Anthropic TypeScript SDK (OpenTelemetry tracing)"],"why":"- **Agent requests fan out across processes.** An HTTP handler calls a planner model, which calls three\n  tools; two hit Postgres and one enqueues a BullMQ job that calls an embedding API on another replica.\n- **Logs say *that*, not *why*.** When that request takes 40 s or costs 60,000 tokens, logs from six\n  processes show it happened, but nothing ties the lines together or records durations as a tree.\n- **Hand-rolled tracing breaks.** A `requestId` threaded through every function and `console.time` pairs\n  break at the first `await` you forgot, do not cross the queue, and produce data no tool can draw as a\n  waterfall.\n- **Node has no thread to hang context on.** \"Which request is this log line from\" must travel along the\n  async continuation chain; OTel's Node SDK does that with `AsyncLocalStorage` and propagates context\n  across processes with standard headers.\n- **Vendor neutrality pays off.** Every backend (Jaeger, Tempo, Honeycomb, Datadog, Langfuse and others\n  via OTLP) accepts the format, which matters *because* the backend you pick in year one is rarely the\n  one you have in year three, and enterprise customers want your spans in *their* collector. The\n  observability module covers concepts; this lesson is the Node mechanics.","how":"### API, SDK and the split between them\n- **Your code calls the API** (`@opentelemetry/api`): `trace.getTracer(name).startActiveSpan(...)`,\n  `metrics.getMeter(...)`.\n- **The SDK decides what happens** (`@opentelemetry/sdk-node`, or `NodeTracerProvider` directly):\n  sampling, batching, exporting. It is configured once.\n- **Libraries depend only on the API** *because* then they can be instrumented without forcing an SDK or\n  exporter on their users; with no SDK registered, API calls are no-ops.\n\n### Startup order and auto-instrumentation\n1. **Instrumentation patches modules as they load:** `http`, `undici`/`fetch`, `pg`, `ioredis`,\n   `express`, `fastify`, `pino` and others.\n2. **So it must be installed before they are imported.** The SDK lives in its own `instrumentation.ts`,\n   loaded with `node --require` (CommonJS) or `node --import` (ESM), or via `NODE_OPTIONS`.\n3. **ESM needs a loader hook.** `import` statements bypass the CommonJS `require` hook, so the file must\n   register OTel's ESM hook (`module.register(\"@opentelemetry/instrumentation/hook.mjs\", ...)`). The\n   zero-code entry point `@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node/register` does this for you.\n4. **The classic silent failure:** importing it from `app.ts` after your framework. No error, no spans.\n\n### Spans, kinds and the tree\n- **A span** is a named, timed operation with a trace id (shared by the whole request), its own span id,\n  a parent span id, attributes, events (timestamped points such as \"first token\") and a status.\n- **The backend rebuilds the tree from parent ids alone,** so a missing parent link produces an orphan\n  root rather than an error.\n\n| Kind | Used for |\n|---|---|\n| `SERVER` | handling an inbound request |\n| `CLIENT` | an outbound call |\n| `PRODUCER` / `CONSUMER` | the two ends of a queue |\n| `INTERNAL` | in-process work such as an agent step |\n\n- **Kinds matter** *because* backends derive service maps and latency breakdowns from them: a `CLIENT`\n  span with no matching `SERVER` child is time spent waiting on something you do not instrument, such as\n  a model provider.\n\n### Context propagation in a process\n- **The SDK installs an `AsyncLocalStorage` context manager,** so the active span follows `await`,\n  promises, timers and callbacks.\n- **The rule:** `AsyncLocalStorage` captures the current store whenever Node creates an async resource (a\n  promise, a timer, a socket callback) and restores it when that continuation runs. \"The active span\" is\n  whatever was active when the continuation was *scheduled*.\n- **Where context gets lost.** A library that queues callbacks and invokes them later from a different\n  continuation (a hand-rolled connection pool, a batching client) runs them in the wrong context. An\n  `EventEmitter` listener runs in the context of whoever called `emit()`, not whoever registered it.\n- **The fix:** bind explicitly with `context.bind(context.active(), fn)` (or `AsyncResource.bind`) where\n  the callback is handed over.\n\n### Context propagation across processes\n- **HTTP is automatic.** The `http` and `fetch` instrumentations inject and extract `traceparent` and\n  `tracestate` (W3C Trace Context) on every outbound and inbound request.\n- **Queues are not, unless the library supports it.** BullMQ 5 has an opt-in `telemetry` option,\n  provided by the `bullmq-otel` package.\n- **Otherwise do it by hand:** serialise context into job data on the producer\n  (`propagation.inject(context.active(), carrier)`) and restore it in the worker\n  (`propagation.extract(...)`) so the job's spans link to the originating request.\n\n### Spans for model calls\n- **The Anthropic TypeScript SDK emits its own span** per request, `anthropic.messages.create` (kind\n  `CLIENT`), through whatever tracer provider the application registered.\n- **Its attributes:** `gen_ai.provider.name`, `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` (cache reads and writes\n  included), `gen_ai.usage.output_tokens`, the cache read and write counts, and `anthropic.request.id`.\n  Its tool runner adds a parent span plus one span per tool. The feature is in beta, so pin the SDK\n  version.\n- **Rule 1: wrap the call in your own span** (`agent.plan`, `tool.search_docs`) with app attributes\n  (tenant, run id, prompt version); the SDK span nests under it. The SDK sends its span's `traceparent`\n  to the API, so server-side spans (if API tracing is configured) join the same trace.\n- **Rule 2: one instrumentation per SDK.** If a third-party instrumentation already patches the SDK, turn\n  one off (`openTelemetry: false` or `ANTHROPIC_OPEN_TELEMETRY=false`) or every request appears twice.\n- **Other providers:** if the SDK emits nothing, set the GenAI semantic-convention attributes yourself\n  (`gen_ai.provider.name`, the request model, `gen_ai.usage.*`). The conventions are still experimental,\n  so expect renames.\n\n### Prompt content in spans\n- **Off by default.** The SDK's content mode is `metadata_only`.\n- **`content` mode is an explicit opt-in** *because* reading conversations from traces is useful but\n  makes your tracing backend a store of customer data under its own retention rules.\n- **Size limit:** content beyond a per-span limit (0.75 MB by default) is truncated.\n- **Alternative:** send content to an LLM-specific tool (Langfuse, Phoenix) built for it.\n\n### Streaming\n- **A streaming call's span ends when the stream is fully read or closed,** so close a stream you abandon\n  or its span never ends.\n- **Spans started while reading chunks are siblings** of the SDK span, not children. Start one span of\n  your own around both the call and the read loop, and add an event at the first token so\n  time-to-first-token is queryable.\n\n### Metrics and logs\n- **Metrics:** a histogram for model latency and a counter for tokens by model and tenant; these\n  aggregate cheaply where traces are sampled.\n- **Logs:** Pino or Winston with the OTel log instrumentation puts `trace_id` and `span_id` on every\n  line, which lets you jump from a log to the trace.\n\n### Export and batching\n- **Pipeline:** spans go through a `BatchSpanProcessor` to an OTLP exporter (HTTP or gRPC), usually to an\n  OpenTelemetry Collector sidecar or gateway that fans out to backends, redacts and tail-samples.\n- **Why batch:** exporting each span synchronously would put a network call on every operation.\n\n| Setting | Default | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| In-memory queue | 2,048 spans | when full, new spans are dropped, not blocked |\n| Batch size | up to 512 spans | exported when a batch fills |\n| Schedule | every 5 seconds | exports whatever is queued |\n\n- **Dropping protects latency** at the cost of silently missing data; tune `OTEL_BSP_MAX_QUEUE_SIZE` and\n  watch the dropped count.\n- **`SimpleSpanProcessor`** (export on end) is for tests only.\n- **Flush on exit.** On shutdown call `sdk.shutdown()` so the batch processor flushes; otherwise the last\n  spans of every deploy vanish.\n\n### Sampling and configuration\n- **Head sampling** in the SDK (`parentbased_traceidratio` at, say, 10%) cuts cost.\n- **`parentbased` follows the caller:** a service obeys the sampled flag in the incoming `traceparent` and\n  applies its own ratio only at the root, *because* independent coin flips per service would produce\n  traces with random holes.\n- **Head sampling decides before anything has gone wrong,** so it throws away 90% of the rare slow or\n  failed traces along with the boring ones.\n- **Tail sampling in the collector** keeps errors and slow traces *because* it decides after seeing the\n  whole trace.\n- **Config is environment-driven:** `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`, `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`,\n  `OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER`, `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES`.","example":"### SDK setup and an application span around a model call\n`instrumentation.ts` configures the SDK and is loaded before the app with\n`node --import ./instrumentation.js app.js` (for a CommonJS build, use `--require` and drop the two\n`register` lines, which only an ES module can run).\n\nThe second block shows an application span around a model call; the Anthropic SDK's own\n`anthropic.messages.create` span, with token usage, nests under it automatically because the SDK uses\nthe registered provider.\n\n```ts\n// instrumentation.ts — loaded first, never imported by application code except for shutdown\nimport { register } from \"node:module\";\nregister(\"@opentelemetry/instrumentation/hook.mjs\", import.meta.url);  // ESM only: lets patches reach `import`ed modules\nimport { NodeSDK } from \"@opentelemetry/sdk-node\";\nimport { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } from \"@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node\";\nimport { OTLPTraceExporter } from \"@opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-http\";\n\nexport const sdk = new NodeSDK({\n  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter(),            // endpoint from OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT\n  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations({ \"@opentelemetry/instrumentation-fs\": { enabled: false } })],\n});\nsdk.start();                                          // registers the provider and the AsyncLocalStorage context manager\n\n// llm.ts — your span carries app semantics; the SDK span carries gen_ai.* usage\nimport { trace, SpanStatusCode } from \"@opentelemetry/api\";\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\nconst tracer = trace.getTracer(\"agent-api\");\nconst client = new Anthropic();                       // SDK tracing on by default, metadata only: no prompt text exported\n\nexport async function plan(tenant: string, runId: string, prompt: string) {\n  return tracer.startActiveSpan(\"agent.plan\", async (span) => {\n    span.setAttributes({ \"app.tenant\": tenant, \"app.run_id\": runId, \"app.prompt_version\": \"planner-v7\" });\n    try {\n      const res = await client.messages.create(\n        { model: process.env.MODEL ?? \"claude-opus-5-5\", max_tokens: 16000, messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: prompt }] },\n        { openTelemetry: { conversationId: runId } },   // written to gen_ai.conversation.id on the SDK span\n      );\n      span.setAttribute(\"app.stop_reason\", res.stop_reason ?? \"unknown\");\n      return res.content.flatMap((b) => (b.type === \"text\" ? [b.text] : [])).join(\"\");\n    } catch (e: any) {\n      span.recordException(e); span.setStatus({ code: SpanStatusCode.ERROR, message: e.message });\n      throw e;\n    } finally {\n      span.end();                                     // always end, or the span never exports\n    }\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### Carrying trace context through a BullMQ job by hand\nWithout the opt-in `telemetry` option, the producer writes `traceparent` into the job data and the\nworker restores it, so the worker's `CONSUMER` span becomes a child of the API request.\n\n```ts\nimport { context, propagation, trace, SpanKind, SpanStatusCode } from \"@opentelemetry/api\";\nimport { Queue, Worker } from \"bullmq\";\nconst tracer = trace.getTracer(\"agent-worker\");\n\n// producer (API process)\nexport async function enqueueRun(queue: Queue, runId: string) {\n  const carrier: Record<string, string> = {};\n  propagation.inject(context.active(), carrier);          // writes traceparent (and tracestate if set)\n  await queue.add(\"run\", { runId, otel: carrier }, { jobId: `run-${runId}` });\n}\n\n// consumer (worker process)\nexport const worker = new Worker(\"runs\", async (job) => {\n  const parent = propagation.extract(context.active(), job.data.otel ?? {});\n  return context.with(parent, () =>\n    tracer.startActiveSpan(\"run.process\", { kind: SpanKind.CONSUMER, attributes: { \"app.run_id\": job.data.runId } },\n      async (span) => {\n        try { return await processRun(job.data.runId); }\n        catch (e: any) { span.recordException(e); span.setStatus({ code: SpanStatusCode.ERROR }); throw e; }\n        finally { span.end(); }\n      }));\n}, { connection });\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what head sampling keeps\nIllustrative traffic: 200 requests per second, about 40 spans per request, 1% of requests fail.\n\n| Policy | Spans per second | Spans per day | Failed traces kept per second |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| No sampling | 8,000 | ~691 million | all 2 |\n| Head sampling at 10% | 800 | ~69 million | ~0.2: 90% of failures discarded |\n| 10% head plus collector tail sampling that keeps every error | ~800 plus error traces | ~69 million plus errors | all 2 |\n\nThe tail-sampling row needs the SDK to export every span to the collector (sample at 100% in the SDK)\nso the collector can decide per trace; the cost moves from the backend bill to collector capacity.","patterns":["**Collector gateway with tail sampling.** When errors and slow traces matter more than volume, run the Collector's `tail_sampling` processor with policies for error status and high latency, and route all spans of a trace to the same collector instance (a trace-id-aware load-balancing exporter). Monitor collector memory, refused spans and the decision-wait backlog.","**Redact in the pipeline, not in every service.** Use a Collector `attributes` processor to delete or hash sensitive keys (user emails, raw prompts if a team enabled content mode) before export. Monitor by sampling exported spans for forbidden keys in a scheduled check.","**Flush on shutdown.** On `SIGTERM` close the HTTP server, drain workers, then `await sdk.shutdown()` before exit, within the orchestrator's grace period. Monitor span counts around deploy times for gaps.","**Cost and latency as metrics, not trace queries.** Record token counts and model latency as histograms with model and tenant attributes (the GenAI conventions name `gen_ai.client.token.usage` and `gen_ai.client.operation.duration`), so dashboards stay correct under sampling. Watch attribute cardinality: tenant ids are fine, run ids are not.","**Correlate logs automatically.** Enable the Pino instrumentation (`@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pino`) so every line carries `trace_id` and `span_id`. Monitor the share of error logs that have a trace id."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Client\n    participant A as API server\n    participant L as Model provider\n    participant Q as BullMQ\n    participant W as Worker\n    participant X as OTel Collector\n    C->>A: POST /runs with traceparent\n    Note over A: SERVER span, agent.plan span via AsyncLocalStorage\n    A->>L: messages.create, CLIENT span, traceparent header\n    L-->>A: response with token usage\n    A->>Q: queue.add with injected carrier\n    Q->>W: job data carries traceparent\n    Note over W: extract carrier, CONSUMER span joins the trace\n    A-->>X: batched OTLP export\n    W-->>X: batched OTLP export","caption":"One trace across an API, a model call and a queue hop. HTTP propagation is automatic; the queue hop\nonly joins the trace because the producer injects context into the job and the worker extracts it."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between the OpenTelemetry API and SDK, and why does a library only depend on the API?","a":"- **The API** is what code calls to create spans and metrics; without a registered SDK every call is a\n  cheap no-op.\n- **The SDK** decides sampling, batching and export, and is configured once by the application.\n- **Libraries use only the API** so they can ship instrumentation without forcing an exporter or backend\n  on their users."},{"q":"Some DB spans attach to the wrong request's trace, but only under load. What is happening?","a":"- **Context follows scheduling, not registration:** `AsyncLocalStorage` restores the context active when\n  a continuation was scheduled, so a pool or batching client that runs queued callbacks later runs them\n  in someone else's context.\n- **`EventEmitter` listeners** run in the context of the `emit()` caller.\n- **Fix:** bind callbacks with `context.bind(context.active(), fn)` or `AsyncResource.bind` where they are\n  handed over, then verify with a concurrent load test."},{"q":"What would you put on a span for an LLM call, and what would you keep off it?","a":"- **On it:** model, provider, input and output tokens including cache reads, stop reason, tenant, run id\n  and prompt version, plus a first-token event for streaming.\n- **Off it by default:** prompt and completion text, because it turns the tracing backend into a store\n  of customer data with its own retention, and attributes are billed per byte.\n- **If content is needed,** opt in deliberately or send it to an LLM observability tool."},{"q":"Head sampling at 10% means you rarely find traces for failed requests. How do you fix that without tracing everything at full cost?","a":"- **Move the decision to the collector:** export every span from the SDK to a collector running tail\n  sampling that keeps all error and slow traces plus a small share of the rest.\n- **Keep `parentbased` sampling** in services so a trace is never half-sampled.\n- **Cost moves** from backend storage to collector capacity, which must buffer whole traces before\n  deciding."},{"q":"Spans from the last seconds before each deploy are missing. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"- **The `BatchSpanProcessor` holds spans in memory** (up to 2,048 by default) and exports every 5 seconds\n  or when a batch of 512 fills; a killed process loses what is queued.\n- **Fix:** on `SIGTERM`, stop accepting work, finish in-flight requests, then `await sdk.shutdown()` to\n  flush before exit.\n- **Also watch the dropped-span count,** since a full queue drops spans silently."}],"tradeoffs":["**Zero-code coverage vs control.** Auto-instrumentation gives HTTP, DB and Redis spans with no code changes, but patches modules and must load first; it also instruments noisy things (`fs`, DNS) you will want to disable to control volume.","**Head vs tail sampling.** Head sampling at the SDK is cheap and predictable but drops rare slow or failed traces; tail sampling in a collector keeps them at the cost of running and sizing the collector.","**Rich attributes vs bill and liability.** Tenant, model, tokens and prompt version make traces queryable for cost and quality, but most vendors bill attributes per byte, and prompt content in spans is a data-protection liability that needs an explicit decision.","**End-to-end traces vs discipline.** Linking queue jobs through injected context gives full traces, but every producer and consumer must carry the carrier; one unlinked hop breaks the tree into orphans.","**LLM tools vs generic tracing.** Langfuse, Phoenix and LangSmith understand prompts, evals and sessions in ways generic tracing does not, while OTel is the common wire; sending OTel spans to such a tool via OTLP avoids instrumenting twice."],"pitfalls":["**SDK initialised after the framework or `pg` is imported.** Symptom: a trace with only your manual spans, no HTTP or DB children, and no error anywhere.","**ESM app started with `--require`.** Symptom: auto-instrumentation silently does nothing because ESM modules bypass the CommonJS hook. Fix: `--import` with the register entry.","**Spans never ended on an error path** (missing `finally`), or an abandoned stream never closed. Symptom: traces that look incomplete or never export, and memory growing with open spans.","**No `sdk.shutdown()` on SIGTERM.** Symptom: the last few seconds of spans missing before every deploy, which is exactly when errors happen.","**Vendor SDK tracing and a third-party instrumentation of that SDK both active.** Symptom: every model call appears twice in the trace with identical timing.","**Context lost across a callback queue or an `EventEmitter`.** Symptom: DB or tool spans attach to the wrong request's trace, or start new roots, only under concurrency, because the callback ran in whichever context was active when it was invoked.","**Trace context not propagated into BullMQ jobs.** Symptom: worker spans form their own root traces, unlinked from the API request that caused them."],"when":"- **Use from the first deployment** in every service that calls a model; retrofitting traces during an\n  incident is the worst time.\n- **Split the work:** auto-instrumentation for transport and storage, the vendor SDK's spans for model\n  calls, manual spans for semantically meaningful work (agent step, tool call, retrieval), metrics for\n  cost and latency aggregates, logs with trace ids for detail.\n- **Add an LLM-specific tool** when you need prompt versioning, evals and human review, and feed it\n  through OTLP.\n- **Measure:** spans per request, export errors, dropped spans from the batch processor, and whether\n  every error trace is kept.\n- **Interviews:** how context crosses `await` in Node, why auto-instrumentation must load first, and what\n  you would put on a model-call span.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns `instrumentation.ts`, wraps agent steps and tool calls in spans with tenant and run attributes, propagates context into jobs, and ensures spans end and flush on every path.","architect":"Chooses the collector topology, sampling policy, attribute and content-mode rules, and whether an LLM observability product sits beside or behind the OTel pipeline.","fde":"Points the exporter at the customer's collector, negotiates which attributes may leave their environment, and uses traces to show where latency and tokens go in their workflow."},"check":{"q":"Traces from your Node agent API show the HTTP server span and your manual spans, but no\nPostgres or Redis spans, and worker-side spans appear as separate traces. What is wrong and how do you\nfix both?","a":"Missing `pg` and `ioredis` spans almost always mean the SDK was initialised after those modules\nwere loaded, so the patches never applied; the fix is a separate instrumentation file loaded via\n`--require` or, for ESM, `--import` with the auto-instrumentation register hook, before any\napplication import. The worker traces are orphans because queue hops do not propagate context on their\nown: on the producer, call `propagation.inject(context.active(), carrier)` and store the carrier in the\njob data; in the worker, `propagation.extract` it and start the processing span inside that context so\nit becomes a child (or a linked span) of the API request. Verify with one request and confirm a single\ntrace spans API, database, Redis and worker."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the OpenTelemetry SDK be loaded before importing your web framework and database client?","options":["Because the SDK reserves port 4318 before the server starts","Auto-instrumentation patches modules when they are first loaded, so modules imported earlier are never wrapped","Because spans need a root span created at process start","Because the exporter needs the framework's config to resolve the endpoint"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Instrumentations hook the module loader; anything required before the hook is installed is the original, unpatched module, which is why the SDK lives in a file passed via `--require` or `--import`."},{"q":"How does the active span follow an `await` in Node when there is no thread to attach it to?","options":["Spans are stored in a global variable that the SDK resets per request","The event loop tags each callback with the span id","The SDK uses `AsyncLocalStorage` so context flows along the async continuation chain","Each promise carries the span in its prototype"],"answer":2,"explanation":"OTel's Node context manager stores the active context in `AsyncLocalStorage`, which Node propagates through promises, timers and callbacks, so code after an `await` still sees the right parent span."},{"q":"Which is the best default for recording model calls in spans?","options":["Store full prompt and completion text as attributes on every span","Record model, token usage, finish reason and tenant as attributes, and keep content out unless explicitly opted in","Record only the HTTP status, since the auto-instrumented span already exists","Skip spans for model calls and rely on provider dashboards"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Token and model attributes make cost and latency queryable at low volume; prompt text is large, sensitive and billed per byte, so it belongs behind an opt-in content mode or in a purpose-built LLM observability store."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","sources":["Official docs: Node.js (process, http)","Official docs: Kubernetes — Pod Lifecycle","Official docs: BullMQ"],"why":"- **Processes are killed constantly:** deploys, autoscaling, node drains, out-of-memory kills.\n  Kubernetes sends SIGTERM and, after `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (30 s by default), SIGKILL;\n  `docker stop` does the same with a 10 s default.\n- **Node without a handler does not drain.** SIGTERM either terminates it immediately or, when Node is\n  PID 1 in the container, is ignored outright (the kernel drops signals PID 1 has no handler for), so\n  the pod sits until SIGKILL.\n- **Every in-flight thing breaks.** An SSE stream halfway through a 2,000-token answer closes without a\n  final event; a BullMQ worker mid-way through a 500-chunk embedding loses its place and the job stalls\n  and re-runs; the server rolls back a Postgres transaction when the socket drops; the last batch of\n  OpenTelemetry spans, covering the deploy itself, is never exported.\n- **The naive fix is still wrong three ways.**\n  `process.on('SIGTERM', () => server.close(() => process.exit(0)))` refuses requests that still\n  arrive during endpoint propagation, can hang on idle keep-alive connections on older Node, and ignores\n  the worker, pool, Redis client and telemetry exporter.\n- **Graceful shutdown is choreography against a deadline:** each resource has its own flush and close\n  semantics and must close in the right order.","how":"### What the orchestrator does\n1. **Pod marked terminating** and removed from the Service's ready endpoints straight away, whatever its\n   readiness probe says. That removal propagates to kube-proxy rules and ingress controllers with a\n   delay of typically a few seconds, during which they still route to the pod.\n2. **`preStop` hook runs,** if any. A common one is `sleep 5` to let endpoint removal propagate before\n   the signal arrives. The exec form needs a `sleep` binary in the image; recent Kubernetes versions\n   also offer a built-in `sleep` preStop action. The hook's time counts against the grace period.\n3. **SIGTERM** goes to PID 1 of the container.\n4. **SIGKILL** after the grace period.\n\n- **The process must receive the signal.** Use the exec form `CMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]`; the shell\n  form `CMD npm start` leaves a shell as PID 1 that does not forward signals. `docker run --init` or\n  `tini` is the safe default.\n- **The app's deadline must be shorter than the grace period minus the `preStop` sleep,** *because*\n  otherwise SIGKILL wins and the work is lost anyway.\n\n### The sequence inside Node\n1. **Flip readiness, then keep serving briefly.** `/ready` returns 503 immediately so any balancer that\n   health-checks the pod directly stops routing. Keep `/live` healthy, *because* a failing liveness\n   probe during shutdown makes the orchestrator restart the container mid-drain.\n2. **Stop intake.** `server.close()` stops accepting connections; for BullMQ, `await worker.close()`\n   stops taking new jobs and waits for active ones (`worker.pause()` is the softer form). Cancel\n   scheduled timers and polling loops.\n3. **Drain in flight.** Requests complete naturally. Streaming responses need a product decision (see\n   below).\n4. **Close resources in reverse dependency order.** Queue and Redis clients first, then `pool.end()`,\n   then telemetry last.\n5. **Exit explicitly,** under a deadline timer.\n6. **Second signal means force.** A second SIGTERM or SIGINT during draining exits immediately;\n   operators expect it.\n\n### Closing the 502 window\n- **Readiness is not what removes the pod** in Kubernetes; termination already did.\n- **What closes the window** is continuing to accept requests for a few seconds while endpoint removal\n  propagates.\n- **Do it once:** a `preStop` sleep, or, if you cannot change the manifest, an in-app delay before\n  `server.close()`, but not both, since each costs grace period.\n\n### HTTP connections\n| Situation | Behaviour | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Node 19 and later | `close()` also closes idle keep-alive connections | nothing extra |\n| Older Node | idle keep-alive sockets keep `close()` waiting | call `server.closeIdleConnections()` |\n| Balancer keep-alive | long-lived idle sockets | keep `keepAliveTimeout` short so balancers reconnect elsewhere |\n| Open SSE streams | never idle, so `close()` waits for each to end | track open streams and end them deliberately |\n| Deadline near | something still open | `server.closeAllConnections()` as the last resort |\n\n### Streams and model calls\n- **Finish them** if they will end within the deadline.\n- **Otherwise send a final event** that tells the client to resume or retry.\n- **Abort the upstream model call** with an `AbortController`, so you stop paying for tokens nobody will\n  receive.\n\n### Close order and exit\n- **Redis:** `queue.close()` and `redis.quit()`, which waits for pending replies, unlike `disconnect()`.\n- **Postgres:** `pool.end()` must come after every worker and request that might still query.\n- **Telemetry last:** `sdk.shutdown()` flushes the batch span processor. The exporter depends on none of\n  the other resources, and flushing last also exports the spans and errors of the close steps. Give the\n  flush its own short timeout.\n- **Exit is your job.** Once a SIGTERM listener is registered, Node no longer exits on the signal by\n  default, so call `process.exit(0)` at the end (or set `process.exitCode` and let the loop drain).\n- **Deadline timer.** `setTimeout(..., 20_000).unref()` for a 30 s grace period with a 5 s `preStop`\n  exits non-zero if draining hangs, *because* a hung shutdown wastes the whole grace period and still\n  ends in SIGKILL.\n\n### Crashes\n- **`uncaughtException` and `unhandledRejection` leave the process in an unknown state.**\n- **Two acceptable responses:** log, then flush telemetry and exit non-zero within a second or two; or\n  run the normal drain under a short deadline (as the example does).\n- **Either way** stop accepting work and exit non-zero; never keep serving.\n- **Recovery is external.** The orchestrator starts a fresh instance and the queue's stall detection\n  re-runs the job, which is why jobs must be idempotent in the first place.\n\n### What \"done\" looks like\nDeploys produce zero 5xx at the load balancer, zero stalled jobs, no truncated streams in client logs,\nand the last trace before shutdown is present in the backend.","example":"### Shutdown orchestrator for an API plus BullMQ worker\nReadiness fails first, HTTP intake and the worker close in parallel, then Redis and Postgres close, and\ntelemetry is flushed last under its own 3-second cap.\n\nThe 20-second deadline leaves margin under a 30-second grace period minus a 5-second `preStop` sleep;\nthe timer is `unref()`'d so it cannot keep an otherwise-finished process alive, and a second signal\nforces exit.\n\n```ts\nimport http from \"node:http\";\nimport { Pool } from \"pg\";\nimport { Worker } from \"bullmq\";\nimport { sdk } from \"./instrumentation\";          // the OpenTelemetry NodeSDK instance\nimport { redis } from \"./redis\";                  // an ioredis client\n\nlet ready = true;\nconst pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\n  if (req.url === \"/ready\") { res.writeHead(ready ? 200 : 503); return res.end(); }\n  if (req.url === \"/live\")  { res.writeHead(200); return res.end(); }\n  handle(req, res);                                 // normal routing\n});\nserver.keepAliveTimeout = 5_000;\nconst workerConn = redis.duplicate({ maxRetriesPerRequest: null });   // BullMQ workers require null\nconst worker = new Worker(\"embed\", processEmbed, { connection: workerConn, concurrency: 4 });\nserver.listen(8080);\n\nlet shuttingDown = false;\nasync function shutdown(signal: string, code = 0) {\n  if (shuttingDown) { console.warn(\"second signal, forcing exit\"); process.exit(1); }\n  shuttingDown = true; ready = false;               // 1. fail readiness first\n  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, Number(process.env.DRAIN_DELAY_MS ?? 0)));  // keep serving while endpoints update; 0 if preStop sleeps\n  const deadline = setTimeout(() => { console.error(\"drain timeout\"); process.exit(1); }, 20_000).unref();\n  console.log(`${signal}: draining`);\n  try {\n    await Promise.all([\n      new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.close(() => resolve())),  // 2. stop intake (closes idle keep-alive on Node 19+)\n      worker.close(),                                                   //    stop taking jobs, wait for active ones\n    ]);\n    await Promise.all([redis.quit(), workerConn.quit()]);              // 3. waits for pending replies\n    await pool.end();                                                   //    only after nothing can still query\n    await Promise.race([sdk.shutdown(), new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 3_000))]);  // 4. flush spans last, capped\n    clearTimeout(deadline);\n    process.exit(code);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error(\"shutdown error\", err); process.exit(1);\n  }\n}\nprocess.on(\"SIGTERM\", () => void shutdown(\"SIGTERM\"));\nprocess.on(\"SIGINT\",  () => void shutdown(\"SIGINT\"));\nprocess.on(\"unhandledRejection\", (e) => { console.error(e); void shutdown(\"unhandledRejection\", 1); });\n```\n\n### Production snippet: the container and manifest side\nThe code above only works if the signal reaches Node and the timings line up. Exec-form `CMD` behind\n`tini` makes Node receive SIGTERM; the manifest sets the grace period, the `preStop` sleep and separate\nprobes.\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-slim\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends tini && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\nENTRYPOINT [\"/usr/bin/tini\", \"--\"]\nCMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]\n```\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30\n  containers:\n    - name: agent-api\n      image: registry.example.com/agent-api:1.4.2\n      lifecycle:\n        preStop:\n          exec:\n            command: [\"sleep\", \"5\"]      # covers endpoint propagation; set DRAIN_DELAY_MS to 0\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /ready, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 2\n      livenessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /live, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 10\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: budgeting a 30-second grace period\nHow the numbers in the example fit together, measured from the moment the pod is marked terminating.\n\n| Time | Event | Budget used |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 s | endpoints removal starts propagating; `preStop` sleep begins | 0 of 30 s |\n| 5 s | SIGTERM; readiness 503; deadline timer set for 20 s | 5 s |\n| 5-22 s | `server.close()` and `worker.close()` drain; a 4-job worker finishes its active jobs | up to 22 s |\n| 22-25 s | `redis.quit()`, `pool.end()`, `sdk.shutdown()` capped at 3 s | up to 25 s |\n| 25 s | latest point the deadline timer forces `process.exit(1)` | 25 s |\n| 30 s | SIGKILL, never reached if the above held | 30 s |\n\nIf a worker's jobs routinely take longer than about 15 seconds, raise `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`\nfor the worker deployment and the deadline with it, or add per-chunk checkpoints so a cut-off job\nresumes cheaply.","patterns":["**Zero-error rollouts as a test.** Run a steady load against a staging deployment while triggering a rollout restart, and fail the check if the load balancer logs any 5xx or connection resets. Monitor 5xx and stalled-job counts per rollout in production too.","**Grace period per workload.** Keep API pods near the default 30 s, and give worker deployments a `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` sized to the longest acceptable job plus margin, with the in-app deadline derived from it. Monitor stalled jobs and drain-timeout exits after deploys.","**Stream handoff on shutdown.** When draining, send a final SSE event telling the client to reconnect (with a resume token or the run id) and abort the upstream model call with its `AbortController`. Monitor aborted streams and client reconnect success.","**One structured log line per shutdown step.** Log each step with its duration and alert on `drain timeout` or non-zero exit codes, so a slow close (often the telemetry flush or a pool still in use) is visible. Monitor the distribution of total shutdown time.","**PodDisruptionBudget for voluntary evictions.** For node drains and cluster upgrades, a PodDisruptionBudget (`minAvailable` or `maxUnavailable`) limits how many replicas drain at once, so graceful shutdown never takes out the whole fleet. Monitor evictions blocked by the budget during upgrades."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant K as Kubernetes\n    participant P as Proxies and ingress\n    participant N as Node process\n    participant D as Redis, Postgres, OTel\n    K->>P: remove pod from endpoints, propagates over seconds\n    K->>N: preStop sleep 5\n    Note over N: still serving requests routed during propagation\n    K->>N: SIGTERM\n    Note over N: readiness 503, liveness stays 200\n    N->>N: server.close and worker.close, drain in flight\n    N->>D: redis.quit, then pool.end\n    N->>D: sdk.shutdown flushes spans, capped\n    N->>K: process.exit before the deadline\n    K-->>N: SIGKILL only if the grace period expires","caption":"The SIGTERM-to-SIGKILL timeline: the `preStop` sleep covers endpoint propagation, and everything inside\nNode must finish before the grace period ends."},"interview":[{"q":"What happens between Kubernetes deciding to stop a pod and the process disappearing?","a":"- **The pod is marked terminating** and removed from endpoints at once, but proxies learn about it over\n  a few seconds.\n- **The `preStop` hook runs,** then SIGTERM reaches PID 1, and SIGKILL follows after\n  `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (30 s by default).\n- **So the app must** keep serving through propagation, drain, close and exit before the grace period\n  ends."},{"q":"Your containers always take exactly 30 seconds to stop and never log a shutdown line. What is wrong?","a":"- **The signal never reaches Node:** a shell-form `CMD npm start` or wrapper script is PID 1 and does not\n  forward SIGTERM, or Node is PID 1 with no handler and the kernel ignores the signal.\n- **Fix:** exec-form `CMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]` behind `tini` or `docker run --init`, plus an\n  explicit SIGTERM handler.\n- **Verify** by sending SIGTERM locally and checking the log line and exit code."},{"q":"Why must the Postgres pool close after the HTTP server and worker, and telemetry after everything?","a":"- **The pool must outlive every request and job** that may still query; ending it first produces \"pool\n  after end\" errors in the last jobs.\n- **Telemetry goes last** because the exporter depends on nothing else, and flushing last also exports\n  the spans and errors of the close steps.\n- **Each step still needs a cap** so one slow close cannot consume the whole deadline."},{"q":"When is a longer grace period the wrong fix for jobs that get cut off during deploys?","a":"- **When jobs are long by nature:** with `concurrency: 10` and ten-minute jobs, no sensible grace\n  period lets every active job finish.\n- **Better:** per-chunk checkpoints and idempotent writes, so a job re-run after a stall resumes where it\n  left off.\n- **Keep the grace period** sized to a reasonable unit of work, and treat re-runs as normal."},{"q":"How do you size the in-app deadline for a 30-second grace period with a 5-second `preStop` sleep?","a":"- **Subtract the hook:** only about 25 seconds remain after SIGTERM, and you want margin before SIGKILL.\n- **So pick about 20 seconds** for the whole drain, with sub-caps such as 3 seconds for the telemetry\n  flush, and exit non-zero when it fires.\n- **Then verify** with shutdown logs: if drains routinely approach the deadline, raise the grace period\n  or shorten units of work."}],"tradeoffs":["**No lost work vs rollout speed.** Draining in-flight work avoids lost requests and duplicate jobs, but every second of drain delays the rollout; with long LLM streams the deadline is a product decision (finish, or cut with a resume token), not just a timeout value.","**No 502s vs grace period.** Keeping the listener open for a few seconds after SIGTERM (a `preStop` sleep or an in-app delay) removes propagation-window 502s, but those seconds come out of the grace period and slow every rollout. Size the delay from how long your ingress actually takes to drop an endpoint, and drop it where the balancer deregisters targets before the signal arrives.","**Guaranteed exit vs cut work.** A hard internal deadline guarantees exit before SIGKILL with a non-zero code, but cuts whatever is still running; keep it below the grace period and raise the grace period for worker pods rather than removing the deadline.","**Graceful workers vs long jobs.** Closing BullMQ workers gracefully prevents stalls on every deploy, but with `concurrency: 10` and ten-minute jobs no sensible grace period suffices; the fix is per-chunk checkpoints so a re-run resumes, not a longer wait.","**Telemetry completeness vs deadline.** Flushing on shutdown preserves the most interesting spans, but a slow or unreachable collector can eat the whole deadline; give the flush its own short timeout."],"pitfalls":["**Shell-form `CMD npm start` or a wrapper script as PID 1.** Symptom: containers always take the full grace period and die by SIGKILL, with no shutdown log line ever appearing.","**A SIGTERM handler that never calls `process.exit`.** Symptom: the process lingers after draining because an open handle (a timer, a Redis socket) keeps the event loop alive.","**`server.close()` on Node 18 or older without `closeIdleConnections()`.** Symptom: shutdown hangs until SIGKILL while the balancer holds idle keep-alive sockets.","**Liveness probe wired to the same flag as readiness.** Symptom: the orchestrator restarts the container in the middle of its own drain.","**Closing the Postgres pool before the worker finishes.** Symptom: \"Cannot use a pool after calling end on the pool\" errors in the last jobs of every deploy.","**Deadline timer not `unref()`'d.** Symptom: a process that finished draining but waits the full timeout before exiting."],"when":"- **Use in every long-running Node process:** API servers, BullMQ workers, MCP servers, schedulers.\n- **Do it on day one of containerising,** because the symptoms (5xx spikes, duplicate jobs, truncated\n  streams, missing traces) are easy to blame on everything else.\n- **Set the deadline** from the orchestrator's grace period and the longest acceptable in-flight unit;\n  raise `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` for worker deployments with long jobs, and add checkpoints\n  where that is not enough.\n- **Measure:** 5xx and connection errors during rollouts, stalled job counts, and whether shutdown logs\n  show the sequence completing.\n- **Interviews:** walk through the SIGTERM-to-SIGKILL timeline and the order of readiness, intake,\n  drain, flush, close, exit.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the shutdown orchestrator, wires readiness to it, closes the worker, telemetry, Redis and pool in order, and tests it by sending SIGTERM to a loaded local process.","architect":"Sets grace periods, `preStop` policy and the rule for in-flight streams across services, and makes idempotent, checkpointed jobs a requirement so shutdown can afford to be strict.","fde":"Verifies the customer's cluster settings (grace period, probe intervals, PID 1 handling in their base image) and demonstrates a deploy with zero dropped requests in their environment."},"check":{"q":"After every deploy your Node API shows a burst of 502s at the load balancer and BullMQ reports a\nhandful of stalled jobs, even though you added `process.on('SIGTERM', () => server.close(() =>\nprocess.exit(0)))`. Explain both symptoms and the full fix.","a":"The 502s come from the window between SIGTERM and the balancer learning the pod is gone: the\nserver stopped accepting instantly while proxies were still routing to it. Kubernetes has already\nremoved the pod from the endpoints, so the fix is to keep serving for a few seconds while that\npropagates (a short `preStop` sleep, or an in-app delay before `server.close()`); fail readiness too\nfor any balancer that probes the pod directly, and keep the liveness probe passing.\nThe stalled jobs come from the worker never being closed: `server.close` only drains HTTP, so\n`process.exit` killed active jobs whose locks then expired and were re-run by the stall checker. The\nfull sequence is: readiness to 503, a short delay, `server.close()` (closing idle keep-alive connections) in parallel\nwith `await worker.close()`, `redis.quit()`, `pool.end()`, flush OpenTelemetry, then `process.exit(0)`,\nall under a deadline below the grace period, with a non-zero exit if the deadline fires. Verify the\nsignal reaches Node at all: exec-form `CMD` so Node (or a forwarding init such as `tini`) is PID 1,\nnot a shell."},"quiz":[{"q":"A Kubernetes pod calls `server.close()` the instant it receives SIGTERM and the load balancer logs a burst of 502s on every deploy. What removes the burst?","options":["Making `server.close()` asynchronous so it does not block the event loop","Continuing to serve for a few seconds, via a `preStop` sleep or an in-app delay, while endpoint removal propagates to the proxies","Failing the readiness probe first, because Kubernetes only removes a pod from endpoints after readiness fails","Calling `process.exit(0)` immediately so the pod disappears from endpoints faster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A terminating pod is removed from endpoints regardless of readiness, but proxies and ingress learn that asynchronously, so traffic keeps arriving for a few seconds; staying up through that window is the fix. Failing readiness helps only balancers that probe the pod directly."},{"q":"Once you register a `process.on('SIGTERM', ...)` handler in Node, what changes?","options":["Node forwards the signal to child processes automatically","The grace period is extended by the orchestrator","The default exit on SIGTERM is disabled, so the process keeps running until you exit explicitly or the event loop empties","Subsequent SIGTERMs are ignored"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Installing a signal listener replaces the default behaviour; if the handler drains but never calls `process.exit` and some handle keeps the loop alive, the process lingers until SIGKILL."},{"q":"After readiness has been flipped to 503, which shutdown order is right?","options":["End the Postgres pool so no new writes can start, then close the worker, then flush telemetry, then exit","Close the HTTP server and the worker, then Redis and the Postgres pool, then flush telemetry, then exit","Flush telemetry, then exit and let the orchestrator close the remaining sockets","Close the worker, then exit, because the stall checker will finish any remaining jobs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pool must outlive every request and job that may still query, so it closes only after the server and worker have drained; ending it first produces \"pool after end\" errors in the last jobs. Telemetry goes last, under a short cap, so the close steps' own spans are exported before exit."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","sources":["Official docs: Vitest","Official docs: Jest","Official docs: Mock Service Worker (msw)","Official docs: Testcontainers for Node"],"why":"- **The model call breaks every test assumption.** It takes seconds, costs money per token, needs a\n  secret, and returns different text for the same input. A suite that hits it is too slow for every push,\n  too expensive to parallelise, leaks a production key into CI logs, and fails whenever the provider is\n  degraded or rephrases. Teams that start this way end up with `it.skip` everywhere and no safety net.\n- **\"Mock everything\" fails more quietly.** A `vi.mock(\"@anthropic-ai/sdk\")` returning a canned object\n  tests only your own branches. The bugs that bite (a renamed field, a tool schema the provider rejects, a\n  stream event type you forgot) live in the serialization and parsing layer that a module mock skips.\n- **In-memory databases lie the same way.** They do not enforce unique constraints, JSON column semantics\n  or transaction isolation, so the test passes and production throws.\n- **The skill is separating assert from measure.** Logic around the model (prompt assembly, retries,\n  parsing, tool dispatch, persistence) is deterministic once the model is faked, so assert it. Output\n  quality is statistical, so measure it in a scheduled eval with a threshold, not a merge gate. The runner\n  is secondary.","how":"### The runners\nVitest and Jest share `describe` / `it` / `expect`, the `beforeAll` / `afterEach` hooks, module mocking,\nfake timers, snapshots and coverage, deliberately: Vitest was built to be largely Jest-compatible. They\ndiffer in how code reaches the test.\n\n| | Jest | Vitest |\n|---|---|---|\n| TypeScript | Transform you configure: `ts-jest`, `babel-jest` or an swc plugin | Vite's transformer (esbuild, or Oxc in recent Vite) strips types without type-checking, so run `tsc --noEmit` separately |\n| ESM | Long behind an experimental flag, so projects accumulate `moduleNameMapper` and `transformIgnorePatterns` | Default; `vite.config.ts` aliases apply to tests too |\n| Workers | Child processes | `pool: \"forks\"` (child processes, default since v2) or `\"threads\"` (lighter, cannot use `process.chdir`, fragile with some native addons) |\n| Serialise files | `--runInBand` | `fileParallelism: false` |\n| Watch | Re-runs related tests | Re-runs only files whose import graph changed |\n\n- **Default choice.** For a TypeScript-first AI service started today, Vitest. Jest stays right when the\n  repo already runs on it, because migrating a large suite buys little.\n- **Isolated module registry per file** (Vitest's default `isolate: true`), *because* module state (a\n  cached client, a memoised config) would otherwise leak between files.\n- **Parallelism is per file, not per test.** Tests inside one file run sequentially unless marked\n  concurrent.\n- **Mocks are hoisted.** `vi.mock` / `jest.mock` calls are moved above imports by the transform, because\n  ESM imports resolve before any statement runs, so a mock in normal source order would arrive too late.\n- **Native ESM Jest is different.** Jest's hoisting is a Babel/ts-jest transform for CommonJS output;\n  under native ESM you need `jest.unstable_mockModule` plus a dynamic `import()` instead.\n\n### Three kinds of double for the model\n1. **An injected fake.** Define the narrowest interface your code needs (`complete(prompt): Promise<string>`,\n   or a stream of events) and pass an implementation in rather than importing the SDK at module scope.\n   Tests hand in a function returning scripted answers, including malformed ones, in a known order. It\n   tests parsing, repair loops, budgets and persistence, in microseconds.\n2. **HTTP interception.** Construct the real SDK and intercept at the network layer with `msw`\n   (`setupServer` from `msw/node`), `nock`, or undici's `MockAgent`.\n   - The request body you assert on is the exact JSON the provider would receive, and the response is\n     parsed by real SDK code. This catches a wrong field name or a stream handler that never fires.\n   - Set `maxRetries: 0` on the client, so a deliberate 500 does not trigger the SDK's default two retries\n     (three attempts) with backoff.\n   - Streams: the handler returns `new HttpResponse(body, { headers: { \"content-type\": \"text/event-stream\" } })`,\n     where `body` is a `ReadableStream` emitting recorded `event: …\\ndata: {…}\\n\\n` frames, so the SDK's\n     real SSE parser runs.\n   - Splitting a frame across two chunks, or ending before `message_stop`, tests the reassembly and\n     early-termination paths that hand-written fakes never exercise.\n3. **Recorded fixtures.** Capture a handful of real responses once (including tool-call turns and a full\n   stream transcript), store them as JSON, and replay them through the interceptor. They are the only\n   cheap way to test the provider's real response shape, and to notice that an SDK upgrade changed a type.\n   Refresh them deliberately, not on every run.\n\n**Each layer catches what the one below cannot see.** The fake cannot see serialization bugs; the\ninterceptor cannot see output-quality drift; evals cannot see a bug in your retry logic because they are\ntoo noisy to attribute a failure to it.\n\n### Integration tests with real dependencies\n- **Keep Postgres, Redis and the queue real**, *because* the behaviours you depend on (constraint\n  violations, `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`, JSONB queries, Redis expiry, BullMQ retries) are what fakes get wrong.\n- **Two ways to start them.** A `globalSetup` that starts containers with Testcontainers and exports\n  connection strings, or a service started by CI (a GitHub Actions `services:` block, a compose file locally).\n- **Design isolation, since the runner is parallel.** Pick one:\n  - each worker gets its own database or schema, named from `VITEST_POOL_ID` / `JEST_WORKER_ID`;\n  - every test runs inside a transaction rolled back in `afterEach`;\n  - truncate between tests and run those files serially.\n- **The rollback trick's precondition.** The code under test must run on the *same connection* as the\n  test's transaction. If it takes its own pooled client, it neither sees the uncommitted fixtures nor gets\n  rolled back. Inject the transaction-bound client (dependency injection again), and map the code's own\n  transactions to savepoints.\n- **Shared mutable state across workers** is the most common source of flaky integration suites.\n\n### Async, streams, time and cancellation\n- **Fakes must be async too.** An `async function*` is a perfect fake stream; a fake that waits on a\n  `signal` lets you assert that aborting the request stops iteration.\n- **Backoff runs under fake timers.** `vi.useFakeTimers()` plus `vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync` makes a\n  30-second backoff test finish in milliseconds. Forgetting to await the async advance is the usual reason\n  a retry test hangs.\n- **Eventually consistent assertions.** `expect.poll` and `vi.waitFor` cover assertions against a queue.\n\n### Snapshots: prompts yes, completions no\n- **Snapshot the rendered prompt.** Inline snapshots of system text plus serialized tool definitions are\n  cheap regression tests: a prompt edit shows up as a diff in review, not a silent behaviour change.\n- **Never snapshot model output.** It fails every run or gets updated blindly with `-u`, which trains\n  people to ignore it.\n\n### Where the real model lives\n- **A separate suite**, selected by tag or directory, run on a schedule or on demand with a budget.\n- **Scored against a threshold** (pass rate ≥ 0.9 on a golden set, judge score above a floor).\n- **It publishes a metric**; it does not block a merge on one bad sample. The evals module covers how to\n  build the set and harness.","example":"### Fake model plus real SDK over msw\nA `summarize` service depends on a narrow `ModelClient` interface,\n`{ complete(prompt: string): Promise<string> }`.\n- It calls `complete`, tries to parse `{\"bullets\": string[]}`, and on failure calls once more with a\n  repair prompt that quotes the bad output.\n- `anthropicAdapter(client)` implements `complete` with `client.messages.create` and joins the text blocks.\n- Test one drives the repair loop with a scripted fake. Test two constructs the real Anthropic SDK and\n  intercepts the HTTP call with `msw`, so the request body and response parsing are real.\n- **The handler *captures* the body; the assertion runs in the test.** An `expect` thrown inside an msw\n  handler becomes a 500, which surfaces as the SDK's `InternalServerError`, not the assertion message.\n\n```ts\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeAll, afterAll, afterEach } from \"vitest\";\nimport { http, HttpResponse } from \"msw\";\nimport { setupServer } from \"msw/node\";\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\nimport { summarize, anthropicAdapter } from \"../src/summarize\";\n\ndescribe(\"summarize (unit, injected fake)\", () => {\n  it(\"feeds a malformed answer back once, then returns parsed bullets\", async () => {\n    const complete = vi.fn()\n      .mockResolvedValueOnce(\"not json\")\n      .mockResolvedValueOnce('{\"bullets\":[\"a\",\"b\"]}');\n    const out = await summarize(\"long text\", { complete });\n    expect(out.bullets).toEqual([\"a\", \"b\"]);\n    expect(complete).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);\n    expect(complete.mock.calls[1][0]).toContain(\"not json\");   // repair prompt quotes the bad output\n  });\n});\n\nlet sent: { max_tokens: number; messages: unknown[] } | undefined;   // the exact JSON on the wire\nconst server = setupServer(\n  http.post(\"https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages\", async ({ request }) => {\n    sent = (await request.json()) as typeof sent;\n    return HttpResponse.json({\n      id: \"msg_test\", type: \"message\", role: \"assistant\", model: \"test\",\n      content: [{ type: \"text\", text: '{\"bullets\":[\"x\"]}' }],\n      stop_reason: \"end_turn\", usage: { input_tokens: 12, output_tokens: 6 },\n    });\n  }),\n);\nbeforeAll(() => server.listen({ onUnhandledRequest: \"error\" }));  // any other network call fails loudly\nafterEach(() => { server.resetHandlers(); sent = undefined; });\nafterAll(() => server.close());\n\ndescribe(\"summarize (integration, real SDK over intercepted HTTP)\", () => {\n  it(\"builds a request the API accepts and parses the response\", async () => {\n    const client = new Anthropic({ apiKey: \"test-key\", maxRetries: 0 });\n    const out = await summarize(\"long text\", anthropicAdapter(client));\n    expect(out.bullets).toEqual([\"x\"]);\n    expect(sent?.max_tokens).toBeGreaterThan(0);    // required field present in the real request\n    expect(sent?.messages).toHaveLength(1);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### Backoff under fake timers\nA self-contained retry helper that sleeps 1 s, then 2 s. Under fake timers the test runs in\nmilliseconds; each `advanceTimersByTimeAsync` is awaited so the promise chain between timers settles.\n\n```ts\nimport { it, expect, vi, afterEach } from \"vitest\";\n\nasync function withRetry<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, attempts = 3, baseMs = 1000): Promise<T> {\n  for (let n = 0; ; n++) {\n    try { return await fn(); }\n    catch (e) {\n      if (n + 1 >= attempts) throw e;\n      await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, baseMs * 2 ** n));   // 1000 ms, then 2000 ms\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nafterEach(() => { vi.useRealTimers(); });\n\nit(\"retries twice with exponential backoff and no real waiting\", async () => {\n  vi.useFakeTimers();\n  const fn = vi.fn()\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error(\"429\"))\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error(\"429\"))\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce(\"ok\");\n  const p = withRetry(fn);\n  await vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(1000);\n  expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);\n  await vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(2000);\n  await expect(p).resolves.toBe(\"ok\");\n  expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\n});\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: restructuring a suite of twenty real-model tests\nIllustrative orders of magnitude for the Check yourself situation, not a benchmark.\n\n| Layer after the split | Tests | What it asserts | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit, injected fake | 20 rewritten | parsing, retries, tool dispatch, persistence | every push, seconds |\n| Interception, real SDK + msw | 1 per provider adapter | request body, response and stream parsing | every push |\n| Integration, Testcontainers | per repository module | constraints, `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`, expiry | every push, per-worker schema |\n| Eval, real model | golden set | pass rate ≥ 0.9 | nightly, with a budget |\n\n- **Before:** each PR paid twenty model calls of seconds each, plus random red builds when the provider\n  was degraded.\n- **After:** the merge gate makes zero model calls and needs no provider key; quality is a tracked number.","patterns":["**Fail closed on network.** Whenever the suite uses msw, call `server.listen({ onUnhandledRequest: \"error\" })` so an unmatched URL fails the test instead of reaching the real provider. Pair it with CI jobs that have no provider key at all, and monitor for any test that needs one.","**Per-worker schema.** When integration files run in parallel, derive a schema name such as `` `test_${process.env.VITEST_POOL_ID}` `` (or `JEST_WORKER_ID`) in a setup file and run migrations into it once per worker. Monitor flake rate per file; tests passing alone but failing together mean shared state.","**Separate type-check job.** Because Vitest strips types without checking them, run `tsc --noEmit` as its own CI step next to the test run, so a type error cannot pass the suite. Watch that step's duration as the repo grows.","**Fixture refresh on SDK upgrade.** When a dependency bot bumps `@anthropic-ai/sdk` or `openai`, re-record the fixture set with a dedicated script and review the JSON diff in the same PR. Monitor the age of the fixtures against the installed SDK version.","**Split suites by config.** Keep the scored real-model suite in its own config or directory and run it from a scheduled CI workflow with `vitest run --config <eval config>`, never from the PR workflow. Track its pass rate and spend per run as metrics with an owner."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Code change\"] --> U[\"Unit: injected fake model\"]\n    U --> I[\"Integration: real SDK via msw\"]\n    I --> D[(\"Real Postgres and Redis\")]\n    D --> G{\"All asserted tests green?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| M[\"Merge\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| F[\"Fix before merge\"]\n    M -.->|\"nightly, with budget\"| E[\"Eval: real model, scored\"]\n    E --> T{\"Pass rate at or above 0.9?\"}\n    T -->|\"no\"| A[\"Alert owner, investigate drift\"]\n    T -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Publish metric\"]","caption":"Each change flows through the fast deterministic layers on every PR; the real model is only touched by\nthe scheduled, scored suite."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is `vi.mock` of the provider SDK not enough to test a model integration?","a":"- **Definition:** a module mock replaces the SDK with a canned object, so only your own branches run.\n- **Why it falls short:** serialization and parsing are skipped, so a renamed field, an invalid tool schema\n  or an unhandled stream event type passes the test and fails in production with a 400.\n- **Better:** keep the real SDK and intercept HTTP with msw, asserting the captured body in the test."},{"q":"Your integration suite passes file by file but fails in the full parallel run. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect shared state first:** parallel workers writing to one database cause ordering-dependent\n  unique-constraint and \"row not found\" errors.\n- **Confirm:** rerun with `fileParallelism: false` (or `--runInBand` in Jest); if it goes green, isolation is the bug.\n- **Fix:** a schema per worker from `VITEST_POOL_ID`, or rollback per test with the transaction client injected."},{"q":"When would you keep Jest instead of moving to Vitest?","a":"- **Keep Jest** when a large suite already runs on it, because migration buys little and Jest's ecosystem\n  is mature.\n- **Choose Vitest** for a new TypeScript-first service: ESM by default, Vite aliases, little config.\n- **Either way** run `tsc --noEmit` separately if the transform strips types without checking them."},{"q":"How would you test that cancelling a request stops a streamed model response?","a":"- **Fake stream:** an `async function*` that checks an `AbortSignal` before each yield, injected behind the\n  model interface.\n- **Assert:** abort mid-stream and check iteration stopped and no further chunks were persisted.\n- **Wire level:** with msw, return a `ReadableStream` that ends before `message_stop` to test the SDK path."},{"q":"How do you keep real-model quality in check without making merges flaky?","a":"- **Separate scored suite** on a golden set, run nightly or on demand with a spend budget.\n- **Threshold, not assertion:** for example pass rate ≥ 0.9 or a judge score floor, published as a metric.\n- **Ownership:** someone owns the threshold and the alert, or regressions go unnoticed."}],"tradeoffs":["**Injected fake: speed vs drift.** Fast and deterministic, but it can drift from the real client's behaviour. The interception layer keeps it honest, so you need both, not one.","**Real containers: fidelity vs startup cost.** They catch database semantics fakes miss, at the cost of slower startup and CI complexity. An in-memory repository is acceptable only while the code relies on no constraint, isolation or JSON semantics, and stops being acceptable the day it does.","**Recorded fixtures: pinned shape vs staleness.** Pinning the provider's response shape is the point, but fixtures go stale when the provider adds fields. Refresh on SDK upgrades rather than letting them rot or auto-updating.","**Vitest vs Jest: setup speed vs maturity.** Vitest's Vite pipeline gives fast TS/ESM with little config; Jest's maturity and ecosystem win in large existing suites. Switching an established Jest repo rarely pays for itself.","**Real model out of the merge gate: velocity vs blind spots.** It protects velocity only if the scheduled eval actually runs and someone owns its threshold; otherwise quality regressions go unnoticed."],"pitfalls":["**Calling the real model in the PR suite.** Symptom: intermittent red builds, a growing list of `it.skip`, and a provider key visible in CI logs. Fix: inject a fake and move real calls to the eval suite.","**Mocking the SDK module instead of the network.** Symptom: a green suite and a 400 from the provider in production because a field was renamed or a tool schema was invalid. Fix: intercept HTTP with msw.","**Sharing one database between parallel workers.** Symptom: tests pass alone and fail in the full run, with unique-constraint or \"row not found\" errors that depend on ordering. Fix: per-worker schemas.","**Testing backoff with real timers.** Symptom: a retry test takes a minute or hits the default test timeout, which then gets raised instead of fixed. Fix: fake timers with awaited async advances.","**Forgetting `maxRetries: 0` on the client under test.** Symptom: a test of a 500 response is slowed by real backoff sleeps and sees three requests where it expected one.","**Snapshotting model text.** Symptom: the snapshot file is updated with `-u` on every change, so the test no longer tests anything. Fix: snapshot the prompt, score the output.","**Rollback isolation while the code takes its own pooled connection.** Symptom: \"row not found\" for fixtures the test just inserted, and rows that survive between tests. Fix: inject the transaction client.","**Leaving `onUnhandledRequest` at its default (`\"warn\"`).** Symptom: a test hit the real network because a URL did not match the handler, with only a warning buried in the output."],"when":"- **Injected fake:** whenever logic surrounds the model: prompt assembly, parsing, repair loops, budgets,\n  tool dispatch, persistence.\n- **Interception layer:** for each provider adapter and any stream-handling code, since that is where\n  wire-format bugs live.\n- **Real dependencies:** in integration tests for anything touching SQL, Redis or a queue; design worker\n  isolation before the suite grows.\n- **Real model:** a separate scored suite with a budget.\n- **Measure:** suite wall-clock time, flake rate, and how many bugs reach production in the serialization\n  layer; those three numbers tell you which layer is missing.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how do you test non-deterministic code\" and \"what can a fake not catch\"; the\n  layered answer, with the merge-gate versus eval distinction, is what they listen for.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `ModelClient` interface, the fakes and the msw handlers, keeps the unit suite under a few seconds, and owns the Testcontainers setup for Postgres and Redis.","architect":"Decides the boundary between asserted tests and scored evals, which dependencies must be real in CI, and the budget and cadence of the real-model suite.","fde":"Builds a replayable fixture set from the customer's real traffic so their team can change prompts and upgrade SDKs with a safety net, without spending their model budget on CI."},"check":{"q":"Your team's suite calls the real model in twenty tests. It is slow, flaky and costs money, but\nthe lead argues that mocks would make the tests meaningless. How do you restructure it, and what does\neach layer guarantee?","a":"Split by what each layer can observe. Inject the model behind a narrow interface and rewrite the\ntwenty tests as unit tests with scripted fakes; they now assert parsing, retries, tool dispatch and\npersistence deterministically. Add one interception test per provider adapter using the real SDK over\nmsw, asserting the request body and feeding back recorded responses; this catches the wire-format bugs\nthe lead is worried about. Keep real dependencies (Postgres, Redis) in integration tests with\nper-worker isolation. Move the real-model cases into a scored eval with a golden set and a pass-rate\nthreshold, run on a schedule with a budget, so quality drift is measured rather than asserted. The\nmerge gate becomes fast and deterministic without pretending the model is deterministic."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does an HTTP-interception test with the real SDK catch that an injected fake model cannot?","options":["Non-deterministic differences in the model's wording","Errors in the request the SDK actually sends and in parsing the real response shape","Database constraint violations in the persistence layer","Slow backoff logic that should run under fake timers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The fake replaces everything below your interface, so a renamed field or an invalid tool schema never reaches a test. Running the real SDK against intercepted HTTP exercises serialization and parsing while remaining deterministic. Model wording belongs to evals, not either test layer."},{"q":"Why are `vi.mock` and `jest.mock` hoisted above the import statements in a test file?","options":["To make the mock apply to every file in the worker pool","Because mocks must be registered before the test runner starts its timers","Because ES module imports resolve before any statement runs, so a mock in source order would register too late","To allow the mocked module to be type-checked by TypeScript"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Imports are evaluated before the module body executes. If the mock registration ran in normal order, the module under test would already hold the real dependency. Hoisting the mock call fixes the ordering; it does not change scope across files or affect type checking."},{"q":"Where should tests that call the real model live, and how should they report?","options":["In the PR suite, retried three times to absorb non-determinism","In the PR suite, with snapshots of the model's text updated when they change","In a separate scheduled, budgeted suite that scores results against a threshold rather than asserting each case","Nowhere; faked tests plus interception tests are sufficient"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Model output is statistical, so a single failing case is not a regression and a single passing one is not proof. A scored suite with a golden set and a pass-rate floor measures quality drift without making every merge depend on provider latency, cost and randomness."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","sources":["Official docs: Docker — Dockerfile reference and multi-stage builds","Official docs: Node.js — Docker and signal handling","Official docs: GitHub Actions"],"why":"- **TypeScript does not run as written.** It must be compiled (or type-stripped), its dependency tree is\n  large and mostly build-time only, and `node_modules` can hold native addons compiled for one libc.\n  Running `npm install` on the server may ignore the lockfile, land `devDependencies` in production, use\n  whatever `tsc` the host has, and add hundreds of megabytes.\n- **Docker makes the tested artifact the running artifact.** The Node-specific work is arranging the\n  build so that is actually true. The general Docker and CI/CD lessons live in backend-fundamentals.\n- **AI requests are long.** A streamed completion holds a connection for seconds to minutes, so a deploy\n  that kills the process on `SIGTERM` without draining cuts users off mid-answer and wastes tokens already\n  billed.\n- **The process holds the company's most sensitive secret.** A provider API key baked into an image layer\n  is visible to anyone who can pull the image.\n- **\"The code\" includes prompts, tool schemas and model identifiers.** Changing them can regress quality\n  with no test turning red. \"Build on push, deploy on green\" is incomplete: without reproducible installs it\n  does not build what you tested, without a drain it does not deploy safely, and without an eval it ships\n  prompt changes blind.","how":"### The image: stages because the runtime needs almost nothing\n1. **Deps stage (the cached layer):** copy only `package.json` and the lockfile, then run `npm ci` (`pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`,\n   `yarn install --immutable`).\n   - `ci` refuses to proceed if lockfile and manifest disagree, exactly the guarantee a build wants.\n   - Copying manifests before source caches this expensive layer until a dependency changes, not on every\n     commit.\n2. **Build stage.** Add the source, run `tsc` (or a bundler such as esbuild), then prune dev dependencies.\n3. **Runtime stage.** Start from a fresh base image and copy only `dist/`, the pruned `node_modules` and\n   `package.json`. Smaller, smaller attack surface, and it cannot accidentally depend on a dev tool.\n\n| Choice | Guidance | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| `node:<major>-slim` | Safe default | Debian-based, glibc |\n| `node:<major>-alpine` | Only after testing native deps | Smaller, but musl breaks some native addons and prebuilt binaries (tokenizer and ONNX packages are common victims) |\n| Version | Pin the major to an LTS line | Predictable runtime |\n| `NODE_ENV=production` | Set it | Some libraries read it to disable debug paths |\n| `.dockerignore` | List `node_modules`, `.env`, `dist`, `.git` | Small build context, local secrets stay out |\n| User | The `node` user the official image provides | Not root |\n\n### Signals: exec form, PID 1 and draining\n- **Exec form makes Node PID 1.** `CMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]` runs Node directly.\n- **Shell form hides Node behind a shell.** `CMD node dist/server.js` runs `/bin/sh -c \"node dist/server.js\"`;\n  the shell is PID 1, it does not forward `SIGTERM`, and the orchestrator waits out the grace period then\n  `SIGKILL`s mid-stream.\n- **Do not rely on shell luck.** Some shells exec a lone `-c` command directly, accidentally making Node\n  PID 1; that breaks the moment someone adds `&&`.\n- **PID 1 gets no default signal action.** The kernel applies none, so Node reacts to `SIGTERM` only if\n  your code installs a handler; otherwise the signal is ignored and you wait for `SIGKILL` again.\n- **PID 1 must reap zombies.** Node does not. If the service spawns subprocesses (an MCP server over stdio,\n  a converter), run with `docker run --init`, `tini` or `dumb-init` as the entrypoint.\n- **Division of labour.** The drain (stop accepting, finish or abort in-flight streams, close the pool) is\n  the graceful-shutdown lesson. The deployment delivers the signal and sets the grace period longer than\n  the longest completion you allow.\n\n### Memory and the container boundary\n- **The default V8 heap limit is a heuristic** from detected memory. Older releases read host memory and\n  ignored the cgroup limit; newer ones read the cgroup limit, with quality depending on the Node version\n  and cgroup v1 versus v2.\n- **The heap limit covers only the JavaScript heap.** `Buffer`s, native addons, thread stacks and the code\n  cache live outside it, so resident memory can cross the container limit while V8 thinks it has room, and\n  the kernel's OOM killer ends the process.\n- **Set `--max-old-space-size` explicitly**, somewhat below the container limit. The example leaves about a\n  quarter of a 1 GiB limit for non-heap memory. Confirm with `v8.getHeapStatistics()` at startup.\n- **Where services discover this:** streaming large contexts and holding many concurrent responses.\n\n### Health, readiness and config\n- **Liveness proves only that the event loop responds; readiness checks dependencies** (database, cache,\n  draining). Keep them apart *because* a liveness probe that checks the database turns a database blip\n  into a restart of every replica at once.\n- **Readiness flips to false the moment `SIGTERM` arrives**, the signal a plain load balancer uses to stop\n  routing.\n- **Kubernetes adds an endpoint race.** A terminating pod is removed from the Service's endpoints, but the\n  removal reaches kube-proxy and ingress controllers asynchronously, so new connections can arrive for a few\n  seconds. Closing the listener immediately fails them.\n- **Fix the race with a delay.** A short `preStop` sleep (or a delay in the handler) before `server.close()`.\n  The grace period must cover that delay plus the longest stream, since `preStop` time counts against it.\n- **Parse config once at startup with a schema**, so a missing `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or a non-numeric `PORT`\n  fails before the first request.\n- **Secrets arrive at runtime** from the platform (Kubernetes Secrets, a secret manager, CI-provided\n  environment), never as `ARG`/`ENV` at build time, because build args are recorded in image history.\n\n### The pipeline\n1. **Pull request**, in parallel where possible: `tsc --noEmit`, lint, unit tests, and integration tests\n   against service containers started by CI.\n2. **`main`** additionally builds the image once with BuildKit, tags it with the git SHA (plus a semver tag\n   at release), pushes it to a registry and deploys that digest. Staging and production run byte-identical\n   images, and a rollback is a tag swap.\n3. **Smoke test the image in staging.** PR tests ran against source on the runner, not the image. A file\n   missing from a `COPY`, a native addon built for the wrong libc, or a `dist/` path typo only appears in the\n   image. Start it, hit readiness, make one request through a stubbed or cheap model.\n4. **Prompts, tool schemas and the model identifier ship in the image**, so changing them is a deploy like\n   any other.\n5. **Eval beside the gate.** A job runs the golden set against the real model with a budget and publishes a\n   score; a drop blocks promotion to production without making every PR pay for model calls.\n6. **Canary prompt or model changes.** Route a slice of traffic, compare quality and cost metrics, widen.","example":"### Multi-stage Dockerfile\nInstalls from the lockfile with a cached layer, compiles, prunes, and runs as `node` in exec form.\nSecrets reach the runtime from the platform, not from this file.\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM node:22-slim AS deps\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package.json package-lock.json ./\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.npm npm ci       # cached until the lockfile changes\n\nFROM deps AS build\nCOPY tsconfig.json ./\nCOPY src ./src\nRUN npm run build && npm prune --omit=dev             # dist/ plus production deps only\n\nFROM node:22-slim AS runtime\nENV NODE_ENV=production\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /app/node_modules ./node_modules\nCOPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist\nCOPY package.json ./\nUSER node\nEXPOSE 3000\n# exec form so SIGTERM reaches node; heap set for a 1 GiB container memory limit\nCMD [\"node\", \"--max-old-space-size=768\", \"dist/server.js\"]\n```\n\n### GitHub Actions: gate, build once, tag with the commit\nTypecheck and tests run against a real Postgres service container; only `main` builds and pushes an image\ntagged with the commit SHA.\n\n```yaml\nname: ci\non: { pull_request: {}, push: { branches: [main] } }\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    services:\n      postgres:\n        image: postgres:16\n        env: { POSTGRES_PASSWORD: test }\n        ports: [\"5432:5432\"]\n        options: --health-cmd pg_isready --health-interval 5s --health-retries 10\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with: { node-version: 22, cache: npm }\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run typecheck && npm run lint && npm test\n        env: { DATABASE_URL: postgres://postgres:test@localhost:5432/postgres }\n  image:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions: { contents: read, packages: write }\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with:\n          registry: ghcr.io\n          username: ${{ github.actor }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n      - uses: docker/build-push-action@v6\n        with:\n          push: true\n          tags: ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing the grace period for streams\nA chat service caps completions at 120 seconds by policy (an illustrative value; use your own maximum).\n\n| Phase after `SIGTERM` | Time budget | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Readiness flips to false | immediate | load balancer stops routing |\n| `preStop` sleep | 5 s | endpoint removal propagates to kube-proxy and ingress |\n| `server.close()`, finish or abort streams | up to 120 s | longest allowed completion |\n| Close pools, flush telemetry | ~5 s | clean exit |\n| Margin | ~5 s | jitter |\n| **`terminationGracePeriodSeconds`** | **135** | default of 30 would cut long streams |\n\n- **Check:** old pods should exit as soon as their last stream ends, not at exactly 135 s; lingering for the\n  full period means the handler never acted.\n- **Cost:** every rollout can now take up to that long per pod batch, which is why the cap is the policy\n  maximum, not \"infinite\".","patterns":["**Deploy by digest, roll back by tag.** Tag every image with `${{ github.sha }}` and deploy that exact digest to staging and then production, so rollback re-points to the previous SHA without a rebuild. Monitor which digest each environment runs and alert on drift between them.","**Smoke test the artifact, not the source.** After the staging deploy, a CI job starts the image, waits for readiness and sends one request through a stubbed or cheap model before promotion. Watch for failures caused by a missing `COPY` or wrong-libc addons that PR tests cannot see.","**`preStop` sleep plus readiness flip.** In the pod spec, add a `lifecycle.preStop` exec hook running a short `sleep` (a few seconds), flip readiness on `SIGTERM`, and size `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` from the longest allowed completion. Monitor 502s and connection resets during each rollout; the target is zero.","**Scheduled rebuild of a pinned major.** Keep `FROM node:22-slim` and add an `on: schedule` cron trigger to the workflow that rebuilds weekly, so base-image security patches land without unpinning. Watch image scan findings and image size over time.","**Audit images for leaked secrets.** Run `docker history --no-trunc <image>` (or a scanner in CI) on release images to confirm no key appears in build args or layers. Alert on any match and rotate the key immediately, since registry copies cannot be recalled.","**Canary prompt and model changes.** Route a small slice of traffic to the new digest, compare quality score, cost per request and error rate against the baseline, then widen. Monitor the eval score as the promotion gate."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    PR[\"Pull request\"] --> G[\"Typecheck, lint, unit, integration\"]\n    G -->|\"merge to main\"| B[\"Build image once, tag git SHA\"]\n    B --> R[(\"Registry\")]\n    R --> S[\"Deploy digest to staging\"]\n    S --> SM[\"Smoke test: readiness plus one request\"]\n    SM --> EV{\"Eval score holds?\"}\n    EV -->|\"no\"| X[\"Block promotion\"]\n    EV -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Canary slice in production\"]\n    C -->|\"quality and cost OK\"| P[\"Widen to all traffic\"]\n    C -.->|\"regression\"| RB[\"Rollback: tag swap\"]","caption":"Pull requests stop at the deterministic gate; `main` builds one SHA-tagged image that is smoke-tested,\neval-checked and canaried before it reaches all traffic."},"interview":[{"q":"What does `npm ci` guarantee that `npm install` does not, and why does it matter in a Dockerfile?","a":"- **Definition:** `npm ci` installs exactly what the lockfile says and fails if lockfile and `package.json`\n  disagree, instead of resolving and possibly rewriting versions.\n- **Why it matters:** the image then contains the dependency tree CI tested, not a version no machine ever ran.\n- **Layer order:** copying only the manifests before `npm ci` keeps that slow layer cached until dependencies change."},{"q":"A pod is killed with exit code 137 under load, but the logs show no JavaScript heap error. What do you check?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** 137 means `SIGKILL`, here from the kernel OOM killer: resident memory crossed the container\n  limit while V8 thought it had heap room.\n- **Cause:** `Buffer`s, native addons, thread stacks and the code cache live outside the V8 heap limit.\n- **Fix:** set `--max-old-space-size` below the limit (768 for 1 GiB leaves about a quarter), check\n  `v8.getHeapStatistics()` at startup, and measure memory under concurrent streams."},{"q":"Why should the provider API key never be a Docker build argument?","a":"- **Build args are recorded in image history**, so anyone who can pull the image, from any registry copy,\n  can read the key with `docker history`.\n- **Runtime injection** from a secret manager or orchestrator secret keeps it out of the artifact, allows\n  rotation without a rebuild, and lets each environment use its own key."},{"q":"When would you choose an Alpine base image over slim, and when not?","a":"- **Choose Alpine** when the dependency tree is pure JavaScript and image size matters.\n- **Avoid it** with native tokenizer, ONNX or other prebuilt binaries, because musl breaks some of them.\n- **Default:** `node:<major>-slim` (glibc), pinned to an LTS major and rebuilt on a schedule for patches."},{"q":"How would you size `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` for a streaming service?","a":"- **Add the phases:** `preStop` delay for endpoint propagation, plus the longest allowed completion, plus\n  pool close and a margin.\n- **Example:** 5 s + 120 s + about 10 s gives about 135 s, versus the default of 30.\n- **Verify:** deploy under synthetic streaming load; zero truncated responses and pods exiting before the\n  deadline mean the drain works."}],"tradeoffs":["**Multi-stage: smaller image vs build complexity.** Shrinks the runtime image and attack surface; for a throwaway prototype a single-stage image is fine until it ships to a shared environment.","**Alpine vs slim: size vs compatibility.** Alpine is smaller, yet musl breaks some native tokenizer and ML addons; the saving flips to a loss the first time a prebuilt binary fails to load.","**esbuild bundle: cold start vs visibility.** One file speeds cold start and removes `node_modules`, but hides dependency versions from scanners and breaks packages relying on `__dirname` or dynamic requires.","**Evals outside the PR gate: speed vs enforcement.** Merges stay fast and cheap only if the scheduled eval blocks promotion; an eval that merely reports is a dashboard nobody reads.","**Grace period: clean streams vs slow rollouts.** Long lets streams finish; very long slows every rollout and masks handlers that never stop. Cap it at the maximum completion time you actually allow.","**Digest pin vs patch flow.** Pinning the base image by digest is reproducible but freezes security patches; a pinned major tag plus a scheduled rebuild is the usual compromise."],"pitfalls":["**Shell-form `CMD` or an entrypoint script that does not `exec`.** Symptom: pods always take the full grace period to stop and users' streams cut off at every deploy.","**Copying the whole source before `npm ci`.** Symptom: a dependency install on every commit and a ten-minute build for a one-line change.","**Passing the provider key as a build `ARG` or `ENV`.** Symptom: the key is readable in image history (`docker history`) and in every registry the image was pushed to.","**Leaving the heap limit to the default heuristic.** Symptom: kernel OOM kills under load (exit code 137) with no JavaScript heap error in the logs.","**Running `npm install` instead of `npm ci`.** Symptom: a dependency version in production that no developer machine or CI run ever tested.","**Readiness green while draining, or the listener closed the instant `SIGTERM` arrives with no `preStop` delay.** Symptom: a burst of 502s or connection resets at every rollout even though the drain code looks correct.","**Deploying prompt or model-id changes with no eval.** Symptom: users report a quality regression days after a deploy that \"only touched a text file\"."],"when":"- **The floor, for any Node AI service beyond a laptop:** a multi-stage Dockerfile, `npm ci`, exec-form\n  `CMD`, runtime secrets, and a PR pipeline with typecheck and tests.\n- **Add as you grow:** integration tests with service containers once a database or queue is in the path;\n  an explicit heap limit and readiness-aware drain once you stream; a budgeted eval job as soon as prompts\n  change regularly.\n- **Measure:** image size, build time for a one-line change (with warm caches, little more than the compile\n  step, since the install layer is reused), deploy duration, and requests failed during a rollout, which\n  should be zero.\n- **Interviews:** why `SIGTERM` is not reaching the process, what `npm ci` guarantees, and where the API key\n  should live; concrete answers separate a senior engineer from someone who has only run `docker build`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the Dockerfile and workflow, keeps the layer order cache-friendly, wires the readiness flip into shutdown and sets heap and grace-period values from measured completion times.","architect":"Owns the promotion policy: which checks gate a merge, which gate production, how evals and canaries fit the rollout, and how secrets and model credentials are isolated per environment.","fde":"Adapts the pipeline to the customer's registry, secret manager and cluster, and proves to their platform team that the image is non-root, lockfile-pinned and free of embedded keys."},"check":{"q":"After each deploy, a streaming chat service shows a spike of truncated responses and the old\npods linger for exactly thirty seconds before dying. What is happening, and what changes fix it?","a":"Thirty seconds is Kubernetes' default `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, and lingering for all of\nit means the process never acted on `SIGTERM`. Three causes are common: the `CMD` is in shell form, so\n`sh` is PID 1 and the signal never reaches Node; Node is PID 1 but installs no `SIGTERM` handler, and\nthe kernel ignores default-action signals for PID 1; or the handler exists but nothing stops the HTTP\nserver and in-flight streams. In each case the orchestrator finally sends `SIGKILL` mid-response. Fix it by switching to exec-form\n`CMD` (or `exec` in the entrypoint script), handling `SIGTERM` by flipping readiness to false, waiting a few seconds\n(a `preStop` sleep) for endpoint removal to propagate, then calling `server.close()`, aborting or finishing active streams with an `AbortController`, and closing pools;\nthen set the grace period above the longest allowed completion. Verify with a deploy under synthetic\nstreaming load and confirm zero truncated responses."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why copy `package.json` and the lockfile and run `npm ci` before copying the source tree?","options":["Because `npm ci` fails if source files are present in the build context","So the dependency layer is cached and only rebuilt when the lockfile changes, not on every commit","To keep `devDependencies` out of the final image","Because TypeScript must be compiled before dependencies can resolve"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Docker caches layers by their inputs. If the source is copied first, every commit invalidates the install layer. Copying manifests first means the slow install reruns only when dependencies change. Pruning dev dependencies is a separate step and unrelated to layer order."},{"q":"A container's `CMD` is written as `CMD node dist/server.js`. What is the practical consequence on `kubectl rollout`?","options":["Node starts faster because the shell preloads the module cache","Environment variables are not expanded, so the process reads no configuration","`/bin/sh` becomes PID 1 and does not forward `SIGTERM`, so the process is killed after the grace period instead of draining","The container cannot expose ports because the shell owns the network namespace"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shell form wraps the command in `sh -c`. The shell receives the signal and does not pass it on, Node never sees it, and the orchestrator escalates to `SIGKILL`. Exec form (`CMD [\"node\", ...]`) makes Node PID 1 so the graceful-shutdown handler you install runs. Environment expansion and ports are unaffected."},{"q":"Where should the provider API key enter a deployed Node AI service?","options":["As a Docker build argument so the image is self-contained","Committed in an `.env` file that the Dockerfile copies into the image","Injected at runtime by the platform (secret manager or orchestrator secret) into the process environment","Hard-coded in the compiled bundle, since `dist/` is not human-readable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Anything present at build time lands in an image layer and is readable from image history or any registry copy. Runtime injection keeps the secret out of the artifact, lets it rotate without a rebuild, and allows different keys per environment."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Vercel AI SDK","Official docs: Zod"],"why":"- **Every provider has its own shape.** OpenAI returns `choices[0].message.content` and tool calls with\n  JSON-string arguments; Anthropic returns an array of content blocks with parsed `input`; Google differs\n  again, and each streams a different event vocabulary. Comparing models, keeping a fallback or letting a\n  customer bring their own key means an adapter per provider and a normalised stream type of your own.\n- **The browser needs a protocol.** A chat UI needs tokens as they arrive, tool-call progress, partial\n  structured objects, stop buttons and history. The naive `fetch` plus `TextDecoder` loop grows into a\n  protocol with no name that only one engineer understands.\n- **Native SDKs and frameworks miss the middle.** Native SDKs are right for one provider and its full\n  surface, but they are what the adapter would wrap. A heavyweight agent framework solves both problems at\n  the cost of owning its abstractions for everything else.\n- **The design bet: standardise the call and the UI stream, then stop.** The unified call covers the large\n  common core (messages, tools, structured output, streaming, usage); the provider-specific long tail is\n  reached through typed provider options. Whether that bet holds for you is this lesson's decision.","how":"### The provider abstraction\n- **One specification, many packages.** `@ai-sdk/openai`, `@ai-sdk/anthropic`, `@ai-sdk/google` and\n  community packages implement the SDK's language-model specification: a small interface with `doGenerate`\n  and `doStream`.\n- **Unified input and output.** Both take the unified prompt (system, messages with typed parts, tool\n  definitions, settings) and return a result or a stream of unified parts: text deltas, reasoning deltas,\n  tool-call deltas, tool results, and a finish part with `finishReason` and `usage`.\n- **Why models are interchangeable.** The core functions are written once against that interface, so\n  `anthropic(\"...\")` and `openai(\"...\")` swap at the call site; the wire-format mapping lives in the\n  provider package, not your code.\n- **The escape hatch.** Provider-only features (Anthropic's `cache_control` breakpoints or thinking settings,\n  OpenAI's reasoning effort, a Google safety setting) go through `providerOptions` keyed by provider. They\n  simply do nothing on a provider that lacks them.\n\n### Core functions\n| Function | Takes | Returns / does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `generateText`, `streamText` | `model`, `system`, `messages` (or `prompt`), optional `tools`, settings such as `temperature`, `maxOutputTokens`, `abortSignal` | Streaming variant: `textStream` and `fullStream` async iterables, plus `text`, `usage` and `steps` promises that resolve at the end, so one call serves the response body and your logging |\n| `tool({ description, inputSchema, execute })` | A Zod (or JSON) schema | Becomes the provider's tool definition; arguments are validated before `execute` runs (contract-as-value, as in the schema-validation lesson) |\n| `generateObject`, `streamObject` | A Zod schema | Typed, validated object (or partial objects); a non-conforming answer is an error (`NoObjectGeneratedError`), not a mis-typed value |\n| `embed`, `embedMany` | Embedding model and values | Same unification for embeddings |\n\n- **Tool execution and the loop.** With `execute` present the SDK runs the tool and records its result. With\n  `stopWhen` (for example `stepCountIs(n)`) it appends the result and calls the model again until the\n  condition holds or the model stops calling tools.\n- **The default is a single step** (`stepCountIs(1)`) for `generateText` / `streamText`. Without `stopWhen`\n  the tool runs but the model never sees its result and never writes the final answer.\n- **Omit `execute`** to receive the tool call and run it yourself, for approvals or client-side tools.\n- **How `generateObject` constrains output.** The provider package picks its best mechanism (a JSON schema\n  response format, a forced tool call, or an instruction) and the SDK validates the result against the schema.\n\n### One request through the tool loop\nWith `tools` that have `execute` and `stopWhen: stepCountIs(4)`, a single `streamText` call runs:\n1. **Step 1.** The SDK maps `system`, `messages` and the tool schemas to the provider's request and streams\n   the reply. The model emits a tool call; the SDK accumulates argument fragments and validates the parsed\n   input against the tool's Zod schema.\n2. **Execute.** `execute` runs with the typed input. The SDK appends the assistant message holding the tool\n   call and a tool message holding the result. (In v5 a failed execution is recorded as a tool-error part\n   rather than thrown, so the model can read it; check how your version treats errors.)\n3. **Check the stop condition.** The loop continues only if the last step ended in tool calls and `stopWhen`\n   is not yet satisfied. Otherwise the finish part is emitted.\n4. **Step 2.** A *new* provider request is sent with the original prompt plus the appended messages. The\n   model now writes the answer.\n\nTwo consequences of step 4:\n- **Each step is a separate billed request that resends the whole history**, so input tokens grow every\n  round. That is *why* `totalUsage` (summed over steps) can be several times the first request's input, and\n  why a step limit is a cost control, not only a loop guard.\n- **`steps` is your trace.** Each step carries its own text, tool calls, results and usage; log them to see\n  where a slow or expensive answer spent its rounds.\n\n### The UI half and its protocol\n- **Client.** `useChat` (from `@ai-sdk/react` in v5; Vue and Svelte packages exist too) keeps the message\n  list, sends it to your route on submit, and appends parts as they stream: text, tool invocations with their\n  state, and custom data.\n- **Server.** The route returns the result's response helper, which encodes unified stream parts into the\n  UI message stream. In v5 that is JSON parts sent as server-sent events and marked by a protocol-version\n  header; v4 used a line-prefixed \"data stream\" format. A plain text alternative exists when you only need tokens.\n- **What it buys.** A tool call started on the server renders as a progress card in the browser without you\n  writing the protocol.\n- **The coupling.** Client and server packages must agree on the protocol version, which is why the two\n  sides are upgraded together.\n\n### Middleware and telemetry\n- **`wrapLanguageModel`** wraps any model with middleware that can transform the prompt, intercept the\n  result or short-circuit the call. Logging, caching, guardrails and default settings live here instead of\n  at every call site.\n- **OpenTelemetry**, switched on per call with the `experimental_telemetry` option, emits spans for each\n  call and step with token usage, into the same tracing pipeline as the rest of the service.\n\n### Runtime fit and versions\n- **Runs anywhere `fetch` exists:** Node, Bun, Deno, edge runtimes, and inside Express, Fastify, Hono or\n  Next.js route handlers. Nothing requires Vercel's platform; the UI hooks need a framework only on the client.\n- **Names shift across majors.** In v5 tool `parameters` became `inputSchema`, `maxTokens` became\n  `maxOutputTokens`, `maxSteps` became `stopWhen`, and `convertToCoreMessages` became `convertToModelMessages`.\n- **v6 moved again.** It deprecated `generateObject` / `streamObject` in favour of `generateText` /\n  `streamText` with an `output` setting, added an agent abstraction, and its migration guide lists further\n  signature changes. Check the version you install against its docs rather than copying old snippets.","example":"### Streaming tool route plus typed classification\nA route handler that streams a tool-using answer to a `useChat` client, and a separate function that\nclassifies text into a typed object. The model line is the only provider-specific code; written against\nAI SDK 5 naming.\n\n```ts\nimport { streamText, generateObject, tool, stepCountIs, convertToModelMessages } from \"ai\";\nimport { anthropic } from \"@ai-sdk/anthropic\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\nconst model = anthropic(process.env.MODEL_ID ?? \"claude-opus-5-5\");   // swap for openai(\"...\") to compare\n\nexport async function chatHandler(req: Request): Promise<Response> {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();                             // UI messages from useChat\n  const result = streamText({\n    model,\n    system: \"You are a support assistant. Look orders up with the tool; never guess order data.\",\n    messages: convertToModelMessages(messages),\n    tools: {\n      lookupOrder: tool({\n        description: \"Fetch an order by its id\",\n        inputSchema: z.object({ orderId: z.string().regex(/^ord_\\w+$/) }),\n        execute: async ({ orderId }) => (await db.orders.find(orderId)) ?? { error: \"not found\" },\n      }),\n    },\n    stopWhen: stepCountIs(4),          // at most 4 model/tool rounds per request\n    abortSignal: req.signal,           // client disconnect stops generation and further billing\n    onFinish: ({ totalUsage, steps }) =>   // `usage` here is the last step only; totalUsage sums all\n      console.log({ totalUsage, steps: steps.length, toolCalls: steps.flatMap((s) => s.toolCalls).length }),\n  });\n  return result.toUIMessageStreamResponse();   // tokens, tool state and data to the browser\n}\n\nconst Intent = z.object({\n  intent: z.enum([\"refund\", \"shipping\", \"other\"]),\n  confidence: z.number().min(0).max(1),\n});\n\nexport async function classify(text: string) {\n  const { object } = await generateObject({ model, schema: Intent, prompt: `Classify:\\n${text}` });\n  return object;                       // typed { intent, confidence }, validated against Intent\n}\n```\n\n### Worked trace: \"Where is ord_42?\"\n| Moment | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Step 1 request | System prompt, one user message and the `lookupOrder` schema are sent |\n| Step 1 reply | Tool call `{ orderId: \"ord_42\" }`; the regex passes; `execute` reads the database |\n| Browser | A tool card moves from \"input available\" to \"output available\" before any answer text |\n| Step 2 request | Everything resent plus the call and its result; the model writes the answer |\n| `onFinish` | `steps.length` is 2; `totalUsage.inputTokens` counts the system prompt twice, because it was sent twice |\n\n- **Bad input never reaches the tool.** Had the model asked for `\"42\"`, the Zod regex would have rejected\n  the input before `execute` ran.\n- **A provider swap changes only the wire.** Swapping `anthropic(...)` for `openai(...)` changes the bytes\n  (a `tool_calls` entry with string arguments instead of a `tool_use` block) and nothing in this file.\n\n### Handling a structured-output failure explicitly\n`generateObject` can still fail when the model's answer does not match the schema. Catch that error type,\nlog what the model wrote, and return an explicit fallback that your metrics count as a failure.\n\n```ts\nimport { NoObjectGeneratedError } from \"ai\";\n\nexport async function classifySafe(text: string) {\n  try {\n    return await classify(text);                       // from the example above\n  } catch (e) {\n    if (NoObjectGeneratedError.isInstance(e)) {\n      console.warn({ msg: \"intent schema failure\", raw: e.text, finishReason: e.finishReason });\n      return { intent: \"other\" as const, confidence: 0 };   // explicit fallback, never silent\n    }\n    throw e;                                            // network, auth, abort: let the caller decide\n  }\n}\n```","patterns":["**Budgeted, cancellable tool loops.** On every route with tools, set `stopWhen: stepCountIs(n)` with a small `n` and pass `abortSignal: req.signal`, so a looping model or a closed tab stops billing. Monitor steps per request and `totalUsage` per conversation from `onFinish`, and alert on the tail.","**Upgrade client and server together.** Pin `ai`, the provider packages and `@ai-sdk/react` to exact versions and bump them in one PR, because the UI stream protocol version must match on both sides. After deploy, watch for client render errors or empty messages on the chat page.","**Cross-cutting policy in middleware.** Wrap the shared model once with `wrapLanguageModel` for logging, default settings or a guardrail, and export that wrapped model to every route. Monitor latency added by the middleware and keep per-route exceptions out of it.","**Per-call tracing.** Turn on `experimental_telemetry` for production calls so each call and step emits an OpenTelemetry span with token usage. Track p95 time to first token, tool-call counts and cost per route in the same dashboards as the HTTP spans.","**Provider comparison as an eval job.** Run the golden set through the same code with the model line set to each candidate (`anthropic(...)`, `openai(...)`) in a scheduled job, and record quality, latency and cost side by side. Also run the tool schemas against each provider, since some reject schemas others accept."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant B as Browser useChat\n    participant R as Route streamText\n    participant P as Provider\n    participant T as Tool execute\n    B->>R: POST messages\n    R->>P: Step 1: system, messages, tool schemas\n    P-->>R: tool call, args validated by Zod\n    R-->>B: tool card: input available\n    R->>T: execute with typed input\n    T-->>R: result\n    R->>P: Step 2: history plus call and result\n    P-->>R: answer text and finish part\n    R-->>B: text parts, output available","caption":"One `streamText` call with a tool is two billed provider requests; the second resends the history plus the\ntool call and result."},"interview":[{"q":"What does the AI SDK actually normalise across providers, and what does it leave to you?","a":"- **Normalised:** the call shape (system, messages, tools, settings), the stream parts (text, reasoning and\n  tool-call deltas, finish with `usage`), tool validation and the structured-output path.\n- **Left to you:** provider-only features via `providerOptions`, endpoints it does not model such as batch\n  APIs, and checking that each provider accepts your tool schemas."},{"q":"A user reports that the assistant \"looked up the order and then said nothing.\" What happened?","a":"- **Likely cause:** no `stopWhen`, so the default single step (`stepCountIs(1)`) ran the tool but never sent\n  its result back for a second model request.\n- **Confirm:** `steps.length` is 1 and the last step ended in tool calls.\n- **Fix:** set `stopWhen: stepCountIs(n)` with a small budget and log steps per request."},{"q":"Why can a tool-using request cost several times its first request's input tokens?","a":"- **Each step is a new billed request** that resends the full history plus the appended tool call and result.\n- **So input grows every round:** with two steps the system prompt is counted twice in `totalUsage`.\n- **Control it** with a step limit, a short system prompt or provider caching, and monitor `totalUsage`, not `usage`."},{"q":"When would you not use the AI SDK?","a":"- **One provider, deep features:** explicit prompt caching, batch jobs or server-side tools favour the native SDK.\n- **Byte-level control:** auditing or caching that needs the exact request body.\n- **Writing a library:** consumers should not inherit your abstraction and its version churn."},{"q":"How would you roll out an upgrade from AI SDK v4 to v5 safely?","a":"- **Upgrade server and client packages together**, because v4 used a line-prefixed data stream and v5 uses\n  server-sent JSON parts with a protocol-version header.\n- **Apply the renames:** `parameters` to `inputSchema`, `maxTokens` to `maxOutputTokens`, `maxSteps` to `stopWhen`.\n- **Verify** with an interception test of the route and a canary, watching empty-message and render errors."}],"tradeoffs":["**One call shape vs newest features.** Comparisons and fallbacks get cheap, but the newest provider-specific feature usually arrives behind `providerOptions` or not at all for a while. If your product is built on such a feature, the native SDK is the better primary interface.","**Built-in loop vs request control.** The tool loop and `generateObject` remove boilerplate, yet they decide the mechanism (forced tool versus JSON mode) for you. When you need deterministic control over every request body, for caching or auditing, drop to the native SDK for that path.","**UI protocol vs ownership.** Streaming tool and data parts come free, at the cost of coupling client and server versions and a wire format you do not own. A plain text stream is simpler when the client is not yours.","**Middleware vs hidden behaviour.** It centralises logging, caching and guardrails, but a stack of middlewares is a second place behaviour hides. It pays while every call site needs the same policy, and stops once middlewares need per-route exceptions; then explicit calls are clearer.","**Following majors vs stability.** Renamed core options are cheap to follow in a small codebase and painful in a large one; pin the version and schedule upgrades rather than floating."],"pitfalls":["**Assuming `generateObject` removes failure handling.** Symptom: an unhandled schema validation error in production when the model returns a value outside the enum.","**A generous `stopWhen` limit, or an agent abstraction with a high default, with no budget.** Symptom: a request makes a dozen tool calls, times out at the proxy and bills for every round.","**Omitting `stopWhen` entirely.** Symptom: the tool runs but the reply ends with no answer text.","**Forgetting to pass `abortSignal` from the request.** Symptom: server logs show completions finishing minutes after users closed the tab.","**Upgrading `ai` on the server without the client package.** Symptom: the UI renders nothing or shows raw protocol lines because the stream format changed.","**Reaching for `providerOptions` without checking the provider.** Symptom: a cache or thinking setting is silently ignored after switching models, and cost and latency change unexpectedly.","**Treating the model string as configuration-free.** Symptom: a provider swap passes tests with the fake and fails on tool schemas the new provider rejects."],"when":"- **Use when** you want to compare or switch providers, a browser UI needs streamed text, tool state or\n  partial objects, or a small team wants typed tools and structured output without owning an agent framework.\n- **Prefer the native SDK when** you commit to one provider and lean on its specific features (explicit\n  prompt caching, batch APIs, server-side tools), need byte-level control over requests, or are writing a\n  library others will consume.\n- **Measure** the same things as with a native client: tokens and cost per request, p95 time to first token,\n  tool-call counts per conversation, and schema validation failure rate.\n- **Interviews:** explain what the abstraction normalises, what it cannot, and when you would step outside it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes route handlers with `streamText`, defines tools with Zod, sets step limits and abort signals, and uses `providerOptions` for caching or reasoning settings per provider.","architect":"Decides whether the unified layer or a native SDK is the primary interface, how provider switching is exercised in CI, and where middleware for logging and guardrails sits.","fde":"Uses the provider swap to run a customer's prompts across models with one codebase and shows them the cost, latency and quality differences before they commit to a vendor."},"check":{"q":"A team built their product on the AI SDK and now wants Anthropic prompt caching on a 40k-token\nsystem prompt and a batch job that classifies a million records overnight. What do you advise?","a":"Split the two. Prompt caching is reachable without leaving the SDK: pass the cache breakpoint\nthrough `providerOptions` for the Anthropic provider on the system message or tool definitions, then\nverify by reading cache-read token counts in `usage` or provider metadata; if the breakpoint is not\nexposed in their version, that single path can use the native SDK while the rest stays unified. The\nbatch job is a different story: a million offline classifications belong on the provider's batch API,\nwhich trades latency for a lower price and is not something the unified call models. Write that job\ndirectly against the native SDK, reuse the same Zod schema for validation, and keep the interactive\npaths on the AI SDK. The rule is: unified layer for the common interactive surface, native SDK for\nprovider-specific economics."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes `anthropic(\"...\")` and `openai(\"...\")` interchangeable in a `streamText` call?","options":["The SDK translates prompts into a provider-neutral language the models all understand","Each provider package implements the same language-model specification, mapping the unified prompt and stream parts to its own wire format","Both providers expose an identical REST API, so no mapping is needed","The SDK proxies every request through Vercel's servers, which normalise the response"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The core functions are written against one interface; the provider packages do the translation in both directions. The provider APIs differ substantially, and nothing is routed through Vercel; the package runs wherever `fetch` runs."},{"q":"When should a team step outside the AI SDK and call a provider's native SDK directly?","options":["Whenever they stream responses to a browser","Whenever they use tools, since the SDK cannot run a tool loop","When they depend on provider-specific features or endpoints the unified layer does not model, such as a batch API or byte-level request control","Never; the SDK exposes every provider feature through its core functions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Streaming and tool loops are the SDK's core strengths. Its limits are the features it chose not to unify; `providerOptions` covers many, but batch processing, exact request bodies and the newest provider capabilities are reasons to use the native client for that path."},{"q":"What does `generateObject` with a Zod schema do that `JSON.parse` on the model's text does not?","options":["It guarantees the model cannot produce an invalid answer, so no error handling is needed","It caches the result so repeated prompts are free","It sends the schema to the provider using its structured-output mechanism and validates the result, so the returned value is typed and conforming or an error is raised","It streams the object to the client automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`JSON.parse` gives you `unknown` from whatever the model wrote. `generateObject` constrains the generation where the provider supports it and validates against the schema, so a non-conforming value becomes an explicit error you must handle rather than a silently wrong object. It does not guarantee success, cache results or stream to the client."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI Node SDK (openai)","Official docs: Anthropic TypeScript SDK (@anthropic-ai/sdk)"],"why":"- **A hand-rolled `fetch` fails in predictable ways.** Under load the provider returns 429 or 529, and the\n  call either gives up or retries immediately and worsens the overload. Timeouts, request IDs for support\n  tickets, rate-limit headers, pagination of file and batch endpoints and the error taxonomy are left to\n  every caller to rediscover.\n- **Streams are harder than they look.** Server-sent events carry several event types that must be parsed,\n  re-assembled into content blocks and matched to a stop reason. A hand-written parser handles text and\n  silently drops tool calls.\n- **Tool arguments arrive with a JSON schema contract TypeScript cannot see**, so they get cast rather\n  than checked.\n- **The SDKs make these behaviours uniform and typed.** Both are generated from the provider's API\n  specification, *which is why* every request and response type matches the wire exactly and changes when\n  the API does. A thin wrapper of your own re-implements a worse version of this.\n- **Their defaults interact with yours.** An outer retry loop multiplies attempts; a proxy timeout shorter\n  than the SDK's hides its retries; a stream not aborted on client disconnect keeps billing.","how":"### Shared client mechanics\n| Setting | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| API key | `new OpenAI()` reads `OPENAI_API_KEY`; `new Anthropic()` reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`; or pass `apiKey` |\n| Other options | `baseURL`, `timeout`, `maxRetries`, `defaultHeaders`, a custom `fetch` |\n| `timeout` | Milliseconds (unlike the Python SDKs' seconds); default 10 minutes |\n| `maxRetries` | Default 2, so up to three attempts |\n| Retries on | Connection errors and 408, 409, 429 and 5xx, with exponential backoff that honours `retry-after` |\n| Runtimes | Supported Node LTS releases (check the `engines` field of your version), Bun, Deno and edge runtimes, because they use the global `fetch` |\n| Per request | Optional second argument `{ timeout, maxRetries, signal, headers }` |\n\n- **Cancellation is a plain `AbortSignal`.** Aborting rejects with `APIUserAbortError` and closes the socket,\n  stopping further generation; tokens already produced are still billed.\n- **Worst-case wall clock is `timeout × (maxRetries + 1)` plus backoff sleeps**, because retries are counted\n  per request and a timeout is retried. Size proxy and handler timeouts from that, not from `timeout` alone.\n- **Anthropic asymmetry.** For non-streaming requests with a large `max_tokens`, the Anthropic SDK raises\n  its default timeout itself, and very large output caps effectively require streaming, *because* an HTTP\n  response that takes many minutes is exactly what proxies and load balancers kill.\n\n### Typed errors and request IDs\n- **Hierarchy under the default export:** `APIError` with a numeric `status`, and subclasses such as\n  `BadRequestError` (400), `AuthenticationError` (401), `RateLimitError` (429), `InternalServerError` (5xx),\n  `APIConnectionError` and `APIConnectionTimeoutError`.\n- **Check with `instanceof`, most specific first**, never by matching message text.\n- **Request IDs** for support tickets: `_request_id` on objects returned by non-streaming calls,\n  `request_id` on Anthropic's `MessageStream` helper.\n- **Raw response:** `.withResponse()` returns `{ data, response }` so you can read rate-limit headers.\n\n### Requests and responses\n| | OpenAI Chat Completions | OpenAI Responses API | Anthropic Messages |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Call | `client.chat.completions.create` | `client.responses.create` | `client.messages.create` |\n| Input | `messages` with `system`, `user`, `assistant`, `tool` roles | `input` | top-level `system` string or blocks, `messages` of `user` / `assistant` turns, required `max_tokens` |\n| Output | `choices[0].message` with `content` and optional `tool_calls` | typed `output` items, `output_text` convenience | `content` array of blocks (`text`, `tool_use`, `thinking`) plus `stop_reason` |\n| Usage | `prompt_tokens` / `completion_tokens` | `input_tokens` / `output_tokens` | `input_tokens` / `output_tokens` plus cache read and creation counts |\n\n- **Responses API state.** It can keep conversation state server-side: pass `previous_response_id` instead\n  of resending history. Responses are stored by default unless you set `store: false`, a data-retention\n  decision, not just a convenience.\n- **Anthropic stop reasons:** `end_turn`, `tool_use`, `max_tokens`, `stop_sequence`, `pause_turn`,\n  `refusal`, and new values as the API grows, so keep a default branch.\n- **The structural difference to internalise.** Anthropic responses are always lists, so code narrows on\n  `block.type`; Chat Completions returns a single message with optional fields.\n- **Normalise usage early** if you log cost.\n\n### Streaming\n- **OpenAI Chat Completions** returns a stream when `stream: true` is set. Concatenate\n  `choices[0].delta.content`; usage arrives in a final chunk when `stream_options: { include_usage: true }`\n  is set.\n- **OpenAI tool calls in a stream** arrive as `delta.tool_calls[]` fragments keyed by `index`; concatenate\n  each index's `function.arguments` strings before parsing, or use the `client.chat.completions.stream()`\n  helper, whose `finalChatCompletion()` does it for you.\n- **OpenAI Responses API** yields named events such as `response.output_text.delta`.\n- **Anthropic `client.messages.stream()`** returns a helper that is both an iterable of raw events\n  (`content_block_start`, `content_block_delta` with `text_delta` or `input_json_delta`, `message_delta`,\n  `message_stop`) and an emitter with `.on(\"text\", …)`.\n- **`.finalMessage()`** resolves the complete, typed `Message` once the stream ends, so one call feeds the\n  user and your logs. The helper exists *because* tool-call arguments arrive as JSON fragments across many\n  events, which is exactly the code you do not want to write per service.\n\n### Tools and structured output: the two wire shapes\n- **OpenAI.** Chat Completions defines a tool as `{ type: \"function\", function: { name, description, parameters, strict } }`;\n  the model answers with `tool_calls[]`, each carrying an `id` and `function.arguments` as a JSON *string*\n  you must `JSON.parse`. The result goes back as a message with `role: \"tool\"` and `tool_call_id`.\n- **Anthropic.** It defines `{ name, description, input_schema }`; the model answers with `tool_use` content\n  blocks whose `input` is already an object. Results go back inside a `user` message as `tool_result` blocks\n  keyed by `tool_use_id`, all results for one turn in one message.\n- **Zod helpers.** OpenAI has `zodFunction` and `zodResponseFormat` from `openai/helpers/zod`, with a\n  `parse` method that returns `message.parsed`. Anthropic has `betaZodTool` with\n  `client.beta.messages.toolRunner`, which also runs the call-tool-append-repeat loop for you.\n- **Structured output.** OpenAI: `response_format` with a JSON schema and `strict: true` (`text.format` on\n  the Responses API). Anthropic: an `output_config.format` JSON schema in current SDK versions. In both, keep\n  your own `safeParse` on the result, as the schema-validation lesson argues.\n\n### Caching, batching, platforms\n- **Anthropic caching is opt-in.** Place `cache_control: { type: \"ephemeral\" }` on the last stable block\n  (system prompt, tool list), or in recent API versions set a top-level `cache_control` that marks the last\n  cacheable block for you. Verify with `usage.cache_read_input_tokens`.\n- **It is fragile by design.** Any byte change before the breakpoint invalidates it, and a prefix below the\n  model's minimum cacheable length is not cached at all.\n- **OpenAI caches long, repeated prefixes automatically** and reports it in\n  `usage.prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`, so the lever is prompt ordering, not annotations.\n- **Batch and files.** Both offer a batch endpoint for offline work at a discount and a files API.\n- **Token counting.** Anthropic has `messages.countTokens`; OpenAI estimates usually come from a tokenizer\n  library such as `tiktoken` (check whether your API version also offers a counting endpoint).\n- **Cloud platforms.** OpenAI ships an `AzureOpenAI` client; Anthropic ships `@anthropic-ai/bedrock-sdk` and\n  `@anthropic-ai/vertex-sdk` with the same `messages` surface behind platform credentials.","example":"### Two adapters behind one interface\nOne narrow `ModelClient` interface with two adapters. Each streams tokens to stdout, returns the full text\nwith normalised usage, relies on the SDK's retries rather than its own, and accepts an `AbortSignal` so a\ndisconnected client stops generation. `isRetryable` is what an outer circuit breaker would consult, using\ntyped errors instead of message text.\n\n```ts\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\nimport OpenAI from \"openai\";\n\nexport interface Completion { text: string; inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number; requestId?: string }\nexport interface ModelClient { complete(system: string, user: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<Completion> }\n\nexport function anthropicClient(model = \"claude-opus-5-5\"): ModelClient {\n  const client = new Anthropic({ maxRetries: 3, timeout: 60_000 });      // ms; retries 408/409/429/5xx\n  return {\n    async complete(system, user, signal) {\n      const stream = client.messages.stream(\n        { model, max_tokens: 2048, system, messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: user }] },\n        { signal },\n      );\n      stream.on(\"text\", (delta) => process.stdout.write(delta));\n      const msg = await stream.finalMessage();                             // typed Message, after the stream\n      const text = msg.content.flatMap((b) => (b.type === \"text\" ? [b.text] : [])).join(\"\");\n      return { text, inputTokens: msg.usage.input_tokens, outputTokens: msg.usage.output_tokens,\n               requestId: stream.request_id ?? undefined };                // from the response header\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nexport function openaiClient(model = process.env.OPENAI_MODEL ?? \"set-a-current-model-id\"): ModelClient {\n  const client = new OpenAI({ maxRetries: 3, timeout: 60_000 });\n  return {\n    async complete(system, user, signal) {\n      const stream = await client.chat.completions.create(\n        { model, stream: true, stream_options: { include_usage: true },\n          messages: [{ role: \"system\", content: system }, { role: \"user\", content: user }] },\n        { signal },\n      );\n      let text = \"\", usage: OpenAI.CompletionUsage | undefined;\n      for await (const chunk of stream) {\n        const delta = chunk.choices[0]?.delta?.content ?? \"\";\n        process.stdout.write(delta); text += delta;\n        if (chunk.usage) usage = chunk.usage;                               // arrives in the final chunk\n      }\n      return { text, inputTokens: usage?.prompt_tokens ?? 0, outputTokens: usage?.completion_tokens ?? 0 };\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nexport function isRetryable(err: unknown): boolean {      // abort and 4xx errors are not retryable\n  if (err instanceof Anthropic.APIConnectionError || err instanceof OpenAI.APIConnectionError) return true;\n  const e = err instanceof Anthropic.APIError || err instanceof OpenAI.APIError ? err : undefined;\n  return e !== undefined && (e.status === 429 || (e.status ?? 0) >= 500);\n}\n```\n\n### The tool-call round trip on the wire\nThe one thing the adapters hide: the four objects of a tool round trip, in each shape.\n\n```jsonc\n// OpenAI Chat Completions: the model's turn, then yours. arguments is a STRING.\n{ \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": null, \"tool_calls\": [{ \"id\": \"call_1\", \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": { \"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"orderId\\\":\\\"ord_42\\\"}\" } }] }\n{ \"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": \"call_1\", \"content\": \"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"shipped\\\"}\" }\n\n// Anthropic Messages: content is a list of blocks; input is an OBJECT; the result rides in a user turn.\n{ \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Let me check that order.\" },\n    { \"type\": \"tool_use\", \"id\": \"toolu_1\", \"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"input\": { \"orderId\": \"ord_42\" } }] }\n{ \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n    { \"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": \"toolu_1\", \"content\": \"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"shipped\\\"}\" }] }\n```\n\n- **Everything a tool adapter does follows from these four objects:** parse or do not parse the arguments,\n  echo the assistant turn back unchanged, and key the result by the call's id.\n- **Two calls in one turn:** OpenAI expects two `tool` messages; Anthropic expects both `tool_result`\n  blocks in the same single user message.\n\n### Worked scenario: worst-case latency of one call\nWith the adapters' settings, `timeout: 60_000` and `maxRetries: 3`, during a provider outage where every\nattempt times out:\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Attempts | `maxRetries + 1` = 4 |\n| Time in attempts | 4 × 60 s = 240 s |\n| Plus backoff sleeps | a few seconds more, longer if `retry-after` says so |\n| A 60 s proxy timeout in front | the user sees a 504 after the first attempt while the SDK keeps retrying |\n| Outer retry loop of 3 attempts on top | up to 12 requests and about 12 minutes for one click |\n\n- **Fix:** pick one retry layer, set the proxy and handler timeouts above `timeout × (maxRetries + 1)`, and\n  pass the request's `AbortSignal` so a user who gives up also stops the chain.\n\n### Reading request IDs and rate-limit headers\n`.withResponse()` exposes the raw `Response`, which carries the provider's rate-limit headers.\n\n```ts\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\n\nconst client = new Anthropic();\nconst { data: msg, response } = await client.messages\n  .create({ model: \"claude-opus-5-5\", max_tokens: 256, messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"ping\" }] })\n  .withResponse();\nconsole.log({\n  requestId: msg._request_id,                                               // quote this in support tickets\n  requestsRemaining: response.headers.get(\"anthropic-ratelimit-requests-remaining\"),\n});\n```","patterns":["**One retry layer only.** When the call already runs inside a queue with redelivery or your own retry and breaker, construct the client with `maxRetries: 0`; otherwise keep the SDK's retries and remove the outer loop. Monitor requests per user action and retry counts during provider incidents.","**Timeouts sized from the retry budget.** Set handler and proxy timeouts above `timeout × (maxRetries + 1)` plus backoff, and stream any generation with a large `max_tokens`. Watch for 504s and `APIConnectionTimeoutError` that appear only on long answers.","**Log request IDs and usage on every call.** Record `_request_id` (or `stream.request_id`), normalised input and output tokens, and cache counts in one structured log line. Monitor cost per route, and keep the ID so support tickets can be traced.","**Verify caching with counters.** For Anthropic, alert when `usage.cache_read_input_tokens` is zero on a route that sets `cache_control`; for OpenAI, track `usage.prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`. Keep timestamps and per-user data after the stable prefix.","**Back off from rate-limit headers.** Read the provider's remaining-request and remaining-token headers via `.withResponse()` (for OpenAI, the `x-ratelimit-remaining-requests` family) and slow a worker pool before hitting 429s. Monitor the 429 rate per API key.","**Same surface on cloud platforms.** When a customer must stay inside their cloud, swap the client for `AzureOpenAI`, `@anthropic-ai/bedrock-sdk` or `@anthropic-ai/vertex-sdk` behind the same `ModelClient` interface. Monitor per-platform latency and error rates separately."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"client call with options\"] --> A[\"Send attempt\"]\n    A --> R{\"Response?\"}\n    R -->|\"2xx\"| OK[\"Typed result or stream\"]\n    R -->|\"abort signal\"| AB[\"APIUserAbortError\"]\n    R -->|\"400, 401 and other 4xx\"| E4[\"Typed error, no retry\"]\n    R -->|\"408, 409, 429, 5xx, timeout, conn error\"| L{\"Retries left?\"}\n    L -->|\"yes\"| B[\"Backoff, honour retry-after\"]\n    B --> A\n    L -->|\"no\"| ER[\"RateLimitError, InternalServerError and similar\"]","caption":"What one SDK call does on failure: retryable errors back off and retry until `maxRetries` is spent, then\nsurface as a typed error; aborts and 4xx errors surface at once."},"interview":[{"q":"Why use the provider SDK instead of a plain `fetch` to the API?","a":"- **The SDK carries production defaults:** retries on connection errors and 408, 409, 429 and 5xx with\n  backoff honouring `retry-after`, timeouts, request IDs and a typed error hierarchy.\n- **Generated types match the wire**, so an API change shows up at compile time.\n- **Streaming helpers** reassemble tool-call fragments into a final typed message, the part hand-written\n  parsers usually drop."},{"q":"A tool-calling request to Anthropic returns a 400 about `tool_use` ids without `tool_result` blocks. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the turn after the assistant's `tool_use`:** it must be a `user` message whose content starts with\n  the `tool_result` blocks, keyed by `tool_use_id`.\n- **Common causes:** text placed before the results, a result sent with the wrong role, another turn in\n  between, or parallel results split across messages.\n- **Also check** that the assistant turn was echoed back unchanged, including its text block."},{"q":"How does cancellation actually stop a streamed completion, and what is still billed?","a":"- **Mechanism:** passing `signal` as a request option; aborting rejects with `APIUserAbortError` and closes\n  the socket, so the provider stops generating.\n- **Billing:** tokens already produced are still billed, so abort promptly on client disconnect."},{"q":"When would you choose OpenAI's Responses API over Chat Completions?","a":"- **Choose Responses** for typed `output` items, named stream events and server-side state via\n  `previous_response_id` instead of resending history.\n- **Mind retention:** responses are stored by default unless `store: false`, a data-retention decision.\n- **Keep Chat Completions** when existing code and tooling depend on the `messages` / `choices` shape."},{"q":"Your Anthropic bill did not drop after adding prompt caching. What do you check?","a":"- **The counter first:** `usage.cache_read_input_tokens` near zero means no hits.\n- **Then the prefix:** any byte change before the breakpoint, such as a timestamp in the system prompt,\n  invalidates it, and a prefix below the minimum cacheable length is never cached.\n- **Fix:** move variable content after the `cache_control` breakpoint and alert on the hit rate."}],"tradeoffs":["**Built-in retries vs multiplied attempts.** Transient failures become invisible, which is what you want until you wrap the call in your own retry or a queue with redelivery; then attempts multiply and you should set `maxRetries: 0` in one of the layers.","**Generated types vs upgrade churn.** Types track the API exactly, so upgrades surface breaking changes at compile time; the flip side is that a minor SDK bump can rename a type and force a same-day fix across the codebase.","**Streaming helpers vs memory.** They accumulate tool arguments and expose the final message for free, but buffer the whole response in memory; for very long outputs you may prefer the raw event iterable.","**Explicit vs automatic caching.** Anthropic's breakpoints give control and a verifiable hit counter at the cost of discipline about prompt ordering; OpenAI's automatic caching needs no annotations but gives less control over what is cached.","**Provider Zod helpers vs portability.** They couple tool definitions to that SDK; a shared schema module converted per provider keeps the option of a second provider open."],"pitfalls":["**Matching on `error.message` strings instead of `instanceof` and `status`.** Symptom: retry logic stops working after an SDK or API wording change.","**Passing the timeout in seconds as in Python.** Symptom: every request times out after a few milliseconds and is retried twice (three attempts) before failing.","**Forgetting the `signal` on streaming calls.** Symptom: completions keep running, and billing, long after the HTTP client disconnected.","**Treating `function.arguments` as an object on OpenAI, or `input` as a string on Anthropic.** Symptom: a `TypeError` or `[object Object]` inside a tool.","**Text before the `tool_result` blocks, the wrong role, or another turn between the `tool_use` and its result.** Symptom: a 400 saying `tool_use` ids were found without `tool_result` blocks immediately after. Splitting parallel results across messages is a quieter mistake that discourages parallel tool calls.","**Reading `response.content[0].text` on Anthropic without narrowing.** Symptom: a compile error at best, and at worst a runtime `undefined` when the first block is `tool_use` or `thinking`.","**Omitting `max_tokens` on Anthropic or setting it too low.** Symptom: a 400 on the former and truncated JSON with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` on the latter.","**A non-streaming request with a very large `max_tokens` through a proxy with a short idle timeout.** Symptom: 504s or `APIConnectionTimeoutError` on long answers only, then retries that may pay for the same long generation again. Stream long generations.","**Verifying caching by vibes.** Symptom: a system prompt with a timestamp in it, a cache hit rate of zero and a bill that never drops."],"when":"- **Use the native SDK** whenever you commit to a provider and want its full surface: streaming helpers,\n  tool runners, batch and files endpoints, explicit caching, platform clients for Azure, Bedrock or Vertex.\n- **Wrap it behind a narrow interface** so tests can inject a fake and a second provider can be added\n  later. Keep the SDK's retries while deciding explicitly where your own retries, queue redelivery and\n  circuit breaker sit.\n- **Reach for a unification layer** such as the Vercel AI SDK when multi-provider or browser streaming is\n  the primary requirement.\n- **Measure** tokens and cost per request from `usage`, p95 time to first token, retry counts, and cache\n  hit rate.\n- **Interviews** probe the tool-call round trip in both shapes, what the SDK retries and what it does not,\n  and how a cancelled request actually stops.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures clients with explicit timeouts and retries, writes adapters that normalise usage and errors, narrows content blocks correctly, and propagates `AbortSignal` into every call.","architect":"Decides the provider boundary (native SDK behind an interface versus a unification layer), where retries and breakers live across layers, and how cost telemetry is normalised.","fde":"Reads a customer's integration for the classic faults (string-matched errors, missing signals, doubled retries, unverified caching) and converts it to typed, cancellable, observable calls."},"check":{"q":"A service wraps `client.messages.create` in its own retry loop of three attempts with backoff,\nand the SDK keeps its default retries. Under a provider outage, how many requests can one user action\ngenerate, how long can it take, and what would you change?","a":"The SDK retries twice by default, so each outer attempt can send three requests; three outer\nattempts give up to nine requests, each waiting up to the SDK timeout plus backoff, so one click can\nhold a connection for many minutes and amplify load on an already failing provider. The fix is to pick\none layer. Either set `maxRetries: 0` and own retries, jitter and a circuit breaker in your client\nwrapper, or keep the SDK's retries and remove the outer loop, treating a thrown `RateLimitError` or\n`InternalServerError` as a signal to shed load or fail fast. Whichever you choose, set the handler and\nproxy timeouts from `timeout × (retries + 1)`, pass an `AbortSignal` so a disconnected user stops the\nchain, and record the request ID from the response for the inevitable support ticket."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the key structural difference between a tool call in OpenAI Chat Completions and in Anthropic Messages?","options":["OpenAI requires a JSON schema while Anthropic infers parameters from the description","OpenAI returns `tool_calls` with `arguments` as a JSON string and takes results as `role: \"tool\"` messages; Anthropic returns `tool_use` content blocks with a parsed `input` and takes results as `tool_result` blocks in a user message","Anthropic executes tools server-side, so no result needs to be returned","OpenAI allows only one tool call per response while Anthropic allows many"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both require a JSON schema and both can return several calls in one turn. The difference is the envelope: a string to parse and a `tool` role on one side, a parsed object inside a content block and a `tool_result` block on the other. Neither executes your custom tool for you."},{"q":"The TypeScript SDKs take `timeout: 30`. What happens, and why?","options":["Requests time out after 30 seconds, matching the Python SDK","Requests time out after 30 milliseconds and are retried `maxRetries` times, because the TypeScript SDKs take milliseconds","The value is ignored because timeouts are set per request only","The SDK throws at construction because the minimum timeout is one second"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unit mismatch is a common port-from-Python bug. The TypeScript clients take milliseconds, and because timeouts count as retryable, the 30 ms failure repeats up to the retry limit before the typed `APIConnectionTimeoutError` surfaces."},{"q":"Why should error handling use `instanceof OpenAI.RateLimitError` rather than inspecting `error.message`?","options":["Because `error.message` is always empty in the SDKs","Because `instanceof` is faster than string comparison","Because the typed classes map to HTTP status and error type stably, while message text can change with any SDK or API release","Because `error.message` contains the API key and must not be read"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The SDKs expose a class per status (and a `status` field) precisely so callers can branch on a stable contract. Message wording is informational and may change; code that depends on it breaks silently when it does."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Model Context Protocol specification","Official docs: MCP TypeScript SDK (@modelcontextprotocol/sdk)","Official docs: Zod"],"why":"- **MCP is a real wire protocol.** JSON-RPC 2.0 messages with an `initialize` handshake, capability\n  negotiation, a fixed set of request methods and notifications, and two transport bindings with their own\n  framing and session rules. The agents module's protocol lesson explains *why* MCP exists; this one covers\n  what the SDK does and where it bites.\n- **Hand-rolling it is a week plus a long bug tail.** A missing `initialized` notification, a tool whose\n  JSON Schema the host rejects, an HTTP endpoint ignoring the session header, a stdout log line that\n  corrupts the stream. Every server author would repeat the work and every host would find different mistakes.\n- **The SDK splits the work.** Server authors write handlers and schemas; host authors write `connect`,\n  `listTools` and `callTool`. Protocol machinery, versioning and transports are shared code maintained\n  alongside the specification.\n- **The naive alternatives lose.** A bespoke HTTP API per tool set reintroduces the per-framework connector\n  problem MCP removed and offers nothing for resources, prompts or server-initiated requests. A framework's\n  proprietary tool format works only inside that framework.\n- **Schemas are single-source values.** Zod values become JSON Schema on the wire and TypeScript types in the\n  handler, the schema-validation lesson's idea at a protocol boundary: the model sees the schema the handler\n  enforces, and the host can validate what it sends.","how":"### What happens on connect\nEvery session, on either transport, starts with three messages that the SDK sends and checks:\n1. **`initialize`.** The client sends the newest protocol version it supports, its capabilities (for\n   example `sampling`, `roots`, `elicitation`) and its name and version.\n2. **Server reply.** The version it will speak (the client's if supported, otherwise one it does; a client\n   that cannot speak it disconnects) and its capabilities (`tools`, `resources`, `prompts`, `logging`, each\n   possibly with `listChanged`).\n3. **`notifications/initialized`.** Only then do ordinary requests such as `tools/list` flow.\n\n- **Why negotiate:** hosts and servers upgrade independently, and each side may only use features the other\n  advertised, so an old host and a new server still interoperate on the common subset.\n- **Requests vs notifications.** Every request carries a JSON-RPC `id` and gets exactly one response;\n  notifications (progress, log messages, `notifications/cancelled`, list-changed) carry none.\n- **Client timeout.** The client enforces a per-request timeout (60 seconds by default in 1.x) and rejects\n  with a request-timeout `McpError`, which matters for slow tools.\n\n### Server side: two levels\n**`McpServer`** (from `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js`) is the high-level API. Construct it with a\nname and version, then register:\n\n| Method | Registers |\n|---|---|\n| `registerTool(name, { description, inputSchema, outputSchema? }, handler)` | A tool |\n| `registerResource(name, uriOrTemplate, metadata, reader)` | A resource |\n| `registerPrompt(name, { argsSchema }, builder)` | A prompt |\n\n- **`inputSchema` is a Zod shape** (recent 1.x releases also accept a full Zod object schema). The SDK\n  converts it to JSON Schema for `tools/list` and validates `tools/call` arguments before your handler runs,\n  so the handler receives a typed object.\n- **Handler result:** `{ content: [...] }` with `text`, `image`, `audio`, resource links or embedded\n  resources; optionally `structuredContent` (required, and validated, when an `outputSchema` is declared);\n  optionally `isError: true`.\n\n**Two kinds of failure**, per the spec:\n\n| Failure | Example | Sent as | Who reads it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tool execution | order not found, business rule violated, upstream API down | normal result with `isError: true` | the model, which can adapt |\n| Protocol | unknown method, malformed request | JSON-RPC error | the host, which treats it as a broken call |\n\n- **Throwing in `McpServer` \"works\" but leaks.** It is caught and converted into an `isError` result whose\n  text is the raw exception message, so you lose control of what the model reads and may leak stack-level\n  detail into its context. Return `isError` results deliberately, written for the model.\n- **Throwing in the low-level `Server` is worse.** It becomes a JSON-RPC error, so the host treats a routine\n  \"not found\" as an outage and the model never learns why the call failed.\n\n**`Server`** (the low level) exposes `setRequestHandler(RequestSchema, handler)` for every method in the\nspec, and is what `McpServer` is built on.\n- **Drop to it** for custom capabilities, fully dynamic tool lists computed per request, or to intercept raw\n  requests. (`McpServer` already sends `tools/list_changed` when you enable, disable or remove a tool.)\n- **Both levels** implement the handshake, capability advertisement, progress and logging notifications\n  and cancellation for you.\n\n### Transports\nA transport moves JSON-RPC messages; the server object is transport-agnostic.\n\n**`StdioServerTransport`** reads requests from `stdin` and writes responses to `stdout`.\n- **Never `console.log` in a stdio server:** anything on stdout that is not a JSON-RPC message corrupts the\n  stream. Log to `stderr` or via the protocol's logging notification.\n- **Right for local tools** launched by an IDE or desktop host, *because* the host owns the process, its\n  environment and lifetime, and no network, authentication or multi-tenancy question arises.\n\n**`StreamableHTTPServerTransport`** serves remote clients from one endpoint, conventionally `/mcp`.\n- **Flow:** the client POSTs JSON-RPC requests; the server replies with a JSON body or a server-sent event\n  stream (for slow responses or notifications); an optional GET opens a long-lived stream for\n  server-initiated messages.\n- **Sessions** are identified by an `Mcp-Session-Id` header issued during `initialize`.\n- **The older HTTP+SSE transport** is still shipped for compatibility but is superseded.\n\n| Mode | Setup | Gains | Costs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stateful | a `sessionIdGenerator`, one transport instance per session, kept in a map | server-initiated requests, resumable streams | a session pins a client to the replica holding its transport |\n| Stateless | no session id, every request self-contained | scales across replicas behind a load balancer | no server-initiated requests or resumable streams |\n\n- **Validate `Origin`.** Over HTTP the specification requires it, *because* a server listening on localhost\n  is otherwise reachable from any web page the user opens (DNS rebinding). Bind local servers to `127.0.0.1`\n  and turn on the SDK transport's host and origin checks.\n- **Authenticate remote servers** with OAuth 2.1 per the specification's authorization section. The SDK\n  ships helpers for both sides; the dedicated security lesson covers the trust decisions.\n\n### Client side\n- **`Client`** (from `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js`) is constructed with a name and version and\n  connected to a transport.\n- **Transports:** `StdioClientTransport({ command, args, env })` spawns the server as a child process;\n  `StreamableHTTPClientTransport(url)` talks to a remote one.\n- **`connect`** performs the handshake and records the server's capabilities.\n- **One-to-one methods:** `listTools()`, `callTool({ name, arguments })`, `listResources()`,\n  `readResource({ uri })`, `listPrompts()` and `getPrompt()` map onto protocol methods and return typed results.\n- **Server-initiated requests are handled here,** each opt-in through capability declarations: sampling\n  (the server asks the host's model for a completion), roots (the host says which directories the server\n  may touch) and elicitation (the server asks the user for input).\n\n### Bridging to a model\nNothing in the SDK calls a language model. The host does the mapping:\n1. **Each tool from `listTools()` becomes a provider tool definition.** `inputSchema` is already JSON Schema,\n   so it drops into Anthropic's `input_schema` or OpenAI's `parameters`.\n2. **A `tool_use` or `tool_calls` entry** in the model's reply becomes `callTool`.\n3. **The returned `content`** becomes the tool result sent back.\n\n- **Name collisions across servers are the host's problem**, usually solved with a server prefix.\n- **Anthropic's server-side MCP connector** does this bridging for remote servers inside the request. It\n  removes the loop from your code but also per-call inspection and approval (you keep only the static tool\n  allow-list its configuration offers), so it suits trusted servers and simple hosts.\n- **Keep the exposed tool set small.** A host forwarding forty tools from three servers pays in tokens and\n  tool-selection accuracy on every turn.","example":"### Stdio server plus a host that bridges to Claude\n- **Server:** one tool with a Zod-typed input, logging to stderr because stdout is the wire.\n- **Host:** discovers the tool, hands it to Claude, and routes the resulting `tool_use` back through `callTool`.\n- **Versions:** written against the 1.x `registerTool` API; older minors used `tool()`, and the maintainers\n  have been preparing a v2 line that splits client and server into separate packages, so import paths\n  depend on which line you install.\n- **Content mapping:** MCP `text` items map directly onto Anthropic text blocks, while images would need\n  re-encoding into Anthropic's `source` shape.\n\n```ts\n// server.ts — one tool over stdio. Nothing but JSON-RPC may touch stdout.\nimport { McpServer } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js\";\nimport { StdioServerTransport } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\nconst server = new McpServer({ name: \"orders\", version: \"1.0.0\" });\nserver.registerTool(\n  \"lookup_order\",\n  { description: \"Fetch an order by id\", inputSchema: { orderId: z.string().regex(/^ord_\\w+$/) } },\n  async ({ orderId }) => {                                    // typed from the Zod shape, already validated\n    const order = await db.orders.find(orderId);\n    if (!order) return { isError: true, content: [{ type: \"text\", text: `no order ${orderId}` }] };\n    return { content: [{ type: \"text\", text: JSON.stringify(order) }] };\n  },\n);\nconsole.error(\"orders server starting\");                      // stderr, never console.log\nawait server.connect(new StdioServerTransport());\n\n// host.ts — discover tools, give them to the model, route tool_use back to the server.\nimport { Client } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js\";\nimport { StdioClientTransport } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/stdio.js\";\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\n\nconst mcp = new Client({ name: \"host\", version: \"1.0.0\" });\nawait mcp.connect(new StdioClientTransport({ command: \"node\", args: [\"dist/server.js\"] }));\nconst { tools } = await mcp.listTools();\nconst claudeTools: Anthropic.Tool[] = tools.map((t) => ({\n  name: t.name, description: t.description ?? \"\", input_schema: t.inputSchema as Anthropic.Tool.InputSchema,\n}));\nconst anthropic = new Anthropic();\nconst ask = (messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[]) =>\n  anthropic.messages.create({ model: \"claude-opus-5-5\", max_tokens: 1024, tools: claudeTools, messages });\nconst messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[] = [{ role: \"user\", content: \"What is the status of ord_42?\" }];\nconst reply = await ask(messages);\nconst results: Anthropic.ToolResultBlockParam[] = [];\nfor (const block of reply.content) {\n  if (block.type !== \"tool_use\") continue;\n  const r = await mcp.callTool({ name: block.name, arguments: block.input as Record<string, unknown> });\n  const text = (r.content as { type: string; text?: string }[])        // keep text items only here\n    .flatMap((c) => (c.type === \"text\" ? [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: c.text ?? \"\" }] : []));\n  results.push({ type: \"tool_result\", tool_use_id: block.id, is_error: r.isError === true, content: text });\n}\nmessages.push({ role: \"assistant\", content: reply.content }, { role: \"user\", content: results });\nconst final = await ask(messages);                 // the model now answers from the tool result\n```\n\n### Stateless Streamable HTTP behind a load balancer\nThe same server object served over HTTP, one fresh transport per request and no session id, so any replica\ncan answer any request. `buildServer()` is assumed to create the `McpServer` and register its tools as above.\n\n```ts\nimport express from \"express\";\nimport { StreamableHTTPServerTransport } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js\";\nimport { buildServer } from \"./server\";\n\nconst app = express();\napp.use(express.json());\n\napp.post(\"/mcp\", async (req, res) => {\n  const server = buildServer();\n  const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });  // stateless\n  res.on(\"close\", () => { transport.close(); server.close(); });                           // free per-request state\n  await server.connect(transport);\n  await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);\n});\n\napp.listen(3000, \"127.0.0.1\");   // put authentication (OAuth) and Origin checks in front before exposing it\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the cost of forwarding every tool\nA host connects to three servers and forwards all forty of their tools. Token figures are illustrative\norders of magnitude, not measurements; count your own with the provider's token counter.\n\n| Setup | Tools sent per turn | Definition tokens per turn (illustrative) | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Forward everything | 40 | ~40 × 150 = ~6,000 | paid on every turn, more similar names to confuse |\n| Curated per host, prefixed | 8 | ~8 × 150 = ~1,200 | cheaper turns, clearer tool choice |\n\n- **Collisions:** two servers each exposing `search` collide unless the host prefixes them, for example\n  `docs_search` and `tickets_search`.","patterns":["**Stateless by default for remote servers.** Construct `StreamableHTTPServerTransport` with `sessionIdGenerator: undefined` and a fresh transport per request when you run several replicas and use neither sampling nor elicitation. Monitor \"session not found\" errors, which should be zero.","**Per-call timeouts and progress for slow tools.** On the client, pass request options as the third argument, `mcp.callTool(params, undefined, { timeout: 300_000, resetTimeoutOnProgress: true })`, and have the server send progress notifications. Track p95 tool latency against the timeout.","**Curated, prefixed tool sets per host.** Map each server's tools to `${server}_${tool}` names and forward only the allow-listed subset each agent needs. Monitor tools exposed per host, tokens per turn and wrong-tool calls in traces.","**Model-facing error messages.** Catch expected failures in handlers and return `{ isError: true, content: [{ type: \"text\", text: \"...\" }] }` written for the model, with internal detail only in server logs on stderr. Split the error rate by `isError` versus protocol error.","**Lock down local HTTP servers.** Bind to `127.0.0.1`, enable the transport's host and origin checks, and require OAuth before any non-local exposure. Alert on rejected origins and unauthenticated requests.","**Clean child-process lifecycle.** Call `await mcp.close()` from the host's shutdown handler so stdio servers exit with it, and run the host with an init process if it spawns many. Watch for orphaned server processes on developer machines and CI runners."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant H as Host with Client\n    participant S as MCP server\n    participant M as Model API\n    H->>S: initialize: version, capabilities\n    S-->>H: chosen version, capabilities\n    H->>S: notifications/initialized\n    H->>S: tools/list\n    S-->>H: tools with JSON Schema\n    H->>M: messages plus tool definitions\n    M-->>H: tool_use block\n    H->>S: tools/call, args validated by Zod\n    S-->>H: content, isError flag\n    H->>M: tool_result, model answers","caption":"The host drives everything: the handshake, discovery, and every tool call the model asks for. The SDK\nhandles the protocol on both ends; the model only ever talks to the host."},"interview":[{"q":"What does the MCP SDK give a server author that a hand-written JSON-RPC endpoint does not?","a":"- **Protocol machinery:** the `initialize` handshake, version and capability negotiation, notifications,\n  cancellation and both transports, maintained alongside the spec.\n- **One schema, three uses:** a Zod shape becomes JSON Schema in `tools/list`, validation on `tools/call`,\n  and the handler's TypeScript types."},{"q":"A host hangs at `initialize` with a stdio server that seems to run fine. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect stdout first:** any `console.log` or library banner on stdout corrupts the JSON-RPC stream.\n- **Confirm** by running the server by hand and looking for non-JSON lines on stdout.\n- **Fix:** log to stderr or through protocol logging notifications, and check dependencies that print on import."},{"q":"When should a tool return `isError: true`, and when is a JSON-RPC error correct?","a":"- **`isError` for execution failures** such as not found or upstream down, with a message written for the\n  model so it can adapt.\n- **JSON-RPC errors for protocol faults** such as an unknown method or malformed request, which the host\n  treats as a broken call.\n- **Do not throw for routine failures:** `McpServer` forwards raw exception text, and the low-level\n  `Server` turns it into a protocol error."},{"q":"Would you use Anthropic's server-side MCP connector or bridge tools in your own host?","a":"- **Connector:** less code, fine for trusted remote servers and simple hosts.\n- **Own host loop:** needed when you must inspect, approve, filter or rate-limit each call, since the\n  connector only offers a static tool allow-list."},{"q":"A slow report tool fails at about 60 seconds while the server keeps working. What is happening?","a":"- **Cause:** the client's default per-request timeout (60 seconds in 1.x) expired and raised a\n  request-timeout `McpError`.\n- **Fix:** pass a longer `timeout` in the request options, or send progress notifications from the server\n  and set `resetTimeoutOnProgress` so progress extends the deadline."}],"tradeoffs":["**Shared protocol code vs release cadence.** The SDK handles handshake, schemas and transports so a server is a few dozen lines, but the high-level API has been renamed between minors (`tool()` to `registerTool`) and error handling has shifted too; pin versions and read release notes on upgrade.","**Stdio vs Streamable HTTP.** Stdio is simple and secure by construction, yet ties the server's lifetime to one host process; once two hosts or a remote user need it, Streamable HTTP and auth are required.","**Stateful vs stateless sessions.** Stateful enables server-initiated requests and stream resumption; stateless scales horizontally with no affinity. Choose stateless unless you actually use sampling or elicitation.","**`isError` results vs leakage.** They keep the model in the loop on failures, but can leak internal detail into model context, and an uncaught throw in `McpServer` does so automatically with the raw exception message. Format tool errors for the model, not from stack traces.","**Server-side connector vs host control.** It removes the bridging loop, at the cost of the host's ability to filter, approve or rate-limit calls; use it for trusted servers only.","**Cheap to expose vs expensive per turn.** Many tools cost little to build and a lot in tokens and selection accuracy every turn; curate per host rather than forwarding everything."],"pitfalls":["**A `console.log` in a stdio server.** Symptom: the host reports a JSON parse error or hangs at `initialize` while the server appears to run fine.","**Throwing from a tool handler for expected failures.** With `McpServer`, the model reads raw exception text (a driver error, an internal hostname) instead of a useful message; with the low-level `Server`, the agent loop aborts on a protocol error instead of the model reading \"not found\" and trying something else.","**A stateful Streamable HTTP server behind a round-robin balancer.** Symptom: intermittent \"session not found\" errors after the first request.","**Vague descriptions or untyped `z.any()` inputs.** Symptom: the model calls the wrong tool or passes arguments the handler cannot use.","**Unprefixed tool names across several servers.** Symptom: one server's `search` silently shadows another's.","**A public HTTP server with no authentication** because the local stdio version needed none. Symptom: anyone with the URL can call your tools.","**A tool that outlives the client's default request timeout.** Symptom: a request-timeout `McpError` at about a minute while the server keeps working. Pass a longer `timeout` in the request options, or send progress notifications and set `resetTimeoutOnProgress`.","**Leaving the child process running when the host exits.** Symptom: orphaned server processes accumulate on developer machines."],"when":"- **Write an MCP server** when a system should be usable by more than one host or agent framework, a\n  customer or vendor already speaks MCP, or you want tools, resources and prompts described once, typed.\n- **Pick the transport:** stdio for local developer tooling and anything an IDE launches; Streamable HTTP,\n  stateless where possible, with OAuth, for shared or remote servers.\n- **Write a client** when your own agent must consume third-party servers.\n- **Avoid MCP** for a single in-process tool used by one service; a plain function with a Zod schema is\n  less code.\n- **Measure** tools exposed per host, tool-call error rate split by `isError` versus protocol error, and p95\n  call latency.\n- **Interviews** ask what the SDK does and does not do (it never calls a model), the tool-error-versus-\n  protocol-error distinction, and why stdout is sacred.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Registers tools with Zod shapes and useful descriptions, returns `isError` results for expected failures, picks the transport, and writes the host loop that bridges tools to the model.","architect":"Decides which systems get MCP servers, stateful versus stateless HTTP, how servers are authenticated and versioned, and which hosts may see which tools.","fde":"Wraps a customer's internal API as an MCP server so their existing hosts can use it, and audits the servers they already run for stdout logging, missing auth and oversized tool sets."},"check":{"q":"A team ported their stdio MCP server to Streamable HTTP, deployed three replicas behind a load\nbalancer, and now see intermittent failures after the first request. What is wrong and what are their\noptions?","a":"The server runs in stateful mode: `initialize` creates a session on one replica and returns an\n`Mcp-Session-Id`, and the next request carrying that id lands on a different replica that has no such\ntransport, so it fails. Options: switch to stateless mode (no session id generator), which makes each\nrequest self-contained and scales freely at the cost of server-initiated requests and resumable\nstreams; keep stateful mode and enable session affinity at the balancer, accepting uneven load and\nsession loss on replica restarts; or externalise session state so any replica can serve it, which the\nSDK does not do for you and is rarely worth it. Most tool servers use neither sampling nor\nelicitation, so stateless is the usual answer, together with OAuth now that the server is reachable."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must an MCP server using the stdio transport never write logs with `console.log`?","options":["Because the SDK disables `console` for performance reasons","Because stdout is the JSON-RPC channel to the host, so any non-protocol output corrupts the message stream","Because logs would be visible to the model and leak information","Because stdio servers run without a TTY and `console.log` throws"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The transport frames messages on stdout; a stray line breaks parsing on the host side, often showing up as a hang during `initialize`. Logs go to stderr or to the protocol's logging notifications. The model never sees server logs unless a tool returns them."},{"q":"A tool handler cannot find the requested record. What should it do?","options":["Throw an error and let the SDK report the failure to the host","Return an empty `content` array and let the model infer the failure","Return a normal result with `isError: true` and a message, so the model sees why the call failed and can adapt","Call `process.exit(1)` to signal the failure unambiguously"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`isError` marks a tool-level failure the model is expected to handle, with a message you control. Throwing is the tempting option but is wrong either way: `McpServer` catches it and forwards the raw exception text as an `isError` result, while a low-level `Server` turns it into a JSON-RPC error that typically aborts the agent step. An empty result hides the cause. Exiting kills the server for every other call."},{"q":"What does the MCP TypeScript SDK not do for a host application?","options":["Perform the `initialize` handshake and capability negotiation","Convert a server's Zod schemas into JSON Schema for `tools/list`","Call a language model and decide which tool to invoke","Spawn a stdio server as a child process and talk to it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The SDK implements the protocol on both sides and manages transports. Bridging to a model, mapping tool definitions into the provider's format, sending the model's tool call to `callTool` and returning the result, is the host's code, or a provider-side connector if one is used."}]}]});
